    2: #  PRE-COMMIT REWORK CHECK.  Run from git-bash before every commit:
    3: #      bash /c/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/precommit_check.sh ["commit message file"]
    4: #
    5: #  WHY THIS EXISTS, measured on 2026-08-14: SEVEN OF FIFTEEN COMMITS THAT DAY
    6: #  (47%) FIXED THE COMMIT IMMEDIATELY BEFORE THEM -- #407->#405, #409->#408,
    7: #  #411->#410, #413->#412, #415->#414, #417->#416, #406->#404.  Raw throughput
    8: #  was never the constraint; REWORK was.  Every one of those was caught later by
    9: #  a gate or a seat, i.e. after the commit existed.
   10: #
   11: #  *** WHAT THIS CANNOT DO, said here so a green run is not read as more than it
   12: #  is.  Of the three rework classes, only ONE is fully decidable by a script:
   13: #    1. twin-tree propagation      -- FULLY mechanical (a cmp).  Check A.
   14: #    2. a claim that outruns the diff -- PARTIALLY mechanical.  Whether a file is
   15: #       named in the message but absent from the diff is decidable; whether a
   16: #       SENTENCE IS TRUE is not.  Checks C and D.
   17: #    3. a detector that does not test what it names -- NOT mechanical at all.
   18: #       Only a mutation run settles it, and the mutant is a human choice.
   19: #       Check E can only ask whether one was RECORDED.
   20: #  A green run means the decidable things were checked.  It is not evidence
   21: #  about correctness. ***
   22: set -u
   23: #  ROOT IS DERIVED FROM THIS SCRIPT'S OWN LOCATION, not hardcoded.  This file lives in
   24: #  GXEMUL-SEC/tools/, so the project root is two levels up.  It used to carry an absolute
   25: #  path, which is only half-tracked: a fresh clone anywhere else would have run against
   26: #  whatever happened to be at the old address, or nothing.  $GXROOT overrides for testing.
   27: _HERE="$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]:-$0}")" && pwd)"
   28: ROOT="${GXROOT:-$(cd "$_HERE/../.." && pwd)}"
   29: SEC=$ROOT/GXEMUL-SEC
   30: EST=$ROOT/est
   31: BLD=$ROOT/build
   32: MSGFILE=${1:-}
   33: fail=0
   34: warn=0
   35: 
   36: say  () { printf '%s\n' "$*"; }
   37: bad  () { fail=$((fail+1)); printf '  FAIL  %s\n' "$*"; }
   38: soft () { warn=$((warn+1)); printf '  WARN  %s\n' "$*"; }
   39: good () { printf '  ok    %s\n' "$*"; }
   40: #  `warn` is called at the four "checker MISSING" branches and was DEFINED NOWHERE -- so a
   41: #  missing pipeline checker printed `warn: command not found` on stderr and its gate was
   42: #  silently disabled, which is the exact failure those branches exist to announce.  Measured
   43: #  by renaming check_stage_panels.py and check_seats_read.py away: both gates vanished and the
   44: #  run still said PRECOMMIT_PASS.
   45: warn () { soft "$@"; }
   46: 
   47: cd "$SEC" || { say "no repo at $SEC"; exit 9; }
   48: # ---------------------------------------------------------------- F
   49: #  THE PASS-2 RECEIPT, AND THIS IS THE SECOND VERSION BECAUSE THE FIRST COULD NOT FIRE.
   50: #
   51: #  R6 shipped without a pass-2 review of its diff.  That was caught externally,
   52: #  recorded, and corrected -- and R7 then did exactly the same thing one round
   53: #  later.  So a mechanical check was written.  A pass-2 seat then MEASURED that
   54: #  the check itself was vacuous: it sat below the "nothing staged" early exit, so
   55: #  on a clean post-commit tree -- the only state in which a missed pass 2 is
   56: #  actually detectable -- the script returned before reaching it.  It also keyed
   57: #  on source mtime, which any checkout or copy moves, and accepted any panel
   58: #  directory as evidence without asking what that panel reviewed.
   59: #
   60: #  A check that cannot fail is worse than no check, because it reassures.  This
   61: #  version fixes all four faults:
