  307: 	#  all of which leave the gate behaving identically.  That is why the count is
  308: 	#  asserted at all.
  309: 	#  19 -> 20 on 2026-08-28: `#489` wired gdrom_toclen_probe.py into gate_sh_rounding.sh
  310: 	#  with a literal denominator pin (`check "gdromtoclen rows run" ... 15`).  Raised
  311: 	#  DELIBERATELY, which is what the growth arm below asks for -- the count is the
  312: 	#  point, so it may not be nudged to silence a red.
  313: 	#  22 -> 23 on 2026-08-30: `sh4_bsc_width_probe.py` wired into gate_sh_rounding.sh with a
  314: 	#  literal denominator pin (`check "sh4bscwidth rows run" ... 13`), discharging the SECOND
  315: 	#  exemption of the day.  Raised DELIBERATELY -- and worth noting that discharging the
  316: 	#  FIRST one broke the wiring checker's own selftest, which had hardcoded the probe it was
  317: 	#  driving toward.  Progress is not supposed to red the harness; that was repaired in the
  318: 	#  same session and its mutant count is now pinned here too.
  319: 	#  21 -> 22 on 2026-08-30: `#496` wired gdrom_cond_probe.py into gate_sh_rounding.sh
  320: 	#  with a literal denominator pin (`check "gdromcond rows run" ... 20`).  Same
  321: 	#  deliberate raise, and the growth arm named this file and this line when it fired --
  322: 	#  which is the property that makes the check worth its noise.
  323: 	SCORE_CLAIMS_MANIFEST=23
  324: 	_spinned=$(printf '%s\n' "$out" | sed -n 's/^SCORE_CLAIMS_PINNED=//p')
  325: 	if [ -z "$_spinned" ]; then
  326: 		bad "check_score_claims printed no SCORE_CLAIMS_PINNED line -- the count cannot be pinned"
  327: 	elif [ "$_spinned" -lt "$SCORE_CLAIMS_MANIFEST" ]; then
  328: 		bad "SCORE-CLAIM COVERAGE DROPPED: $_spinned probes pinned, manifest says $SCORE_CLAIMS_MANIFEST."
  329: 		bad "  A pin stops being seen when its literal is quoted, its label gains a"
  330: 		bad "  word before the stem, or the count moves to a variable -- all of which"
  331: 		bad "  leave the gate behaving identically.  Restore it; do not lower the manifest."
  332: 	elif [ "$_spinned" -gt "$SCORE_CLAIMS_MANIFEST" ]; then
  333: 		bad "SCORE-CLAIM COVERAGE GREW: $_spinned probes pinned, manifest says $SCORE_CLAIMS_MANIFEST."
  334: 		bad "  This is not a failure -- it means a new gated probe arrived.  Raise"
  335: 		bad "  SCORE_CLAIMS_MANIFEST to $_spinned in tools/precommit_check.sh, deliberately."
  336: 		bad "  Growth is caught on purpose: a floor that only catches shrinkage goes"
  337: 		bad "  slack every time the count rises, which is how this check sat at 16"
  338: 		bad "  against a real 18."
  339: 	else
  340: 		good "coverage pinned: $_spinned probes (manifest $SCORE_CLAIMS_MANIFEST, equality)"
  341: 	fi
  342: else
  343: 	bad "STALE SCORE CLAIMS -- prose disagrees with the gate pin:"
  344: 	printf '%s
  345: ' "$out" | sed 's/^/          /'
  346: 	bad "  the pin is authoritative; fix the prose, or re-measure and fix both"
  347: fi
  348: 
  349: say ""; say "T. seat outcomes: a CLOSED row's panel entries should be graded"
  350: #  SOFT ON PURPOSE, and the reason matters.  A hard gate here would force a grade at
  351: #  commit time, and a forced grade is a guessed grade -- which is precisely the recall
  352: #  bias seat_scorecard.py exists to replace.  What this does is make coverage VISIBLE
  353: #  every commit, so a seat's accuracy stops being something anyone has to remember.
  354: #  Introduced 2026-08-22 after the ledger, with 1525 entries across nine seats, could
  355: #  not answer "which seat is most accurate" -- it recorded participation and nothing
  356: #  else.
  357: #
  358: #  *** OWNER DECISION 2026-08-22: STAYS SOFT, AND THE REVIEW IS SCHEDULED. ***
  359: #  The pass-2 panel argued HARD, and the argument is good: a soft check ignored for six
  360: #  months yields 1.3% coverage forever, which is indistinguishable from not having the
  361: #  tool at all.  The counter is that a hard gate forces a grade at commit time, and a
  362: #  forced grade is a guessed grade -- the exact bias this tool replaces.
  363: #
  364: #  Neither side is measured, so it is settled BY MEASUREMENT rather than by argument:
  365: #  leave it soft and RE-CHECK COVERAGE AFTER ROUGHLY TEN MORE ROUNDS.  If it has not
  366: #  moved off the floor, the panel was right and this goes hard -- preferably in the
  367: #  closed-rows-only form, since closing a row is the moment its outcome is actually
  368: #  known.  Write the coverage number down when you check, so the next reader sees a
  369: #  trend instead of re-arguing it.
  370: #
  371: #  BASELINE AT THE DECISION: 1558 entries, 20 graded, 1.3%.
  372: SCARD=$_HERE/pipeline/seat_scorecard.py
  373: if [ ! -f "$SCARD" ]; then
  374: 	warn "seat_scorecard.py MISSING -- seat accuracy NOT tracked"
  375: else
  376: 	#  Grep the WHOLE output, not a tail window.  The first version took `tail -n 8`
  377: 	#  and broke the same day: adding the self-graded disclosure banner pushed the
  378: 	#  summary line out of the window, so the check reported "produced no summary"
  379: 	#  about a tool that was working perfectly.  A positional grep over another
  380: 	#  program's output is a brittle check, and this one proved it within hours.
  381: 	_sc=$(python "$SCARD" 2>&1 | grep -E "entries, [0-9]+ graded")
  382: 	if [ -n "$_sc" ]; then
  383: 		good "seat scorecard: $_sc"
  384: 	else
  385: 		warn "seat_scorecard.py produced no summary -- check it by hand"
  386: 	fi
  387: 	#  The row that JUST closed is the one whose outcome is actually known, so name
  388: 	#  closed rows whose panel entries are still ungraded.  That is the moment to grade.
  389: 	_ug=$(GRADING_CUTOFF_NOTE="$GRADING_CUTOFF_NOTE" python - "$_HERE/pipeline/ledger.json" <<'PYEOF'
  390: import json, io, os, sys
  391: GRADING_CUTOFF = os.environ.get("GRADING_CUTOFF_NOTE", "2026-08-23")
  392:                                 # seat_scorecard.py shipped MID-DAY on 2026-08-22 (8185920),
  393:                                 # so rows closed ON that day are a boundary this cannot
  394:                                 # resolve from dates alone -- grandfather the whole day
  395:                                 # rather than guess intra-day ordering.  From 08-23 the
  396:                                 # check is live and every closure carries its outcomes.
  397: d = json.load(io.open(sys.argv[1], encoding="utf-8"))
  398: bad, old = [], []
  399: for r in d.get("rows", []):
  400:     if r.get("state") != "closed":
  401:         continue
  402:     ents = [e for e in r.get("entries", []) if e.get("phase") in ("assess", "review")]
  403:     if len(ents) <= 1:
  404:         continue                      # filed, never panelled -- nothing to grade
  405:     #  *** UNVERIFIED IS NOT A GRADE, AND COUNTING IT AS ONE MADE THIS CHECK
  406:     #  VACUOUS. ***  A review seat found it by reading the predicate rather than
  407:     #  the docstring: `any(e.get("outcome"))` is true of the string the design
  408:     #  defines as NOT a grade, so writing UNVERIFIED everywhere would green the
  409:     #  check.  Latent rather than live -- zero rows trip it today -- but a check
  410:     #  that CAN be satisfied without doing the thing is the shape this project
  411:     #  calls vacuous, and it was in the tool built to refuse exactly that.
  412:     if any(e.get("outcome") not in (None, "UNVERIFIED") for e in ents):
  413:         continue
  414:     #  *** FORWARD-ONLY FROM THE DAY THE SCORECARD SHIPPED (8185920, 2026-08-22). ***
  415:     #
  416:     #  This check asked for something seat_scorecard.py's own banner FORBIDS: "Grade an
  417:     #  entry when its claim is CHECKED -- reconstructing grades later reproduces exactly
  418:     #  the recall bias this tool replaced."  Eight rows closed between 08-14 and 08-20,
  419:     #  before the tool existed, and every commit since has printed them as an ungraded
  420:     #  warning that CANNOT BE HONESTLY CLEARED -- only by the reconstruction the tool was
  421:     #  built to refuse.  A warning nobody can clear is a warning everybody learns to skip,
  422:     #  which is worse than not having it.
  423:     #
  424:     #  So they are GRANDFATHERED and COUNTED, in the idiom check_stage_panels.py already
  425:     #  uses -- named as a number rather than a to-do list, so the gap stays visible without
  426:     #  masquerading as an action item.  A row closed on or after the cutoff is still named
  427:     #  individually, because that IS the moment its outcome is known.
  428:     if max([e.get("date", "") for e in ents] or [""]) < GRADING_CUTOFF:
  429:         old.append(r["id"])
  430:         continue
  431:     bad.append(r["id"])
  432: #  Report the TOTAL, not just the first eight.  The original printed bad[:8]
  433: #  with no count, so 37 ungraded rows read as 8 -- a silent cap, which is the
  434: #  shape this project already refuses elsewhere ("silent truncation reads as
  435: #  covered-everything when it did not").
  436: print("%s|%d|%d" % (" ".join(bad[:8]), len(old), len(bad)))
  437: PYEOF
  438: )
  439: 	_ugnames=$(printf '%s' "$_ug" | cut -d'|' -f1)
  440: 	_ugold=$(printf '%s' "$_ug" | cut -d'|' -f2)
  441: 	_ugtot=$(printf '%s' "$_ug" | cut -d'|' -f3)
  442: 	if [ -n "$_ugnames" ]; then
  443: 		warn "closed rows with panel entries but NO seat outcomes: $_ugtot total, first 8: $_ugnames"
  444: 		warn "  grade them with an outcome + outcome_note; see seat_scorecard.py"
  445: 		warn "  ($_ugold more are GRANDFATHERED pre-$GRADING_CUTOFF_NOTE and not counted above)"
  446: 	elif [ "${_ugold:-0}" != "0" ]; then
  447: 		good "every post-cutoff closed row carries seat outcomes ($_ugold grandfathered
  448:           -- closed before seat_scorecard.py existed; grading them now would be the
  449:           reconstruction its own banner forbids)"
  450: 	else
  451: 		good "every closed-and-panelled row carries seat outcomes"
  452: 	fi
  453: fi
  454: 
  455: say ""; say "Q. matrix.html is current with ledger.json"
  456: GEND=$_HERE/pipeline/gen_dashboard.py
  457: if [ ! -f "$GEND" ]; then
  458: 	warn "gen_dashboard.py MISSING -- matrix freshness NOT verified"
  459: else
  460: 	_mx=$(mktemp)
  461: 	if python "$GEND" --out "$_mx" >/dev/null 2>&1 && cmp -s "$_mx" "$_HERE/pipeline/matrix.html"; then
  462: 		good "matrix.html matches a fresh render of ledger.json"
  463: 	else
  464: 		bad "matrix.html IS STALE -- run: python tools/pipeline/gen_dashboard.py"
  465: 		bad "  (and REPUBLISH the artifact; this check cannot see that half)"
  466: 	fi
  467: 	rm -f "$_mx"
  468: fi
  469: 
  472: #
  473: #  Detector escapes in this project come in three shapes and TWO already had mechanical
  474: #  guards: gate_offline's SM/SC manifests catch a differential with no self-mutant control,
  475: #  and gate_hygiene's EXPECT_CONVERTED pin catches a probe that does not carry the #392 pty
  476: #  constructs.  The third had none -- NOBODY RUNS IT -- and it was the largest.  MEASURED at
  477: #  3d752b4: FOUR committed detectors totalling SIXTY-SIX rows executed in no gate at all.
  478: #    #439/#440 pit8253_latch_probe.py      18 rows         #441 sh4_bsc_width_probe.py  13
  479: #    #442      fbpending_drain_probe.py    11, report-only  #444 hpcmips_ctor_probe.py   24
  480: #  The finding that named the gap put it at 24 rows across two rounds; it is four rounds and
  481: #  66, because #444's detector landed after the census and nothing existed to notice.
  482: #
  483: #  THE MEASURE IS NAMED BECAUSE THE FIRST ONE WAS WRONG: `row()` call sites with a literal
  484: #  label, counted through python's `ast`.  A first pass grepped for 'row("R' and reported 56,
  485: #  having silently dropped every control row that is not R-prefixed.  The padded-column grep
  486: #  trap in another costume -- a number that looks right and counts the wrong set.
  487: #
  488: #  GATE 6's CENSUS PIN IS NOT THIS CHECK IN DIFFERENT CLOTHES, and the distinction is the
  489: #  same one this file already draws between G and H: the PIN asks whether the probe's SOURCE
  490: #  carries the right constructs, R asks whether anything EXECUTES it.  All four files above
  491: #  satisfy the pin today.  Neither catches the other's failure.
  492: #
  493: #  A MENTION IS NOT WIRING, and that is the whole difficulty.  gate_sh_rounding.sh:185
  494: #  mentions sh4_bsc_width_probe.py in a comment COMPLAINING that it is unwired; gate_hygiene
  495: #  mentions all four in its census annotations; and gate_hygiene.sh:331 carries a probe
  496: #  basename and the token `python3` on ONE non-comment line whose message is that the probe
  497: #  did NOT run.  A grep-for-the-name check scores every one of those as wired and prints
  498: #  green over the entire defect.  check_probe_wiring.py decides by COMMAND POSITION, derives
  499: #  the battery from run.sh's own GATES array, and PRINTS the file:line it found -- so the
  500: #  derivation is auditable rather than trusted.  It is the check_fable_queue substring bug
  501: #  from earlier the same day, refused in advance.
  502: #
  503: #  HARD, and retroactive rather than forward-only-by-date: the domain is 36 files in one
  504: #  flat directory, and a date cutoff would grandfather exactly the four files it exists for.
  505: #  Existing gaps are carried as DATED exemptions that EXPIRE (2026-09-20, chosen to fall due
  506: #  with gate_offline's fbpending_bound), never as silent grandfathering.
  507: #
  508: #  THE SELFTEST RUNS ONLY WHEN THE CHECKER ITSELF CHANGES.  --selftest applies 18 mutants to
  509: #  temp copies and asserts each exit status, which takes ~14 s -- too slow for every commit,
  510: #  and pointless when the checker did not move.  Firing it on the one commit that could break
  511: #  it is the section-E idea: a check nobody has to remember.
  512: say ""; say "R. every committed detector is run by a gate (or dated-exempt)"
  513: PWCHK=$_HERE/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py
  514: if [ ! -f "$PWCHK" ]; then
  515: 	warn "check_probe_wiring.py MISSING -- probe wiring is NOT verified"
  516: else
  517: 	if out=$(python "$PWCHK" 2>&1); then
  518: 		good "$(printf '%s' "$out" | grep -E 'PROBEWIRING_PASS')"
  519: 		printf '%s\n' "$out" | grep -E '^  note ' | sed 's/^/        /'
  520: 	else
  521: 		bad "A COMMITTED DETECTOR IS RUN BY NO GATE (or an exemption expired):"
  522: 		printf '%s\n' "$out" | grep -E 'PROBEWIRING_FAIL|^  FAIL ' | sed 's/^/          /'
  523: 		bad "  wire it into a gate, or add a DATED line to EXEMPT naming the gate that owes it"
  524: 	fi
  525: 	#  UNTRACKED COUNTS AS TOUCHED.  The first version asked only `git diff` and
  526: 	#  `git diff --cached`, and reported "checker unchanged" on the very commit that
  527: 	#  INTRODUCED the checker -- it was untracked, so neither diff could see it, and the
  528: 	#  one commit that most needed the selftest was the one that skipped it.  Measured on
  529: 	#  this file's own first run.
  530: 	_pwtouched=$( { git diff --cached --name-only; git diff --name-only;
  531: 	                git ls-files --others --exclude-standard; } \
  532: 	              | grep -c 'tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring\.py' )
  533: 	if [ "$_pwtouched" -gt 0 ]; then
  534: 		if stout=$(python "$PWCHK" --selftest 2>&1); then
  535: 			good "$(printf '%s' "$stout" | grep -E 'PROBEWIRING_SELFTEST_PASS')"
  536: 			#  *** THE EXIT CODE IS NOT ENOUGH, AND A SEAT NAMED THE GAP:
  537: 			#  "precommit checks the exit code, not the mutant census -- the
  538: 			#  suite goes green with MALFORMED-EXEMPTION coverage gone." ***
  539: 			#
  540: 			#  Every mutant that REMAINS can behave correctly while mutants
  541: 			#  that were DELETED say nothing at all.  The selftest itself now
  542: 			#  holds its denominator when a case cannot run (it appends a
  543: 			#  failure rather than skipping), but that only covers the SKIP
  544: 			#  path -- an edit that removes a case outright still exits 0.
  545: 			#
  546: 			#  So the count is pinned to a LITERAL here, which is the same
  547: 			#  discipline every gate row in this tree already uses for its own
  548: 			#  denominator: a suite that loses cases cannot pass by shrinking.
  549: 			#  Raise it DELIBERATELY when mutants are added, and never nudge it
  550: 			#  down to silence a red -- the count is the point.
  551: 			_stden=$(printf '%s' "$stout" \
  552: 			         | sed -n 's/.*PROBEWIRING_SELFTEST_PASS *[0-9]*\/\([0-9]*\).*/\1/p' \
  553: 			         | tail -1)
  554: 			if [ "${_stden:-0}" -eq 22 ] 2>/dev/null; then
  555: 				good "selftest mutant count is 22, the pinned value"
  556: 			else
  557: 				bad "THE PROBE-WIRING SELFTEST HAS LOST MUTANTS: ${_stden:-unparseable}, pinned 22."
  558: 				bad "  Every REMAINING mutant can behave correctly while DELETED ones say"
  559: 				bad "  nothing at all.  Raise the pin DELIBERATELY when mutants are added;"
  560: 				bad "  never nudge it down to silence a red -- the count is the point."
  561: 			fi
  562: 		else
  563: 			bad "THE PROBE-WIRING CHECKER CAN NO LONGER BE SHOWN TO FAIL:"
  564: 			printf '%s\n' "$stout" | grep -E 'SELFTEST_FAIL|^  FAIL ' | sed 's/^/          /'
  565: 		fi
  566: 	else
  567: 		good "checker unchanged -- selftest not re-run (fires when it is edited)"
  568: 	fi
  569: fi
  570: 
  571: #  ---- V ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  572: #  THE VERDICT EXTRACTOR DECIDES WHETHER A PANEL WAS SPLIT OR UNANIMOUS, AND IT WAS
  573: #  WRONG ABOUT THAT FOR ITS WHOLE LIFE WITH NOTHING EXECUTING IT.
  574: #
  575: #  Two defects, both found by a seat READING the regex rather than running it: markdown
  576: #  emphasis made a LIVE seat's correct verdict read as a format miss, and a single-word
  577: #  capture collapsed SHIP against SHIP WITH CHANGES and every OPTION A/B/C into one
  578: #  token -- so every split panel this project ran was reported as unanimous.
  579: #
  580: #  Same shape as section R's selftest and wired the same way: it fires only when the
  581: #  extractor itself is touched, and UNTRACKED COUNTS AS TOUCHED, which is the lesson R
  582: #  paid for -- the first version reported "unchanged" on the very commit that
  583: #  introduced the file.
  584: say ""; say "V. the verdict extractor can still be shown to fail"
  585: SVCHK=$_HERE/pipeline/seat_verdicts.py
  586: if [ ! -f "$SVCHK" ]; then
  587: 	warn "seat_verdicts.py MISSING -- panel verdicts are NOT extracted"
  588: else
  589: 	_svtouched=$( { git diff --cached --name-only; git diff --name-only;
  590: 	                git ls-files --others --exclude-standard; } \
  591: 	              | grep -c 'tools/pipeline/seat_verdicts\.py' )
  592: 	if [ "$_svtouched" -gt 0 ]; then
  593: 		if svout=$(python "$SVCHK" --selftest 2>&1); then
  594: 			good "$(printf '%s' "$svout" | grep -E 'SEAT_VERDICTS_SELFTEST_PASS')"
  595: 		else
  596: 			bad "THE VERDICT EXTRACTOR CAN NO LONGER BE SHOWN TO FAIL:"
  597: 			printf '%s
  598: ' "$svout" | grep -E 'SELFTEST_FAIL|^  FAIL ' | sed 's/^/          /'
  599: 		fi
  600: 	else
  601: 		good "extractor unchanged -- selftest not re-run (fires when it is edited)"
  602: 	fi
  603: fi
  604: 
  605: #  ---- W ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  606: #  A ROUND WAS NEARLY REDONE FROM SCRATCH BECAUSE A LEDGER ROW SAID `open` ON WORK
  607: #  THAT HAD SHIPPED HOURS EARLIER.
  608: #
  609: #  `sqreadtype` carried a witness field saying its warrant measurement was PENDING;
  610: #  the measurement is in its own CHANGELOG block and the round shipped as 3ff2138.
  611: #  It was caught by accident -- the row was picked as the next round and the source
  612: #  comment already said the fix was in.  The carrier names this class (a
  613: #  state-vs-substance mismatch) and nothing checked it.
  614: #
  615: #  *** THE FIRST DESIGN WAS VACUOUS AND ITS POSITIVE CONTROL SAID SO. ***  It looked
  616: #  for the row id in a commit SUBJECT; subjects here carry ROUND NUMBERS and name no
  617: #  row, so reinstating the defect did not flag it.  The association that works is the
  618: #  `witness` field, which exists for exactly this purpose: an open row naming a
  619: #  COMMITTED probe is claiming to be unstarted while its detector is in the tree.
  620: #  Negative-controlled both ways; opt out with a `state_note` that says what is left.
  621: #  W ALSO CHECKS THE QUEUE FILE NOW.  Section P checks ledger -> file; nothing
  622: #  checked file -> ledger, and SEVEN headings in OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md said `open` on
  623: #  work the ledger recorded as closed.  That is the EXPENSIVE direction: the
  624: #  carrier's first-stop procedure names that file as the live queue, so each stale
  625: #  heading offers shipped work to a fresh session, which has no reason to doubt it.
  626: #  Deliberately preserved originals are exempt, and the sweep measured exactly one
  627: #  of those against six genuine cases.
  628: say ""; say "W. a row's state agrees with what shipped"
  629: RSCHK=$_HERE/pipeline/check_row_states.py
  630: if [ ! -f "$RSCHK" ]; then
  631: 	bad "check_row_states.py MISSING -- stale row states are NOT verified"
  632: elif rsout=$(python "$RSCHK" 2>&1); then
  633: 	good "$(printf '%s' "$rsout" | grep -E 'ROW_STATES_PASS')"
  634: else
  635: 	bad "A LEDGER ROW SAYS open ON WORK THAT SHIPPED:"
  636: 	printf '%s\n' "$rsout" | grep -E 'STALE|ROW_STATES_FAIL|^ ' | sed 's/^/          /'
  637: fi
  638: 
  639: #  ---- X ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  640: #  A CITED MEASUREMENT MUST EXIST.  The rule "a score claim names its artifact" was
  641: #  already in force and already being followed when it failed TWICE in one day:
  642: #
  643: #    * `#462` cited `probe_base.raw` for 52/52; a later 57-row table REUSED that name,
  644: #      so the claim became CONTRADICTED BY ITS OWN EVIDENCE, not merely unverifiable.
  645: #    * `#461`'s lethal mutant `m_dmarevert` (22/31) has NO per-row artifact at all --
  646: #      0 candidate lines across every `.out` in both scratchpads.  A retrospective audit
  647: #      of that detector was ATTEMPTED on 2026-08-24 and could not run.
  648: #
  649: #  EXISTENCE ONLY, and the scope cut is the safety argument.  It does not parse the log
  650: #  or re-derive the score: section U already checks DENOMINATORS against the gate pin,
  651: #  nothing mechanical can tell a measured numerator from a carried one, and a checker
  652: #  that tried would grow without bound -- the #392 shape.  A gate that stats cited paths
  653: #  cannot creep into policing scratch directories.
  654: #
  655: #  Specified by the fable5 adjudication of #462, Decision 4c, which set both the altitude
  656: #  ("checks committed records, never scratchpad state") and the scope cut.  Forward-only
  657: #  from round 462; earlier blocks predate the rule, and a red that no edit can clear is
  658: #  its own hazard.  NEGATIVE-CONTROLLED ON ITS FIRST RUN: it reported two missing
  659: #  artifacts, both real, and went green only after they were archived to a durable path.
  660: say ""; say "X. every measurement cited in a round block still exists"
  661: CACHK=$_HERE/pipeline/check_cited_artifacts.py
  662: if [ ! -f "$CACHK" ]; then
  663: 	bad "check_cited_artifacts.py MISSING -- cited measurements are NOT verified"
  664: elif caout=$(python "$CACHK" 2>&1); then
  665: 	good "$(printf '%s' "$caout" | grep -E 'CITED_ARTIFACTS_PASS')"
  666: else
  667: 	bad "A ROUND BLOCK CITES A MEASUREMENT THAT DOES NOT EXIST:"
  668: 	printf '%s\n' "$caout" | grep -E 'CITED_ARTIFACTS_FAIL|^    #' | sed 's/^/          /'
  669: 	bad "  restore the artifact, or correct the claim to say the log was not preserved"
  670: fi
  671: 
  672: #  THE SELFTEST FIRES ONLY WHEN THE CHECKER ITSELF CHANGES -- the section-R idiom, and
  673: #  wired the same way.  A completeness check found that this gate shipped WITHOUT one
  674: #  while both its siblings have them: the gate enforcing "every score names verifiable
  675: #  evidence" had no evidence of its own correctness, and the three false-green fixes it
  676: #  went through had nothing pinning them.  ***  IT FAILED ON ITS FIRST RUN AND FOUND A
  677: #  REAL BUG: *** `LOOSE` had been compiled with a literal BACKSPACE byte where `\\b`
  678: #  belonged, so the unenforced-mentions denominator matched NOTHING and had been printing
  679: #  a PHANTOM ZERO all evening -- in the counter added specifically to make phantom zeros
  680: #  visible.  UNTRACKED COUNTS AS TOUCHED, per section R's own scar.
  681: #  DETECT THE TOUCH INLINE, exactly as section R does -- `$CHANGED` is not defined
  682: #  until much later in this file, so the first version of this block never fired.
  683: #  UNTRACKED COUNTS AS TOUCHED: section R's own scar is that a checker was untracked
  684: #  on the very commit that introduced it, so neither diff could see it and the one
  685: #  commit that most needed the selftest was the one that skipped it.
  686: _catouched=$( { git diff --cached --name-only; git diff --name-only; git ls-files --others --exclude-standard; } | grep -c 'tools/pipeline/check_cited_artifacts\.py' )
  687: if [ "$_catouched" -gt 0 ]; then
  688: 	if stout=$(python "$CACHK" --selftest 2>&1); then
  689: 		good "$(printf '%s' "$stout" | grep -E 'SELFTEST_PASS')"
  690: 	else
  691: 		bad "check_cited_artifacts.py SELFTEST FAILED -- the gate is not trustworthy:"
  692: 		printf '%s\n' "$stout" | grep -E 'SELFTEST_FAIL|^    ' | sed 's/^/          /'
  693: 	fi
  694: else
  695: 	good "checker unchanged -- selftest not re-run (fires when it is edited)"
  696: fi
  697: 
  698: #  ---- J ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  699: #  THE CARRIER IS TRACKED BY COPY, AND A COPY THAT NOTHING CHECKS GOES STALE.
  700: #
  701: #  Owner decision, 2026-08-17, asked as a question: track `CLAUDE.md` and `PIPELINE.md`
  702: #  rather than leave the files that direct every session outside version control.
  703: #
  704: #  The literal answer was "track them at the project root" -- and that is not possible,
  705: #  because the git root is GXEMUL-SEC/, one level BELOW the project root where the carrier
  706: #  must sit to be auto-loaded.  (The question's own diagram drew GXEMUL/ as the repo.  It
  707: #  was wrong.)  So the tracked form is a COPY under tools/, and the copy reintroduces
  708: #  exactly the divergence risk the owner rejected when rejecting the dated snapshot.
  709: #
  710: #  This check is what makes the copy safe: byte-identity, HARD, using check A's own `cmp`
  711: #  idiom.  A carrier edit that never reaches the repo is then a red commit rather than a
  712: #  copy that silently describes last week's process.
  713: #
  714: #  tools/ and not GXEMUL-SEC/ deliberately: Claude Code auto-loads CLAUDE.md from the cwd
  715: #  upward, and the session cwd DRIFTS -- it has sat in GXEMUL-SEC/regress for whole rounds.
  716: #  A copy at GXEMUL-SEC/CLAUDE.md would then load ALONGSIDE the root original, putting two
  717: #  copies of a 38 KB carrier in one context.  tools/ is never a cwd ancestor in practice.
  718: #
  719: #  An ABSENT original is not a failure: on a fresh clone the repo copy is the only one
  720: #  there is, and demanding a file that version control cannot deliver would make the check
  721: #  fail hardest in the one situation it exists to serve.
  722: say ""; say "J. tracked carrier copies match the originals"
  723: for _c in CLAUDE.md PIPELINE.md; do
  724: 	if [ ! -f "$SEC/tools/$_c" ]; then
  725: 		bad "$_c has no tracked copy at tools/$_c -- the carrier is untracked again"
  726: 	elif [ ! -f "$ROOT/$_c" ]; then
  727: 		good "tools/$_c tracked; no project-root original (fresh clone -- repo copy is it)"
  728: 	elif cmp -s "$ROOT/$_c" "$SEC/tools/$_c"; then
  729: 		good "tools/$_c is byte-identical to the project-root original"
  730: 	else
  731: 		bad "tools/$_c HAS DRIFTED from $ROOT/$_c -- the tracked carrier is stale."
  732: 		bad "     cp \"$ROOT/$_c\" \"$SEC/tools/$_c\"   (the root file is the live one)"
  733: 	fi
  734: done
  735: 
  736: #  ---- K ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  737: #  A ROW THAT CLOSES WITH COMMITS MUST NAME ITS WITNESS.
  738: #
  739: #  Specified by the flagship seat that adjudicated the WITNESS LADDER, and needed for a
  740: #  measured reason: asked whether the tightened rule would retroactively invalidate any
  741: #  shipped round, a measure seat could only answer by READING ROUND PROSE, because nothing
  742: #  in the schema names the witnessing artefact.  A rule that cannot be audited backwards or
  743: #  enforced forwards is a rule kept by memory.
  744: #
  745: #  It enforces the SHAPE, never the truth -- it cannot tell whether `probe:foo.py` really
  746: #  preserves routing, and a script that claimed to would be the false comfort the ladder was
  747: #  written against.  Its first run found a gap in the ladder itself: none of the four rungs
  748: #  fit a HARNESS round, which ships commits with no guest path to witness.
  749: say ""; say "K. a row that closed with commits names its witness"
  750: WITCHK=$_HERE/pipeline/check_witness.py
  751: if [ ! -f "$WITCHK" ]; then
  752: 	warn "check_witness.py MISSING -- witness naming NOT verified"
  753: elif out=$(python "$WITCHK" 2>&1); then
  754: 	good "$(printf '%s' "$out" | grep -E 'WITNESS_PASS|check_witness:' | tr '
  755: ' ' ')"
  756: else
  757: 	bad "A ROW CLOSED WITH COMMITS AND NO ADMISSIBLE WITNESS:"
  758: 	printf '%s
  759: ' "$out" | sed 's/^/          /'
  760: fi
  761: 
  762: #  ---- L ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  763: #  DOES THE LEDGER KNOW ABOUT THE WORK THAT SHIPPED?
  764: #
  765: #  G, H, I and K all ask whether a row's PROCESS was carried out -- seats fired, seats
  766: #  recorded, owed work queued, witness named.  None of them asks the blunter question:
  767: #  did the row notice that the code landed?  Measured on this check's first run: of the 40
  768: #  most recent commits 33 were named by no row at all, and two of those were sharp --
  769: #  6014b7e's subject reads "selfmutant6 CLOSED" against a row that read held with empty
  770: #  commits, and gate3scope's `commits` named 2a5bc48, the R9/#435 m8820x DEVICE round, which
  771: #  touches zero selfmutant lines.  The board renders from the ledger, so that drift shows
  772: #  finished work as open queue -- the direction that matters, because it is invisible.
  773: #
  774: #  It reports TWO blindnesses every run rather than hiding them: 13 row ids are too short to
  775: #  match safely, and 13 rows carry commits that no scanned subject ever names.  Both are
  776: #  printed on GREEN runs too.
  777: say ""; say "L. the ledger names the work that shipped"
  778: ATTRCHK=$_HERE/pipeline/check_commit_attribution.py
  779: if [ ! -f "$ATTRCHK" ]; then
  780: 	warn "check_commit_attribution.py MISSING -- ledger attribution NOT verified"
  781: elif out=$(python "$ATTRCHK" 2>&1); then
  782: 	good "$(printf '%s' "$out" | grep -E 'ATTRIBUTION_PASS' | tr '\n' ' ')"
  783: 	#  The blindnesses are part of the result, not a footnote -- echo them on green.
  784: 	printf '%s\n' "$out" | grep -E 'NOT MATCHABLE|NEVER NAMED' | sed 's/^/          /'
  785: else
  786: 	bad "THE LEDGER DISAGREES WITH WHAT SHIPPED:"
  787: 	printf '%s\n' "$out" | grep -E '^  HARD' | sed 's/^/          /'
  788: fi
  789: 
  790: #  ---- I ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  791: #  Owner directive 2026-08-17: "make sure to gate and queue up any fable work; don't skip it."
  792: #
  793: #  H proves a stage did not proceed short.  It does NOT prove that the work a short stage
  794: #  still owes was ever written down: H reads a HELD marker as "waiting", and waiting is
  795: #  indistinguishable from forgotten once the round scrolls out of view.  On its first run
  796: #  this check found a closed row (`m8online`) whose regression review had never happened
  797: #  and was recorded nowhere -- and, next to it, two rows that looked identical and had
  798: #  merely lost their attribution.  A held job and a dropped job must not look alike.
  799: say ""; say "I. flagship work that is owed is written down"
  800: FQCHK=$_HERE/pipeline/check_fable_queue.py
  801: if [ ! -f "$FQCHK" ]; then
  802: 	warn "check_fable_queue.py MISSING -- owed flagship work NOT verified"
  803: elif out=$(python "$FQCHK" 2>&1); then
  804: 	good "$(printf '%s' "$out" | grep -E 'FABLE_QUEUE_PASS|check_fable_queue:' | tr '
  805: ' ' ')"
  806: else
  807: 	bad "FLAGSHIP WORK IS OWED AND NOT QUEUED:"
  808: 	printf '%s
  809: ' "$out" | sed 's/^/          /'
  810: fi
  811: 
  812: say ""; say "H. every stage ran the full panel"
  813: STAGECHK=$_HERE/pipeline/check_stage_panels.py
  814: if [ ! -f "$STAGECHK" ]; then
  815: 	warn "check_stage_panels.py MISSING -- stage completeness NOT verified"
  816: elif out=$(python "$STAGECHK" 2>&1); then
  817: 	good "$(printf '%s' "$out" | grep -E 'STAGE_PANELS_PASS|check_stage_panels:' | tr '
  818: ' ' ')"
  819: else
  820: 	bad "A STAGE MOVED ON WITHOUT THE FULL PANEL:"
  821: 	printf '%s
  822: ' "$out" | sed 's/^/          /'
  823: fi
  824: 
  825: say ""; say "G. every seat that answered in a cited panel is recorded"
  826: SEATCHK=$_HERE/pipeline/check_seats_read.py
  827: if [ ! -f "$SEATCHK" ]; then
  828: 	warn "check_seats_read.py MISSING -- seat-recording NOT verified"
  829: elif out=$(python "$SEATCHK" 2>&1); then
  830: 	good "$(printf '%s' "$out" | grep -E 'SEATS_READ_PASS|substantial answers' | tr '
  831: ' ' ')"
  832: else
  833: 	bad "SEATS THAT ANSWERED ARE RECORDED NOWHERE:"
  834: 	printf '%s
  835: ' "$out" | sed 's/^/          /'
  836: 	bad "Read them and add a ledger entry.  If a seat truly had nothing to say,"
  837: 	bad "record THAT explicitly -- a blank cell and a seat failure must not look alike."
  838: fi
  839: 
  840: say ""; say "F. pass-2 receipt for the previous src-touching commit"
  841: LEDGER=$SEC/regress/pass2_ledger.tsv
  842: PREV=$(git -C "$SEC" log -1 --format=%H -- 'src/**' 2>/dev/null || true)
  843: if [ -z "$PREV" ]; then
  844: 	good "no src-touching commit in history"
  845: elif git -C "$SEC" log -1 --format=%s "$PREV" | grep -qiE 'pass 2'; then
  846: 	good "$(git -C "$SEC" log -1 --format=%h "$PREV") is itself a pass-2 commit"
  847: elif [ -f "$LEDGER" ] && awk -v p="$PREV" '!/^#/ && $1 != "" { if (index(p, $1) == 1) found=1 } END { exit !found }' "$LEDGER"; then
  848: 	#  PREFIX match, not equality: git gives a FULL sha and a human writing the receipt
  849: 	#  will naturally paste the SHORT one.  The first version compared them with grep -x
  850: 	#  and could therefore NEVER match -- caught by running the positive control instead
  851: 	#  of only the negative one.  A check that always fails is a different bug from one
  852: 	#  that always passes, and just as useless.
  853: 	good "pass-2 receipt found for $(git -C "$SEC" log -1 --format=%h "$PREV")"
  854: 	grep "^$PREV" "$LEDGER" | sed 's/^/          /'
  855: else
  856: 	bad "NO PASS-2 RECEIPT for $(git -C "$SEC" log -1 --format='%h %s' "$PREV")"
  857: 	bad "A round owes a pass 2 on its own diff.  R6 and R7 both shipped without one,"
  858: 	bad "the second time one round after the first was caught and written down."
  859: 	bad "Run it, then append a line to regress/pass2_ledger.tsv."
  860: fi
  861: 
  862: #  -z + strip the status columns, and take the RIGHT-hand side of a rename.  The old form
  863: #  was `awk '{print $2}'`, which is not a path extractor: `R  old -> new` yields the OLD
  864: #  path so the new file is never checked, and a quoted path containing a space is truncated
  865: #  at the space, silently skipping checks A, B and E for it.
  866: #  *** SCOPED TO WHAT IS ACTUALLY BEING COMMITTED, NOT THE WHOLE WORKING TREE. ***
  867: #
  868: #  This was `git status --porcelain`, i.e. staged AND unstaged together.  That is correct for a
  869: #  single-writer session and WRONG the moment two rounds run in parallel: on 2026-08-21 a
  870: #  records-only commit (tools/ and OUTSTANDING_BUGS only) was blocked by sections A and B firing
  871: #  on `src/machines/machine_hpcmips.c` -- a file a CONCURRENT agent was editing and had not yet
  872: #  propagated, and which the commit did not touch.
  873: #
  874: #  Nothing is weakened by the narrowing.  The un-propagated file is still caught -- at the moment
  875: #  it is committed, because then it IS staged.  What changes is that it no longer blocks an
  876: #  unrelated commit, which is the difference between a gate and an obstacle.
  877: #
  878: #  The fallback matters: with nothing staged (an interactive run, or `git commit -a`), fall back
  879: #  to the full working tree, so a bare invocation still audits everything.  A check that silently
  880: #  audited nothing when run the ordinary way would be worse than the problem being fixed.
  881: CHANGED=$(git diff --cached --name-only)
  882: if [ -z "$CHANGED" ]; then
  883: 	CHANGED=$(git status --porcelain -z | tr '\0' '\n' | sed -e 's/^...//' -e 's/.* -> //')
  884: fi
  885: 
  886: #  ANNOUNCE what the narrowing means for this run, so a divergent file that is merely OUT OF
  887: #  SCOPE is never mistaken for one that was checked and passed.
  888: _unstaged_src=$(git diff --name-only -- 'src/*' | tr '\n' ' ')
  889: if [ -n "$_unstaged_src" ]; then
  890: 	warn "UNSTAGED src/ edits NOT audited by this run (they are when committed): $_unstaged_src"
  891: fi
  892: say ""; say "Z. no tracked script invokes wsl -c with a bare dollar"
  893: #
  894: #  WSL DOLLAR RATCHET.  A wsl -c invocation does not deliver shell variables to the
  895: #  inner shell, EVEN INSIDE SINGLE QUOTES -- measured 2026-08-29 with controls in
  896: #  regress/artifacts/makercfalse/wsl_dollar_20260829.out.  A mutant never applied
  897: #  then scores the BASE binary and reports a clean kill table.
  898: #
  899: #  *** ITS RECALL ON THE RECORDED HISTORY IS ZERO, STATED HERE RATHER THAN
  900: #  DISCOVERED LATER. ***  The class bit FIVE times and every occurrence was an
  901: #  INTERACTIVE, ad-hoc command; the committed tree holds the form four times and all
  902: #  four are QUOTATIONS of the defect in prose.  This check would have caught NONE of
  903: #  them, and it is NOT the primary defence.
  904: #
  905: #  The primary defence is the PreToolUse hook at .claude/hooks/wsl_dollar_guard.py,
  906: #  which fires where all five happened -- at the keyboard.  THIS does the one thing
  907: #  the hook cannot: stop the form being COMMITTED into a script, to run unattended.
  908: #
  909: #  ZERO-POPULATION RATCHET: 229 tracked .sh/.py at introduction, NONE carrying the
  910: #  form, so it grandfathers nothing and needs no dated cutoff.  Controlled both ways
  911: #  on the day it landed: 9/9 line cases, a clean tree passing with its denominator,
  912: #  and an injected violation failing with file:line.
  913: #
  914: #  ABOVE THE EMPTY-CHANGED EXIT ON PURPOSE, for the reason recorded just below about
  915: #  F/G/H/I/J/K: it scans TRACKED files, so a clean tree is exactly when it matters.
  916: if [ -f "$SEC/tools/pipeline/check_wsl_dollar.py" ]; then
  917: 	if wslout=$(python "$SEC/tools/pipeline/check_wsl_dollar.py" "$SEC" 2>&1); then
  918: 		good "$(printf "%s" "$wslout" | sed -e "s/^ *//" | head -1)"
  919: 	else
  920: 		printf "%s\n" "$wslout" | while IFS= read -r l; do bad "$l"; done
  921: 		fail=$((fail+1))
  922: 	fi
  923: else
  924: 	bad "check_wsl_dollar.py MISSING -- section Z proved nothing"
  925: 	fail=$((fail+1))
  926: fi
  927: 
  928: if [ -z "$CHANGED" ]; then
  929: 	say "nothing staged or modified -- no per-file checks."
  930: 	#  *** DO NOT exit 0 HERE.  *** Sections F/G/H/I/J/K have already run above and may have
  931: 	#  set `fail`.  The old form discarded them: measured on a clean tree with a stale carrier
  932: 	#  and a missing pass-2 receipt, it printed SIX `FAIL` lines, no verdict line at all, and
  933: 	#  exited 0.  A clean tree is precisely the state in which those checks are meaningful --
  934: 	#  F was deliberately moved above this exit for that reason, and then the exit threw its
  935: 	#  answer away.
  936: 	if [ $fail -gt 0 ]; then
  937: 		say ""
  938: 		say "=========================================================="
  939: 		say "PRECOMMIT_FAIL   $fail hard, $warn soft"
  940: 		exit 1
  941: 	fi
  942: 	say "PRECOMMIT_PASS   0 hard, $warn soft"
  943: 	exit 0
  944: fi
  945: 
  946: say "=== #418-era PRE-COMMIT REWORK CHECK ==="
  947: say "files in play:"; printf '    %s\n' $CHANGED
  948: 
  949: # ---------------------------------------------------------------- A
  950: #  TWIN-TREE PROPAGATION.  est/ and GXEMUL-SEC differ by exactly five named
  951: #  files plus SEC-only dev_ne2000.c; ANY OTHER src/ file must land byte-
  952: #  identical in both.  #415 is a whole commit that exists because this was
  953: #  skipped once -- and the gate that should have caught it named two files when
  954: #  the truth was three, because an allowlist entry had gone stale.
  955: say ""; say "A. twin-tree propagation (the #415 class -- FULLY mechanical)"
  956: #  DERIVED, NOT PINNED -- because a pinned list has now gone stale TWICE, by one file and
  957: #  then by five.  Measured 2026-08-19: the tree has SEVEN divergent paths (.index,
  958: #  Makefile.skel, autodev.c, dev_jazz.c, machine_arc.c, arcbios.c, and SEC-only
  959: #  dev_ne2000.c) while this list named TWO.  The consequence was not a missed check but an
  960: #  ACTIVELY HARMFUL one: touching any unlisted ARC/Jazz file hard-failed and instructed the
  961: #  operator to overwrite the SEC-only ARC layer with est/'s version.  And `misc.h` was listed
  962: #  while NOT being divergent, so it false-passed a genuine unpropagated edit.
  963: #
  964: #  The tracked file is the declaration; this recomputes reality and compares.  A drift in
  965: #  either direction is then a red row naming the path, instead of a silent wrong instruction.
  966: DIVERGENCE_FILE=$SEC/tools/est_divergence.txt
  967: DIVERGENT_OK=$(grep -vE '^\s*(#|$)' "$DIVERGENCE_FILE" 2>/dev/null | tr '\n' ' ')
  968: if [ -z "$DIVERGENT_OK" ]; then
  969: 	warn "tools/est_divergence.txt missing or empty -- twin-tree allowlist NOT derived"
  970: fi
  971: for f in $CHANGED; do
  972: 	case "$f" in
  973: 	src/*) ;;
  974: 	*) continue ;;
  975: 	esac
  976: 	skip=0
  977: 	for d in $DIVERGENT_OK; do [ "$f" = "$d" ] && skip=1; done
  978: 	[ $skip = 1 ] && { good "$f (known-divergent, not compared)"; continue; }
  979: 	if [ ! -f "$EST/$f" ]; then
  980: 		soft "$f has no counterpart in est/ -- SEC-only?  Confirm deliberately."
  981: 	elif cmp -s "$SEC/$f" "$EST/$f"; then
  982: 		good "$f == est/"
  983: 	else
  984: 		bad "$f DIFFERS from est/ -- propagate it, then cmp against THE REPO (never a backup)"
  985: 	fi
  986: done
  987: 
  988: # ---------------------------------------------------------------- B
  989: #  BUILD-TREE STALENESS.  The build trees have NO VPATH, so an unpropagated
  990: #  edit means you measured the wrong binary.  A .MUTANT sentinel means a prior
  991: #  run left a tree mutated and NOTHING measured against it can be trusted.
  992: say ""; say "B. build tree (measuring the wrong binary is worse than not measuring)"
  993: if [ -f "$BLD/.MUTANT" ]; then
  994: 	bad ".MUTANT sentinel present in build/ -- restore from SEC and rebuild BEFORE trusting any measurement:"
  995: 	sed 's/^/          /' "$BLD/.MUTANT"
  996: else
  997: 	good "no .MUTANT sentinel"
  998: fi
  999: for f in $CHANGED; do
 1000: 	case "$f" in src/*) ;; *) continue ;; esac
 1001: 	[ -f "$BLD/$f" ] || continue
 1002: 	if cmp -s "$SEC/$f" "$BLD/$f"; then good "$f == build/"
 1003: 	else bad "$f DIFFERS from build/ -- the measured binary is not this source"; fi
 1004: done
 1005: 
 1006: #  *** build-asan/ JOINED THE PROPAGATION SET BY OWNER RULING, 2026-08-27. ***
 1007: #
 1008: #  It was measured FOUR ROUNDS STALE on 2 of 4 sampled files, and it is the tree
 1009: #  that catches heap overflows.  Two findings in one session turned on an ASAN
 1010: #  build: a heap-buffer-overflow mutant that the ordinary detector scored 52/52
 1011: #  GREEN, and a NULL dereference.  A stale ASAN tree is a detector that has
 1012: #  silently stopped detecting -- worse than no detector, because it reassures.
 1013: #
 1014: #  ABSENCE IS NOT A FAILURE.  On a machine that never built the tree the rows are
 1015: #  skipped, exactly as section A treats a missing est/.  A gate that hard-fails on
 1016: #  a tree someone chose not to build is a gate that gets switched off.
 1017: ASAN=$ROOT/build-asan
 1018: if [ -d "$ASAN/src" ]; then
 1019: 	if [ -f "$ASAN/.MUTANT" ]; then
 1020: 		bad ".MUTANT sentinel present in build-asan/ -- restore from SEC and REBUILD:"
 1021: 		sed 's/^/          /' "$ASAN/.MUTANT"
 1022: 	else
 1023: 		good "no .MUTANT sentinel in build-asan/"
 1024: 	fi
 1025: 	for f in $CHANGED; do
 1026: 		case "$f" in src/*) ;; *) continue ;; esac
 1027: 		[ -f "$ASAN/$f" ] || continue
 1028: 		if cmp -s "$SEC/$f" "$ASAN/$f"; then good "$f == build-asan/"
 1029: 		else bad "$f DIFFERS from build-asan/ -- the sanitiser tree is not this source"; fi
 1030: 	done
 1031: else
 1032: 	say "      --    build-asan/ absent -- rows skipped, not failed"
 1033: fi
 1034: 
 1035: #  *** THE FOURTH TREE.  As of 2026-08-30 the no-VPATH rule names FOUR build trees --
 1036: #  est/, build/, build-asan/ and WSL /tmp/gxsec-build -- and this gate enforced THREE.
 1037: #  Filed as `novpadfourth` and left open on the assumption that a WSL tree is
 1038: #  unreachable from the Windows side. ***
 1039: #
 1040: #  *** THIS COMMENT SAID "HAS ALWAYS NAMED FOUR", AND THAT WAS BOTH UNDATED AND FALSE.
 1041: #  A panel seat caught it on the diff, under the standing license. ***  `build-asan/`
 1042: #  joined the propagation set BY OWNER RULING ON 2026-08-27 -- the section thirty lines
 1043: #  above says so in its own first line -- so the set has been four for three days, not
 1044: #  always.  An empirical claim about a RULE'S HISTORY, sitting undated in a tool
 1045: #  comment, is the same defect this round repaired in a probe's header: a sentence that
 1046: #  is wrong on the day it is committed and that nothing re-reads.
 1047: #
 1048: #  THE HYGIENE RULE GENERALISES, and the seat stated it better than this comment can:
 1049: #  **claims about prior state either cite the rule's text and date, or are DATED.**
 1050: #  That applies to TOOL comments exactly as it does to probe comments.
 1051: #
 1052: #  MEASURED 2026-08-30, and the assumption is false: `//wsl.localhost/<distro>/tmp/...`
 1053: #  is readable from git-bash, and a `cmp` across that boundary completes in 83 ms.  The
 1054: #  obstacle was never technical; the check simply was never written.
 1055: #
 1056: #  THE HAZARD IT CLOSES IS THE SAME ONE build-asan/ CLOSES, and this tree is the one
 1057: #  the arc rig actually runs: a stale source here means a measurement attributed to a
 1058: #  commit that the binary never contained.
 1059: #
 1060: #  ABSENCE IS NOT A FAILURE, for two reasons rather than one: the tree may never have
 1061: #  been built, AND the WSL distro may simply not be running, in which case the UNC path
 1062: #  is unreadable and nothing is wrong.  Both skip.  A gate that hard-fails because a VM
 1063: #  is asleep is a gate that gets switched off.
 1064: WSLT=${GXWSLTREE:-//wsl.localhost/Gentoo/tmp/gxsec-build}
 1065: if [ -d "$WSLT/src" ]; then
 1066: 	if [ -f "$WSLT/.MUTANT" ]; then
 1067: 		bad ".MUTANT sentinel present in the WSL tree -- restore from SEC and REBUILD:"
 1068: 		sed 's/^/          /' "$WSLT/.MUTANT"
 1069: 	else
 1070: 		good "no .MUTANT sentinel in the WSL tree"
 1071: 	fi
 1072: 	for f in $CHANGED; do
 1073: 		case "$f" in src/*) ;; *) continue ;; esac
 1074: 		[ -f "$WSLT/$f" ] || continue
 1075: 		if cmp -s "$SEC/$f" "$WSLT/$f"; then good "$f == WSL /tmp/gxsec-build"
 1076: 		else bad "$f DIFFERS from the WSL tree -- the arc rig's source is not this source"; fi
 1077: 	done
 1078: else
 1079: 	say "      --    WSL /tmp/gxsec-build unreachable (tree absent, or the distro is"
 1080: 	say "            not running) -- rows skipped, not failed"
 1081: fi
 1082: 
 1083: #  *** THE PreToolUse HOOK'S 18 CONTROLS ARE NOW RUN BY SOMETHING.  Until 2026-08-30
 1084: #  they were run by nothing -- filed as `hookctlgate`. ***
 1085: #
 1086: #  THE CASE IS MEASURED, NOT HYPOTHETICAL: that hook shipped on 2026-08-29 with FOUR
 1087: #  defects and nobody noticed for hours -- two false negatives (including the multi-line
 1088: #  `bash -c` shape that actually bites) and two false positives that line-scoping was
 1089: #  adopted to fix and did not.  A guard that fires on every Bash call had no regression
 1090: #  test that anything executed.
 1091: #
 1092: #  CONDITIONAL ON PURPOSE.  The suite takes ~28 s, which is too slow for every commit
 1093: #  and is exactly how a gate earns being switched off.  It runs only when the hook, its
 1094: #  shim, or the suite itself is in the changed set -- which is when it can regress.
 1095: #
 1096: #  NOT a regress gate: it tests a Claude-Code hook, not the emulator, so no battery gate
 1097: #  can host it.  This is the harness gate, which is where harness properties belong.
 1098: HOOKCTL="$SEC/regress/artifacts/makercfalse/hook_controls_20260829/hookctl.py"
 1099: hookctl_wanted=no
 1100: for f in $CHANGED; do
 1101: 	case "$f" in
 1102: 	tools/hooks/wsl_dollar_guard.py|regress/artifacts/makercfalse/hook_controls_20260829/hookctl.py)
 1103: 		hookctl_wanted=yes ;;
 1104: 	esac
 1105: done
 1106: if [ "$hookctl_wanted" = yes ] && [ -f "$HOOKCTL" ] && command -v python > /dev/null 2>&1; then
 1107: 	#  *** PASS AN EXPLICIT OUTPUT PATH.  The suite defaults to `hookctl.out` in the
 1108: 	#  CURRENT DIRECTORY, so the first run of this section dropped a stray file in
 1109: 	#  the repo root -- caught by `git status` one minute after the commit that
 1110: 	#  introduced the section.  A gate that litters the tree it guards is a gate
 1111: 	#  people delete. ***
 1112: 	hctmp=$(mktemp 2>/dev/null || echo "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/hookctl.$$.out")
 1113: 	hcout=$(python "$HOOKCTL" "$hctmp" 2>&1)
 1114: 	rm -f "$hctmp"
 1115: 	if printf '%s' "$hcout" | grep -q HOOKCTL_DONE; then
 1116: 		hcn=$(printf '%s' "$hcout" | grep -oE '[0-9]+ of [0-9]+ controls pass' | head -1)
 1117: 		hcw=$(printf '%s' "$hcout" | grep -oE '[0-9]+ wrong' | head -1)
 1118: 		case "$hcw" in
 1119: 		"0 wrong") good "wsl-dollar hook controls: $hcn" ;;
 1120: 		*)         bad  "wsl-dollar hook controls: $hcn, $hcw -- the guard regressed" ;;
 1121: 		esac
 1122: 	else
 1123: 		#  No sentinel is NOT a pass.  The suite refuses outright if the hook is
 1124: 		#  missing, and that refusal must read as a failure rather than a skip.
 1125: 		bad "wsl-dollar hook controls did not reach HOOKCTL_DONE:"
 1126: 		printf '%s\n' "$hcout" | tail -3 | sed 's/^/          /'
 1127: 	fi
 1128: elif [ "$hookctl_wanted" = yes ]; then
 1129: 	soft "the hook changed but its control suite could not be run -- NOT passed"
 1130: fi
 1131: 
 1132: # ---------------------------------------------------------------- C
 1133: #  A MESSAGE THAT OUTRUNS ITS DIFF.  A commit message once claimed a citation
 1134: #  fix that no hunk made.  Write messages FROM THE DIFF.  Decidable half: a
 1135: #  source path named in the message that no hunk touches.
 1136: say ""; say "C. commit message vs diff (write messages FROM THE DIFF)"
 1137: if [ -n "$MSGFILE" ] && [ -f "$MSGFILE" ]; then
 1138: 	for p in $(grep -oE '\b(src|regress|man)/[A-Za-z0-9_./-]+\.(c|h|sh|1)\b' "$MSGFILE" | sort -u); do
 1139: 		if printf '%s\n' $CHANGED | grep -qx "$p"; then good "message cites $p, and the diff touches it"
 1140: 		else bad "message cites $p but NO HUNK TOUCHES IT -- the claim outruns the diff"; fi
 1141: 	done
 1142: 	[ -z "$(grep -oE '\b(src|regress|man)/[A-Za-z0-9_./-]+\.(c|h|sh|1)\b' "$MSGFILE")" ] &&
 1143: 		good "message cites no source paths (nothing to cross-check)"
 1144: else
 1145: 	soft "no commit-message file passed -- check C skipped, NOT passed"
 1146: fi
 1147: 
 1148: # ---------------------------------------------------------------- D
 1149: #  ADDED CITATIONS AND SELF-INVALIDATING COUNTS.
 1150: #  D1: a `file.c:123` citation added by this diff whose line does not exist.
 1151: #      #414 invalidated a line-range citation elsewhere in the tree by moving
 1152: #      code; prefer naming the CONSTRUCT over the line.
 1153: #  D2: #418's rule -- a comment may not state a count of an identifier it also
 1154: #      spells.  Saying "occurs in exactly seven places" in a sentence naming
 1155: #      the token MADE THE COUNT EIGHT.
 1156: say ""; say "D. added citations and self-invalidating counts"
 1157: ADDED=$(git diff -U0 | grep '^+' | grep -v '^+++')
 1158: CITES=$(printf '%s\n' "$ADDED" | grep -oE '\b[a-z0-9_]+\.(c|h)\b:[0-9]+' | sort -u)
 1159: if [ -z "$CITES" ]; then good "no new file:line citations"; else
 1160: 	for c in $CITES; do
 1161: 		cf=${c%%:*}; cl=${c##*:}
 1162: 		p=$(git ls-files "*/$cf" | head -1)
 1163: 		if [ -z "$p" ]; then soft "cited $c -- file not found in the repo, check by hand"
 1164: 		elif [ "$(wc -l < "$p")" -lt "$cl" ]; then bad "cited $c but $p has only $(wc -l < "$p") lines"
 1165: 		else good "cited $c exists (READ THE LINE -- existence is not correctness)"; fi
 1166: 	done
 1167: fi
 1168: #  D2 REFINEMENT, found by running this check against the very commit that
 1169: #  established its rule: all three hits were the OLD wrong claim QUOTED INSIDE
 1170: #  ITS OWN CORRECTION.  The rule is about ASSERTING a count, not MENTIONING
 1171: #  one -- a correction must be able to quote what it corrects, and a
 1172: #  strikethrough is a quotation by definition.  Assertion-vs-quotation is not
 1173: #  reliably decidable by regex, so this does not guess: a count inside quote
 1174: #  marks or `~~` becomes a SOFT warning that names the ambiguity, and a bare
 1175: #  one stays hard.  A check that cannot decide should hand the call back, which
 1176: #  is a different thing from a check that cannot fail.
 1177: COUNTS=$(printf '%s\n' "$ADDED" | grep -inE 'exactly (one|two|three|four|five|six|seven|eight|nine|ten|[0-9]+) (places|occurrences|sites)|occurs in [0-9]+ (places|occurrences|sites)|[0-9]+ occurrences of')
 1178: if [ -z "$COUNTS" ]; then good "no added occurrence counts"; else
 1179: 	hard_hits=$(printf '%s\n' "$COUNTS" | grep -v '~~' | grep -v '"' || true)
 1180: 	quoted=$(printf '%s\n' "$COUNTS" | grep -E '~~|"' || true)
 1181: 	if [ -n "$hard_hits" ]; then
 1182: 		bad "an added line ASSERTS an OCCURRENCE COUNT.  A record may not state a count of an identifier it also spells (#418) -- state the structural property instead:"
 1183: 		printf '%s\n' "$hard_hits" | sed 's/^/          /' | head -5
 1184: 	fi
 1185: 	if [ -n "$quoted" ]; then
 1186: 		soft "a count appears inside quote marks or strikethrough -- reads as a QUOTATION"
 1187: 		soft "of a corrected claim, which is legitimate.  CONFIRM it is not a fresh assertion:"
 1188: 		printf '%s\n' "$quoted" | sed 's/^/          /' | head -5
 1189: 	fi
 1190: 	[ -z "$hard_hits" ] && good "all count mentions are quoted/struck (see the warnings above)"
 1191: fi
 1192: 
 1193: # ---------------------------------------------------------------- E
 1194: #  DETECTOR HYGIENE.  Not a correctness check and does not pretend to be.
 1195: #  E1: a new regress/diff_*.c should carry an identity guard -- two files with
 1196: #      the same name once differed only by parent-directory case, and the stale
 1197: #      one encoded a REVERSED design decision.
 1198: #  E2: ONLY A MUTATION TEST PROVES A ROW REACHES THE CODE IT NAMES.  This can
 1199: #      only ask whether one was run; it cannot run it for you.
 1200: say ""; say "E. detector hygiene (NOT a correctness check)"
 1201: NEWDET=$(printf '%s\n' $CHANGED | grep -E '^regress/diff_.*\.c$' || true)
 1202: if [ -z "$NEWDET" ]; then good "no detector added or modified"; else
 1203: 	for d in $NEWDET; do
 1204: 		if grep -q 'IDENTITY' "$SEC/$d"; then good "$d carries an identity guard"
 1205: 		else bad "$d has NO identity guard -- add a row asserting its own row count"; fi
 1206: 	done
 1207: 	soft "MUTATION RUN NOT VERIFIABLE HERE.  Confirm by hand: every new row killed at"
 1208: 	soft "least one mutant, each kill NAMED ITS ROW, and no mutant merely failed to build"
 1209: 	soft "(a build fault is a FAULT, not a detection)."
 1210: fi
 1211: 
 1212: say ""; say "Y. no tracked TEXT file has gained a NUL byte"
 1213: #
 1214: #  WHY THIS EXISTS.  A shell heredoc once wrote three literal NUL bytes into two
 1215: #  tracked text files (row `nulheredoc`).  The damage is not the bytes -- it is that
 1216: #  grep then treats the file as BINARY and silently stops reporting matches.  Almost
 1217: #  every gate here is grep-based, so one NUL can hollow out a whole gate while every
 1218: #  row still reads green.
 1219: #
 1220: #  *** THIS CHECK WAS WRITTEN WRONG TWICE ON 2026-08-27 AND BOTH FAILURES ARE THE
 1221: #  REASON IT LOOKS LIKE THIS. ***
 1222: #
 1223: #  (1) The first version used `LC_ALL=C grep -qm1 -P "\\x00"`.  **grep REFUSES to
 1224: #      run -P under LC_ALL=C** -- "supports only unibyte and UTF-8 locales" -- and with
 1225: #      -q plus 2>/dev/null that refusal is invisible.  Every file reported clean.  A
 1226: #      check that cannot fail, built inside a checker.  The same mistake made an
 1227: #      earlier 1161-file census inert: it reported 0 hits because grep never ran.
 1228: #
 1229: #  (2) Writing that pattern through a heredoc turned `\\x00` into an actual NUL
 1230: #      byte in this script -- reproducing `nulheredoc`s exact mechanism inside the
 1231: #      check meant to catch it.  Ordinary grep then called precommit_check.sh binary.
 1232: #
 1233: #  So: `tr -cd` and not grep.  No regex engine, no -P, no locale dependency, and no
 1234: #  backslash to survive a quoting layer.  If you edit this line, re-run the negative
 1235: #  control below; do not assume a pattern-based rewrite is equivalent.
 1236: #
 1237: #  Scoped to text extensions on purpose: a future tracked .pdf or .png carries NULs
 1238: #  legitimately, and widening this to every tracked file would make it a check that
 1239: #  has to be switched off, which is worse than no check.
 1240: nulhits=0
 1241: nulscanned=0
 1242: for f in $(git -C "$SEC" ls-files -- "*.c" "*.h" "*.py" "*.sh" "*.md" "*.json" "*.txt" "*.tsv" 2>/dev/null); do
 1243: 	[ -f "$SEC/$f" ] || continue
 1244: 	nulscanned=$((nulscanned+1))
 1245: 	if [ "$(tr -cd "\000" < "$SEC/$f" | wc -c)" -gt 0 ]; then
 1246: 		bad "$f contains a NUL byte -- grep will treat it as BINARY and go quiet"
 1247: 		nulhits=$((nulhits+1))
 1248: 	fi
 1249: done
 1250: if [ "$nulscanned" -eq 0 ]; then
 1251: 	bad "NUL check scanned ZERO files -- the ls-files pattern selected nothing, so it"
 1252: 	bad "proved nothing.  A pattern that matches nothing reports 0 for everything."
 1253: elif [ "$nulhits" -eq 0 ]; then
 1254: 	good "no NUL bytes in $nulscanned tracked text files"
 1255: fi
 1256: 
 1257: say ""
 1258: say "=========================================================="
 1259: if [ $fail -gt 0 ]; then
 1260: 	say "PRECOMMIT_FAIL   $fail hard, $warn soft"
 1261: 	say "A hard failure is a rework class this project has already paid for."
 1262: 	exit 1
 1263: fi
 1264: say "PRECOMMIT_PASS   0 hard, $warn soft"
 1265: say "Decidable checks passed.  This is NOT evidence about correctness --"
 1266: say "the mutation run and the truth of any claim remain yours."
 1267: exit 0
