tools/pipeline/check_row_states.py:7: `sqreadtype` sat at state `open` with a witness field reading "the
tools/pipeline/check_row_states.py:8: oracle-correction rule requires the NEW pin measured RED on the COMMITTED build
tools/pipeline/check_row_states.py:9: before any source edit -- that measurement is the round's warrant and is
tools/pipeline/check_row_states.py:10: PENDING".  The round had shipped hours earlier as `3ff2138`, and that
tools/pipeline/check_row_states.py:11: measurement is recorded in its own CHANGELOG block: re-pinned oracle 26/31 RED
tools/pipeline/check_row_states.py:12: on the committed build, 31/31 on the fixed build, in that order.
tools/pipeline/check_row_states.py:14: It was caught by accident.  The row was picked as the NEXT round, and the source
tools/pipeline/check_row_states.py:15: comment already said "#454: JUDGED AS A READ".  Without that coincidence the
tools/pipeline/check_row_states.py:16: work would have been redone.
tools/pipeline/check_row_states.py:18: The carrier already names this class -- a state-vs-substance mismatch, where
tools/pipeline/check_row_states.py:19: "a future reader who trusts states reads that as 'not done', which is false".
tools/pipeline/check_row_states.py:20: Nothing checked it.
tools/pipeline/check_row_states.py:22: *** THE FIRST DESIGN WAS VACUOUS, AND ITS POSITIVE CONTROL SAID SO. ***
tools/pipeline/check_row_states.py:24: It looked for the row's id in a commit SUBJECT.  Reinstating `sqreadtype` as
tools/pipeline/check_row_states.py:25: open did not flag it, because commit subjects here carry ROUND NUMBERS -- the
tools/pipeline/check_row_states.py:26: one that shipped it reads "#454 the store-queue PREF transfer is judged READ
tools/pipeline/check_row_states.py:27: type" -- and name no row at all.  Measured: the id appears zero times in that
tools/pipeline/check_row_states.py:28: commit's message and zero times in its ledger diff.  A check that cannot catch
tools/pipeline/check_row_states.py:29: the incident that motivated it is worse than no check, and only running the
tools/pipeline/check_row_states.py:30: positive control revealed it.
tools/pipeline/check_row_states.py:32: *** THE ASSOCIATION THAT WORKS IS THE `witness` FIELD, and it is there by
tools/pipeline/check_row_states.py:33: design. ***
tools/pipeline/check_row_states.py:35: A row's `witness` names the artefact that proves the defect reachable --
tools/pipeline/check_row_states.py:36: `probe:<path>`, `boot:<rig>`, `construction:<gate>` -- forward-only since
tools/pipeline/check_row_states.py:37: 2026-08-19.  A row that names a PROBE is asserting that probe exists.  So:
tools/pipeline/check_row_states.py:39:     an `open` row whose witness names a probe file that has been COMMITTED
tools/pipeline/check_row_states.py:40:     is a row claiming to be unstarted while its detector is in the tree.
tools/pipeline/check_row_states.py:42: That is narrow on purpose.  It says nothing about rows with `witness: none`
tools/pipeline/check_row_states.py:43: (the honest state for an unstarted item), nothing about `in work` (which means
tools/pipeline/check_row_states.py:44: exactly "committed something, still working", so a commit proves nothing), and
tools/pipeline/check_row_states.py:45: nothing about rows whose witness is a boot or a construction gate that predates
tools/pipeline/check_row_states.py:46: them.
tools/pipeline/check_row_states.py:48: Measured on the tree the day it was written: the reinstated defect is FLAGGED,
tools/pipeline/check_row_states.py:49: naming the probe and the shipping commit; with the row correctly closed, 0
tools/pipeline/check_row_states.py:50: flagged, and no other open row trips it.
tools/pipeline/check_row_states.py:52: A row may opt out with `"state_note"` explaining why code shipped under its name
tools/pipeline/check_row_states.py:53: while it is genuinely still open -- a partial fix, or a sibling site left for a
tools/pipeline/check_row_states.py:54: later round.  The note is required to say which; an empty opt-out is the silent
tools/pipeline/check_row_states.py:55: exemption this harness keeps being bitten by.
tools/pipeline/check_row_states.py:56: """
