tools/pipeline\ledger.json:2107:     "note": "*** CLOSE IT -- MEASURED FIXED, AND MEASURED TO HAVE HAD NIL GUEST HARM EVEN WHEN BROKEN. ***  Reading the fixed code cannot prove the row was ever a defect, so the seat built a discriminator on one property (t->interval == 1.0/t->freq) and compiled the SAME DRIVER against shipped timer.c and against `git show 39748e3:src/core/timer.c`:\n    shipped 020a672  12 rows, 0 fails GREEN     upstream 39748e3  5 fails RED\nThree things a reading could not establish: (1) the row WAS real -- 5 of 12 go red pre-fix, so not vacuous; (2) *** timer_add WAS NEVER THE DEFECTIVE PATH *** -- upstream already clamped before storing there, and only timer_update_frequency stored raw, so the row's title ('the timer core') names a function too broadly; (3) grep -rn '->freq' src/ outside timer.c returns NOTHING -- t->freq is read only by the early-out inside timer_update_frequency itself, which pre-fix held raw-vs-raw, so idempotence survived too.\n\n*** AND THE BRIEF HEADER I WROTE IS A WRONG RECORD THAT SPLIT THE PANEL. ***  It says 'the LAST THREE unpanelled rows, each filed by ONE seat, none ever panelled.' The ledger gives tfreq 29 ENTRIES ACROSS ALL FOUR PHASES FROM ALL NINE SEATS, and the brief's own body quotes fourteen of them. The real criterion was 'thin ASSESS cell', not 'never panelled'. Seats trusting the header saw an open unreviewed defect; seats reading the body saw a finished round. Both were reading the same packet.\n\ntfreq/n2/n4 carry IDENTICAL closure notes, the same three commits (863d238 b9daca1 cdc4849) and the same hold. They are ONE ROUND WEARING THREE NAMES, active only because nobody flipped the state.\n\n---\n*** PHASE CORRECTED 2026-08-20: this answer was first filed under `review` and belongs in `assess`. ***  The cluster brief it answers asked the assess questionnaire -- rank, drop/merge, fix-vs-document-vs-detector, reachable-vs-theoretical, smallest harm, witnessable at what rung, what the filing seats missed -- and none of that is a diff review. Fourteen rows carried the same mis-filing.\n\nIt was caught by precommit section H, which reported something else: 'tfreq CLOSED-WHILE-HELD, a round may not close while a stage awaits a seat.' True as stated, and the cause was not a missing seat -- opus5 HAD answered, at 1628 bytes, into the wrong phase. A gate that names a real problem by the wrong mechanism is still the gate earning its keep; the alternative was closing fourteen rows over holds that looked live.",
tools/pipeline\ledger.json:2121:   "witness": "harness:offline differential of the SAME driver against `git show 39748e3:src/core/timer.c` -- 12 rows on t->interval == 1.0/t->freq, 0 fails on shipped, 5 fails on upstream. NOT a rung-2-4 witness and must not be read as one: it is a rung-1 direct-call detector, which the ladder says is NEVER a reproduction. It proves the row was real (not vacuous) and that the defect is gone; it does not prove the path was guest-reachable -- and separately grep shows t->freq is read NOWHERE outside timer.c, so the guest could never have observed it."
tools/pipeline\ledger.json:4842:     "note": "*** VERDICT: KEEP OPEN. The python half is PARTLY closed by a different tool; the directory half is UNCHANGED and now WORSE; and it MEASURED A FOURTH BLIND SPOT THE ROW DOES NOT NAME, which makes check D vacuous on the normal commit path. *** LINE NUMBERS: the row cites :155 and :92, and at HEAD they are :970 and :907 -- ~800 lines of drift with the TEXT IDENTICAL. It reconciled this explicitly as intra-file growth rather than the wrong-tree signature (which shifts arms and changes which cases exist). PYTHON HALF, split answer: check E is unchanged and can never see a python file, but section R's check_probe_wiring.py sees all 56 python artefacts. **They answer different questions** -- E asks whether a detector carries an identity guard, R asks whether anything runs it -- so the gap is real but narrower than recorded. For a python probe the identity-guard analogue is a gate-side `rows run` pin, **and nothing requires a new probe to have one**. *** THAT IS THE SAME HOLE AS `gateflr`, and one extension to check_probe_wiring.py closes both. *** DIRECTORY HALF, unchanged and BROADER than the row says: check C's prefix alternation is `(src|regress|man)`, so **the entire `tools/` tree is invisible** -- precommit_check.sh cannot cross-check a commit message naming ITSELF, or panel.sh, or any pipeline checker. The alternation predates the 2026-08-17 harness migration into git, so this is NEW, not a restatement. It tabulated nine representative strings; `regress/drive_guest.py`, `tools/...`, and bare filenames are all invisible. The false-green is still there verbatim: no match -> `good \"message cites no source paths\"`, an ok line about a message it could not read. *** THE FOURTH BLIND SPOT: CHECK D AUDITS A DIFFERENT CHANGE SET FROM EVERY OTHER SECTION. *** `$CHANGED` prefers `git diff --cached`; check D uses BARE `git diff -U0` -- unstaged only, always. Sections R and V both use BOTH and carry a comment saying they learned it the hard way. **So on the ordinary workflow -- git add, run precommit, commit -- `$ADDED` is EMPTY and both of D's sub-checks print green about a diff they never read.** Its negative control for that is elegant and uses material already in the tree: the two stale `.sh:NNN` citations it found in check_probe_wiring.py's docstring are exactly what a widened check must name, plus a correct `.sh:NNN` that must stay green so the check is not merely 'reject all shell citations'."
tools/pipeline\ledger.json:7227:     "note": "*** REPRODUCED AT RUNG 3 -- AND THREE OF THIS ROW'S CLAIMS ARE MEASURED FALSE. ***  The defect is real; the severity argument that put it top of a cluster is not.\n\nTHE WITNESS: unmodified in-tree `testppc` (-E testppc -C PPC750 -M 32), cold debugger, real guest instructions, real decode, real memory_rw. A PPC guest sets DBAT0 over a 128 KB window, sets MSR[DR]=1, and executes `stw r6,0(r5)` with r5 = 0x1201fffe -- unaligned, crossing the 1 KB split granule. Head (2 bytes) reaches the in-tree `fb` device; tail (2 bytes) has no BAT and no PTE, so a real DSI fires; a planted `rfi` at the DSI vector is the GUEST'S OWN restart. The same instruction re-executes, unbounded. Counters: one architectural store, FOUR device writes. **The trace is from a fully stock HEAD binary with zero instrumentation** -- the counters only counted. Controls all green: liveness (RAM through the same decode), DEVICE SIGNATURE (same program, only DBAT0L changed: PA->device reads back 0x3344, PA->no device reads back 0x0000), and a negative control two bytes lower that does not split.\n\nCLAIM 1 -- 'not introduced by #430' -- MEASURED FALSE. Built from `git show 2458cfb^:src/cpus/memory_rw.c`, stock otherwise: the same guest program produces NO exception, NO restart, ONE device write. Pre-#430 the tail was silently mis-applied to the physically-following location -- the defect #430 fixed -- so there was nothing to restart. **#430 CREATED the fault, and the double-application is a direct consequence of it.**\n\nCLAIM 2 -- 'the memblock split has had this property all along' -- MEASURED FALSE, twice. Post-#430 the memblock split can only fire on PHYSICAL accesses (a translated 4 KB write across 1 MB costs 4 translations / 4 host lookups; PHYSICAL costs 0 / 2), and its head can NEVER be a device in either version because device dispatch `goto do_return_ok`s first -- measured with a device straddling the memblock boundary.\n\nCLAIM 3 -- '256x' -- MEASURED WRONG: it is **1024x**. The granule was measured by walking offsets, not read: MRW_SPLIT_MASK is 0x3ff, so 1 MB / 1024 = 1024. The 256 came from comparing 1 MB to a 4 KB page -- the granule this split does not use. *** THE SAME WRONG NUMBER WAS ALSO IN THE SOURCE, at memory_rw.c's own 'one boundary in 256' comment: ONE mis-derived constant became TWO records defects, and the row repeated it as a severity argument. Both corrected. ***\n\nREACHABILITY IS FAR NARROWER THAN THE ROW IMPLIES, and this is why it re-ranks DOWN:\n  * ONLY PowerPC can emit a straddling store at all. MIPS (AdES; swl/swr do an ALIGNED RMW), ARM, SH, M88K and Alpha all force alignment -- each checked in source. PPC's `if (addr & (LS_SIZE-1))` at cpu_ppc_instr_loadstore.c:229 is a FAST-PATH BAIL-OUT, not an exception, so the generic path passes the unaligned address through.\n  * PPC is also the ONLY family whose device-failure path can restart: memory_rw's device-failure arm raises an exception only under #ifdef MEM_MIPS / #ifdef MEM_M88K.\n  * THREE REAL GUEST BOOTS, instrumented: OpenBSD/pmax to root login, OpenBSD/luna88k to `login:`, NetBSD/macppc to the root-device prompt -- **ZERO straddling stores from guest code**. The seat first read macppc's 95 split-loop writes as guest activity and REFUTED ITSELF by logging the addresses: all are len=65536 at pc=0x0, i.e. file_elf.c:448's loader chunks running before any guest instruction.\n  * Of the 1024 offsets in a granule only ELEVEN (0x3f9..0x3ff) can start a straddling 2/4/8-byte access, and the head's device offset is always within 7 bytes BELOW a 1 KB boundary -- so a register at offset 0x00/0x10/0x20 can never be a straddle head.\n\n*** AND THE FIX MAY ITSELF BE WRONG. ***  Partial completion on a page-crossing misaligned store, with restart re-applying the first part, is what real PowerPC hardware does -- so gxemul may be CORRECT here and a fix may introduce a divergence. There is no PowerPC manual in _scratchpad/ (ARM, MIPS and m88k only), so the seat stated that as REASONING, NOT A CITATION -- the same trap CLAUDE.md records for SH. **Obtain a PPC manual before any edit.**\n\nThe minimal fix, if one is wanted: a dry-run pre-pass on WRITES only, walking the chunks with FLAG_NOEXCEPTIONS and committing only if all translate. Zero cost on the hot path -- it sits inside the `while (len > granule - offset)` body, which no aligned access enters. Watch TLB-miss handlers with side effects (PPC603 software reload) and do not let the dry run update referenced/changed bits.\n\nmrwstore2_rung3.py is close to shippable as a regress detector; its controls already satisfy the liveness and device-signature clauses.",
