tools/pipeline\ledger.json:9986:     "note": "ATTRIBUTION CORRECTED, and a new check found it. The `commits` field read `2a5bc48`, which is the R9/#435 m8820x DEVICE round: `git show 2a5bc48 -- regress/gate_offline.sh | grep -c selfmutant` is ZERO, and `git log -S \"selfmutant_one()\"` names 2134a98. This row's own witness cites \"the five selfmutant_one controls\", so it had been attributed to a round that never touched them. Corrected to 2134a98 (#436: introduced selfmutant.py and selfmutant_one), abd7bad (#436 pass 2: the four measured defeats) and 7e3b120 (the SELFCHECK sentinel and its manifest; its own subject says \"closes gate3scope\"). 7172859 is the pass-2 receipt, one .md file, so it is deliberately NOT listed -- a row is authored by what shipped, not by what was written about it.",
tools/pipeline\ledger.json:13098:     "note": "MEASURED and now INSTRUMENTED. The ledger is the single source of truth and the dashboard renders from it, so a row whose work shipped but whose `commits` is empty makes the board under-report finished work and over-report the open queue. Of the 40 most recent commits, 33 were named by no row -- most legitimately (receipts, records fixes). The sharp subset was not: 6014b7e's subject reads 'selfmutant6 CLOSED' while that row read state=held, commits=(empty); and gate3scope's `commits` named 2a5bc48, which is the R9/#435 m8820x DEVICE round -- `git show 2a5bc48 -- regress/gate_offline.sh | grep -c selfmutant` is ZERO. Both corrected in this round. tools/pipeline/check_commit_attribution.py ships with the finding; it was negative-controlled in both directions and TWO defects in it were found that way: the closure-vs-state test sat below an early-out so a row could name the commit and still contradict it undetected, and records-only commits (.md/.txt) were being scored as authorship, which produced three false positives on m8820x's research commits. It reports its own blindness every run: 13 row ids are shorter than 4 characters and are not matched at all.",
tools/pipeline\ledger.json:16722:   "witness": "probe:regress/sh_pclock_probe.py (rung 3, 16 rows, SHIPPED). MEASURED 6/16 on the true pre-fix build and 16/16 post-fix, SAME SCRIPT BOTH SIDES. The pre-fix build was made from `git show HEAD:src/devices/dev_sh4.c` into a private clone and grep-confirmed to still contain `INTERNAL ERROR: pclock`, NOT from build/ -- a seat measured build/gxemul's mtime moving underneath it mid-review, so a run it believed was pre-fix was post-fix and nothing in the output distinguished them. Controls: LIVENESS (guest store then guest load of an asymmetric 0x12345678) and DEVICE SIGNATURE (IPRD reset value 0xda74, which RAM cannot produce and an unmapped address answers with `non-existant paddr`), both green on both sides and both in their OWN sessions so neither touches TCR.",
tools/pipeline\ledger.json:16745:     "note": "*** THE ONLY SEAT THAT CAUGHT THE ROUND'S FALSE PANEL CLAIM, AND IT WAS THE SEAT BEING MISCOUNTED. *** It read the shipped comment's 'a panel of nine seats reached this 8-1', observed it was written while this seat was still running, and noted it does not vote B -- an L4 wrong record about the panel itself, inside a round about a guard nobody checked. It ALSO measured that build/gxemul's mtime moved underneath it mid-review, so its own 'pre-fix' run was post-fix and indistinguishable in the output; the round's witness was re-derived from `git show HEAD:` because of that. Further measured findings, all rebuilt in a private clone with build/ and est/ untouched: -E baresh reproduces (third machine); -e jornada690 reproduces; all five machines carry the device (IPRD=0xda74 on each); the accept coverage was ASYMMETRIC (dreamcast had frequency but no readback -- row added); the guard-only growth mutant reddens all four accept rows while every kill row stays green; and Option D works mechanically (-C SH7708R needs no rebuild, store hits non-existant paddr, banner byte-identical) but must be REJECTED because arch-3 removes the entire SH-4 register block, SH-3 interrupt handlers are /* TODO */ stubs at cpu_sh.c:527-533, and cpu_sh.c:224 uses a leftover UTLB loop counter as templ.line, inert only because that handler is a stub. Its verdict was OPTION C (B plus a synthetic clock for baresh/testsh); the B half shipped, the second half is filed as sh4testclock on the round-scope rule."
tools/pipeline\ledger.json:17999:   "witness": "probe:regress/gdrom_data_probe.py (rung 3, 23 rows, SHIPPED). MEASURED 5/23 pre-fix and 23/23 post-fix, SAME SCRIPT, with the pre-fix build rebuilt from `git show HEAD:` into a private clone and grep-confirmed to still contain the old fatal text -- NOT from build/, which a seat had measured moving underneath it mid-review the previous round. All 17 pre-fix FAILs read `died`; none failed by a wrong value. Controls: LIVENESS (guest store then guest load), SH4 DEVICE SIGNATURE (IPRD reset 0xda74), and a GDROM-SPECIFIC mapping proof (a served GDROM_BUSY read rather than a paddr fault) -- all three green on both sides and each in its OWN session, so no control touches GDROM_DATA.",
tools/pipeline\ledger.json:19484:     "note": "VERDICT: **SHIP THE FIX** -- the diff is correct and the detector is above the floor.  But the\ncapped ask-again BUILT AND MEASURED **THREE mutants that escape all 20 rows**, and two of my\nsettled premises were false.  Artifacts:\n`regress/artifacts/gdromexitsix/p2askagain_20260829/`, every log sentinel-verified.\n\n*** THREE 20/20 ESCAPES -- MEASURED, NOT PREDICTED.  Each mutant's 20-row block is\nBYTE-IDENTICAL to base, so these are TOTAL escapes rather than ties. ***\n\n    E1_aliasover   (1u << GDROM_W_FMT_B) -> (1u << GDROM_W_OVERREAD_B)        20/20\n    E2_assignA     `d->warned |= GDROM_W_FMT;` -> `d->warned = GDROM_W_FMT;`  20/20\n    E3_widenA      `& GDROM_W_FMT` -> `& (GDROM_W_FMT | GDROM_W_CMD)`         20/20\n\nThe clone rebuilt the baseline to binary md5 `8d473baa`, **byte-identical to the r2measure\ntable's own baseline**, with predictions written before any mutant score existed.\n\n* **E1** is the escape THREE seats predicted today (fable5, codex, grok) and nobody measured.\n  This is the measurement.  `crossbit` stages only `GDROM_W_TOCLEN` -- 1 of 7 pre-existing\n  bits -- and no session sets the other six.\n* **E2 IS ONE CHARACTER AND IS THE WORST OF THE THREE.**  Site A still announces once, so\n  every Site-A row stays green -- but the ASSIGNMENT clears the other eight latches, so a\n  guest alternating a short TOC with a bad format re-announces #489's diagnostic **without\n  bound**, and `fatal()` cannot be silenced by `-q`.  **No row observes any latch ACROSS a\n  decline at the other site** -- every latch row checks its OWN bit.  That is the structural\n  gap, and `wdcsectorreset` is its recorded twin.\n* **E3 is invisible ONLY because of row order.**  Both two-site rows run Site A before Site B,\n  so widening Site A's test is unobservable; the same edit at Site B WOULD be caught (`1,0`).\n  **And pass 1's `S3 both` ran the OPPOSITE order -- the shipped rows inverted it and nothing\n  recorded that.**\n\n**A FOURTH, `E4`, WAS PREDICTED TO ESCAPE AND WAS REFUTED -- stated plainly rather than\nquietly dropped.**  Deleting `case 0x70:` escapes all 20 rows, but `gdrompio`'s `staged quiet`\ncatches it through a bare `unimplemented` regex.  *A cross-probe catch nobody designed.*\n\nSEVERITY: **coverage debt, NOT a floor breach**, on #464's precedent.  All four floor clauses\nmeasure clean.  **E1 is now disclosed in the contract; E2 and E3 were disclosed NOWHERE** --\nclause (e) had them as neither-claimed-nor-excluded.  All three are now written into the\ncontract with their measurements.\n\n*** A ONE-DECLARATION FIX FOR THE E1 CLASS, CONTROLLED IN FIVE STATES. ***  A compile-time\ndisjointness assert in this file's OWN `gdrom_w_fits_its_carriers` idiom: correct COMPILES;\nE1's alias REFUSED; `Ma_toclen`'s shape REFUSED; `M3shared`'s shape REFUSED; and **E1 WITHOUT\nthe assert COMPILES** -- the negative control.  **The addition-licence objection that declined\ntwelve alias mutants does not apply to a COMPILE-TIME bound**: it costs no rows and fails\nCLOSED when a tenth bit arrives.  The three grounds on which a disjointness assert was\nrejected for `dev_sh4.c` were each checked and each fails here.  FILED for the owner.\n\n*** MY `acceptA` CONCLUSION WAS RIGHT ON A HALF-ESTABLISHED WARRANT. ***  `acceptA`'s kill set\nis `{M_growA}`; `accept sil`'s is `{M_growA, Md_hoistannounce}` -- **a strict SUBSET.**  So\n`accept sil` is proven non-redundant and **`acceptA` is not**.  KEEP BOTH is right, but on\nCONSTRUCTION (they read different channels), not on 'both independently killed'.  `RESULTS.md`\nstates it correctly; my BRIEF over-generalised -- *the same shape it apologises for one\nsentence earlier.*\n\nPREMISE AUDIT -- TWO FALSE, and one of them is a number I invented:\n  * **the `(once)` census**: brief said 9; deriver `git show <rev>:src/devices/*.c | grep -o\n    '(once)' | wc -l` gives **HEAD 14, working tree 16**.  Conclusion unchanged and\n    STRENGTHENED -- the idiom is commoner than I claimed.\n  * **the reference-implementation premise is INVERTED** (four seats now).  `dev_wdc.c` is the\n    reference; the cited comment is THIS file's own and says *'FOR THE MECHANISM ONLY --\n    dev_wdc.c SHARES one flag between its two sites ... must not be attributed to that\n    precedent.'*  **If believed, my premise points at the shared-bit form this round MEASURED\n    to be wrong.**\n\nA FIFTH OPTION-SET MEMBER, found by grepping the fork: **DECLINE + SIGNAL THE ERROR TO THE\nGUEST** -- `dev_wdc.c`'s sibling ATA/ATAPI `default:` ships `d->error |= WDCE_ABRT` OUTSIDE\nthe latch, and #464 already worked out that placement.  Correctly NOT chosen here (the GD-ROM\nmodels no error channel) but it deserves a named sentence rather than silence.\n\n*** AND THE DROPPED PRE-TABLE CLAUSE IS EXACTLY WHAT E1 EXPLOITS. ***  The PRE-TABLE block's\nprescribed row 8 never shipped (`MOUNT = cmd(0x70)` is its unused fossil), and pass 1's\n`crossbit` spec had a SECOND clause -- *'extend it to touch OVERREAD and NODATA first'* --\nwhich was dropped.  **That dropped clause is the E1 escape.**  Third and fourth instances of\nthe class that same document's header warns about."
tools/pipeline\ledger.json:21084:   "state_note": "*** ADJUDICATED 2026-08-26: NO CHECK. The proposed WARN was REJECTED ON MEASUREMENT, and THREE STATEMENTS I put in this row yesterday are WRONG RECORDS, corrected here. ***\n\n**(a) 'So today's `:733` passed GREEN' is FALSE, and it was the load-bearing claim of my diagnosis.** Section D's regex is `\b[a-z0-9_]+\\.(c|h)\b:[0-9]+` -- it requires a FULL `file.c:` prefix. The stale citation is BARE: `\":720 is :733, ...\"`. VERIFIED: D's extraction from `5b5089d` yields `dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c:307/338/515/611/722/889/922` and no 733. **It did not pass green -- it was never looked at.** That is a materially different defect from the one I recorded.\n\n**(b) 'never by a gate' contradicts this row's own instance 5**, which the row says section X caught -- and the CHANGELOG headlines it *'The last thing a gate caught was a citation that decayed by MULTIPLYING.'*\n\n**(c) 'MEASURED: fires on the real instance' is true at COMMIT granularity only, not CITATION granularity** -- the rule fires on that commit, but not on the citation that went stale.\n\n*** THE PROPOSED WARN FAILS ITS OWN NEGATIVE CONTROLS IN BOTH DIRECTIONS. *** Measured over 429 commits since 2026-07-20: it fires on **57 commits / 263 citations (~11 per week)** and its **recall on the eight recorded instances is ZERO**, because every recorded instance is a BARE `:NNN` that D's extraction cannot see. On the motivating round itself it emits 15 warns, none of them the stale one. **A checker policing the visible minority of a class while blind to its dominant form IS the check-that-cannot-fail shape -- the exact defect I had attributed to section D.**\n\n*** AND THE CLASS DOES NOT CLEAR THE `countderiver` BAR. *** The adjudicator hunted with denominators -- 135 'stale' mentions in the CHANGELOG, 19 citation-flavoured, every region read -- and **no stale line citation has ever consumed a decision.** The records say so themselves: *'Nothing the citations said was wrong -- only the numbers had moved'* (#408); *'The rows were right, the table number sent a reader to the wrong page'* (#457).\n\n**AND THE REASON IS STRUCTURAL, which is why this class is WEAKER than the counts one that was already declined: NO GATE, PROBE OR SCRIPT IN THIS TREE READS A LINE CITATION FUNCTIONALLY** -- gates key on grep anchors and content. A citation is consumed only by a READER, and a reader using one opens the file, so a drifted number SELF-REFUTES AT THE POINT OF USE. A carried count does not. That is why all eight were caught by a seat or a re-read.\n\n*** THE REMEDY HAS BEEN ADOPTED FOUR SEPARATE TIMES, EACH ROUND-LOCAL, NEVER CARRIED -- the `countderiver` 'already shipping, uncodified' shape exactly. *** The #340-era round (*'replaced by symbol-anchored citations instead of a third set of numbers'*), #408 (*'every citation now names the construct'*), the fourth-instance note (*'the arms are named by what they ARE'*), and my own grep-anchor conversion today. **Section D1's own header already says 'prefer naming the CONSTRUCT over the line'.** The class keeps re-biting because each adoption stayed inside one round's records.\n\n**THE RULE, NOW STANDING RATHER THAN ROUND-LOCAL:** line numbers only ACROSS files, with the qualifier ON the citation; SAME-FILE references positional or by CONSTRUCT NAME; durable cross-file references as VERIFIED-UNIQUE grep anchors. **It removes the measured population -- 7 of 8 instances were same-file or bare forms the rule eliminates outright** -- and it is structure in the sense that matters: it lives where citations are WRITTEN and fails at the point of USE, because an anchor that matches nothing is loud.\n\n**PRE-SPECIFIED ESCALATION, so nobody re-argues it: the first MEASURED decision consumed by a stale line citation -- a fix mis-aimed by one, a gate passing falsely, a seat verifying green against wrong content -- converts the form rule into an anchor-verification check bounded to ADDED citations, forward-only from a cutoff. It audits nothing retroactively and builds nothing until that trigger fires.**\n\n*** TWO MORE INSTANCES 2026-08-26, BRINGING THE RECORDED COUNT TO EIGHT -- AND THE SECOND IS THE WORST SHAPE THIS CLASS HAS PRODUCED. ***\n\n**(7) A CORRECTION NOTE WENT STALE INSIDE THE COMMIT THAT WROTE IT.** #465's probe gained a note reading *\":720 is :733, :475-480 is :486-494\"*. Re-derived: `d->cond = idata;` is at **:736** -- :733 was true at `4d05cf0` and was invalidated by `5b5089d`, **the very commit carrying the note** -- and the tail is :491-496, which was NEVER right, since even at `4d05cf0` it began at :488. Fourth generation of drift on one file: pre-fix numbers, invalidated; a note written against the previous commit, invalidated again.\n\n**(8) AND THE FIX FOR (7) WAS DEFEATED BY ITS OWN EXPLANATION.** The repair stripped the `panel_` prefix from a backlog list so the names could not false-cite -- then wrote the full name in the sentence EXPLAINING the circularity, so it false-cited anyway. Caught by the count moving 78 -> 77 only after a second pass.\n\n*** THE REMEDY IS NOW MEASURED, NOT PROPOSED: RE-KEY TO GREP ANCHORS. *** Six drifted carrier citations were converted to greppable tokens earlier the same day (`grep -n 'LOGDIR/mutation'`, `'rm -rf \"$dir\"'`, the \"honour $LOGDIR\" comment, gate_hygiene's own \"READS THE RAW PTY LOGS ON PURPOSE\" header) and **each was verified to resolve UNIQUELY** -- because a key that matches nothing is worse than the stale number it replaced, which I then proved by shipping an anchor that matched ZERO and having to fix it.\n\n*** AND THE DIAGNOSIS THE ROW WAS MISSING: THE MECHANISM ALREADY EXISTS AND IS BLIND BY CONSTRUCTION. *** `precommit_check.sh` section D extracts every added `file.c:NNN` and checks the file has at least that many lines. **Its own success message says so: \"cited $c exists (READ THE LINE -- existence is not correctness)\".** So `:733` passed GREEN today -- the file has more than 733 lines -- while the content had moved to `:736`. That is the third check-that-cannot-fail-for-its-class found in one day.\n\n**A BOUNDED STRENGTHENING IS AVAILABLE, and it is verified against the real instance:** for each added `file.ext:NNN`, if THAT FILE IS ALSO MODIFIED BY THE SAME DIFF, warn -- the commit's own hunks move the lines, so the citation is at risk BY CONSTRUCTION. **MEASURED: `git show --name-only 5b5089d` lists `src/devices/dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c`, so the rule fires on today's case.** It must be a WARN and not a FAIL: correctness is undecidable, and a self-citation into an edited file is sometimes right. Cost is a few lines beside the existing D loop.\n\nNOT BUILT -- put to the adjudicator, because it changes a checker every commit runs and owes negative controls in both directions (a same-file citation into an edited file must WARN; one into an untouched file must stay silent).\n\nThe carrier already states the rule (*'a line number in a comment is invalidated by your own edits to that file; re-grep every same-file citation AFTER the last edit'*). **#463 broke it SIX TIMES while knowing it**, which is the argument for mechanising rather than restating: (1) `:200,:203` for the debug() calls, stale when the #463 comment was first inserted; (2) the SAME cite stale AGAIN after the comment was trimmed three lines shorter -- **a correction that went stale a second time**; (3) `:230` for `default:` in the round block, found by a records-audit seat; (4) `:144-152` for the mask_13 handler in the probe docstring; (5) four BARE artifact names with seven candidates each -- citation decay by NAMESPACE rather than by movement, caught by section X; (6) `:227` for `default:` going stale inside the very edit that corrected a different overstatement. *** THE SHAPE IS ALWAYS THE SAME AND ALWAYS INVISIBLE: the text does not change and stops being true. *** Section D checks that ADDED citations EXIST; it cannot see a citation that still resolves to a line of the wrong content, and existence is not correctness -- its own output says so. PROPOSED CHECK, cheap and mechanical: for each `:NNN` in a source comment, require an adjacent ANCHOR TOKEN (a short quoted fragment of the cited line) and verify the token is actually ON line NNN. That converts every citation into a self-verifying pair and catches movement, trimming and namespace decay alike. Cost: one token per citation, and the round that wrote six stale ones would have caught all six at commit time.  *** A SEVENTH INSTANCE, AND IT PRODUCED A BETTER FIX THAN THE CHECKER THIS ROW PROPOSED. *** Adding seven lines about transaction closure moved `default:` again, and `:229` came to point at `d->mask_9[r] = idata;`. Caught only because the citation was re-derived immediately after the edit -- the manual discipline works exactly as long as someone remembers it, which is the definition of a check that eventually does not happen. **THE REAL FIX IS CHEAPER THAN AN ANCHOR-TOKEN CHECKER: a SAME-FILE citation does not need a line number.** `default:` is in the same function, below the comment; `the default: arm below` and `the struct above` are unambiguous, need no maintenance, and CANNOT DECAY -- there is nothing there to go stale. Applied: all same-file `:NNN` citations in this comment are now positional, and the only numbers left are CROSS-FILE -- and the ENUMERATION here named four when there are SIX (`core/debugmsg.c:375`, `core/debugmsg.c:384-390`, `cpu_sh_instr.c:790-798`, `dreamcast_sysasicvar.h:72`, `dreamcast_sysasicvar.h:74`, `configure:312-314`); the CLAIM that all remaining are cross-file is true and zero bare `:NNN` remain, but the list was not exhaustive -- which edits to THIS file cannot move. **THE RULE THAT FALLS OUT: cite a line number only ACROSS files; within a file, cite by POSITION or by the name of the thing.** That removes the entire class from same-file comments at zero cost, and leaves the checker worth building for the cross-file remainder, which is a much smaller surface -- seven instances this round were ALL same-file.  *** AN EIGHTH INSTANCE, AND THIS ONE REFUTES A JUDGEMENT RATHER THAN AN EDIT. *** After converting every same-file citation to a positional reference, two bare cross-file numbers remained, each following a QUALIFIED cite to the same file. I inspected them and judged the idiom clear enough -- `core/debugmsg.c:384-390, against debug()'s at :375`. **A pass-2 seat then read `:375` as a SAME-FILE citation and reported claim 3 as failing**, with a second seat independently flagging one `:NNN` it 'cannot prove cross-file'. **The judgement was refuted by demonstration, which is stronger than any argument I had for it**: the question was never whether the idiom CAN be read correctly but whether it IS, and two of six readers did not. Fixed by repeating the filename rather than relying on proximity. THE RULE SHARPENS: **a citation's qualifier must be ON the citation, not merely NEAR it.** Proximity is not qualification -- it depends on the reader carrying context across a line break, which is exactly what a reader skimming a 60-line comment does not do."
tools/pipeline\ledger.json:21959:     "ev": "read-only research against GXEMUL-SEC/src at HEAD; every quoted region diffed against `git show HEAD:` because the working tree was being edited concurrently by the #494 round",
tools/pipeline\ledger.json:23511:   "note": "*** EVERY LINE NUMBER `diag_div_sweep.py` PRINTS IS TOO SMALL, AND `gate_hygiene.sh`'s OWN COMMENT CITES TWO OF THEM. *** MEASURED 2026-08-29: the sweep reports `dev_ns16550.c:79` for `115200 / d->divisor`, whose call really begins at **:180**; and `diskimage.c:543` for `bytes_left % ct->sector_size`, whose call really begins at **:976**.  *** THIS ROW FIRST SAID 181 AND 983.  THOSE ARE THE DIVISION LINES, NOT THE CALL-START LINES, and the sweep correctly reports the latter -- a call may span lines and the start is the sensible anchor.  Corrected after the scratch proof reported 180/976 and I checked WHICH SIDE was wrong instead of assuming the tool was. ***  It also reports `dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c:211 fatal`, where line 211 is PROSE inside a comment block. MECHANISM, read from the source rather than guessed: `strip_lits()` does NOT preserve offsets -- a string literal of any length becomes a single space, and a `/* ... */` block becomes a single space WITH ALL ITS NEWLINES DESTROYED.  The scan then runs over the stripped text while the line number is computed as `raw[:m.start()].count(chr(10))` -- an offset into the STRIPPED string indexed against the RAW one.  Since stripping only ever shortens, every reported line is too small, and the error grows with the size of the comment blocks above the site.  `dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c` carries 40-line comment blocks, which is why its site lands 400+ lines early, inside prose. *** THE COUNTS ARE UNAFFECTED AND THE RATCHET IS SOUND. ***  Match counting does not depend on line numbering, so `DIAG_DIV_TOTAL=21` and `DIAG_DIV_NONCONST=2` are correct, and both non-constant sites are correctly IDENTIFIED by file and expression.  Only their CITATIONS are false.  This is a records defect, not a coverage one. **WHY IT MATTERS ANYWAY:** the wrong numbers did not stay in the tool. They were transcribed into `gate_hygiene.sh`'s rationale comment, where a future round reads them as the authoritative location of the two sites the ratchet exists to protect -- and this project's citation-form rule exists precisely because a drifted number is consumed by a reader who then opens the wrong place. FOUND BY CHASING A REFUTED SEAT CLAIM: agy attributed the 24->21 drop to '#490 consolidating 4 fatal() calls into 1 at dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c:211'.  Checking it showed #490 removed ZERO fatal lines containing a division and that :211 is a comment -- so the claim was false, but the line number it rested on was the tool's, not the seat's.  *A seat's wrong answer led straight to a real defect in the tool that supplied its premise.* REMEDY: make `strip_lits` length-preserving -- replace each removed character with a space and each removed newline with a newline -- so stripped offsets index the raw text correctly.  That is the standard fix and it makes the two texts positionally identical.  Then re-derive the two citations in gate_hygiene.sh's comment.  Owes a control: the known sites must report 181 and 983.  *** REMEDY PROVEN IN SCRATCH, 2026-08-29, before any tree was touched. ***  A length-preserving `strip_lits` (one space per consumed character, newlines preserved) moves the two citations from 79 -> 180 and 543 -> 976, the true call-start lines, while DIAG_DIV_TOTAL stays 21 and DIAG_DIV_NONCONST stays 2 -- so it is a pure CITATION repair with no behavioural change, which is the property that makes it safe.  The proof harness itself failed twice first and both failures were apparatus: the scratch copy resolves ROOT from its own location and scanned an empty directory (reporting 0 sites -- the phantom-absence shape), and a `re.sub` replacement ate the backslashes in the override path.  Both were caught because the control demanded specific numbers rather than merely 'different'.  *** AND THE SURROUNDING STORY WAS WRONG TOO, MEASURED BY THE fable5 SEAT. ***  I recorded the class ratchet's 24 -> 21 as 'three sites legitimately REMOVED'.  It is a CONSOLIDATION: the pre-#462 tree carried FOUR one-division `fatal(\"  %08x\", (int) d->dma_reg[N / sizeof(uint32_t)])` calls, and HEAD carries the SAME FOUR DIVISIONS as arguments of ONE fatal().  **No instance of the class was removed at all** -- `DIAG_DIV_TOTAL` counts CALLS carrying at least one division, so merging four calls into one moves the total while the division census is unchanged.  A round acting on my version would have gone looking for three removals that never happened.  ATTRIBUTION SETTLED BY MEASUREMENT, NOT BY VOTE: the two Agent seats DISAGREED -- opus5 said `fb0cc78` (#490), fable5 said `7c9dd9e` (#462).  `git show <sha> -- src/devices/dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c | grep -c dma_reg` gives **9 for 7c9dd9e and 0 for fb0cc78**, so fable5 is right.  One command, no tally.",
tools/pipeline\ledger.json:23531:     "note": "*** CAUGHT THAT I DROPPED A RED, AND THE DROPPED ONE DEFEATS THE ROUND'S OWN MOTIVATION. ***  It is SIX reds, not five: the brief omits `asic_badaddr rows run got=14 want=13`, **which my own note file had already dispositioned as its item 5**.  A round fixing 'the five' leaves gate_sh_rounding red and the battery REGRESS_FAIL, so 'get the battery green' fails entirely on the dropped row.  It named this as my own third-listed error class recurring inside the brief that warns about it -- and noted the note file itself gives three different counts ('four ratchet reds' / 'six FAIL rows' / 'five of the seven') in one document, so the round owes ONE derived count with its deriver.  *** MEASURED CLAIM 4 FALSE: the 24 -> 21 drop is a CONSOLIDATION, not removals. ***  Four one-division fatal() calls became ONE fatal() carrying the same four divisions; `DIAG_DIV_TOTAL` counts CALLS.  Attribution `7c9dd9e` (#462) -- and opus5 said `fb0cc78` (#490); settled by `git show ... | grep -c dma_reg` giving 9 vs 0, so fable5 is right.  It found the sweep's line-number defect on the way and correctly filed rather than fixed it, per the stopping rule.  A2 UPHELD THE OWNER'S PRIORITISATION with a concrete warrant: this very run proves the cost, because a REAL regression (#491's sign-compare pair) sat in the same FAIL list as six bookkeeping reds -- under red-is-normal the next real regression is invisible.  A3 REFUTED MY ESTIMATE'S PROXY AS VINTAGE-BIASED: 'witness names an artifact kind' marks 45/86, 'row has commits' marks 64/86 -- 52% vs 74% -- and the 21-row disagreement is real emulator fixes predating 2026-08-19, when the witness field became mandatory.  **The proxy bins a third of the sample by VINTAGE, not substance.**  Report a RANGE (~60-120) with a per-row deriver (did the row's commits touch `src/`?), and note documented-only rows are not free -- each owes a rung-3 attempt, a panel and records.  A4: a cheap commit-time check DOES exist for 5 of the 6, because the class splits -- five are STATIC source greps needing no boot, so a `--static` gate mode invoked from precommit would have caught them at their offending commits (#462, #467/8, #484/5, #489, #490).  The two runtime row-count pins genuinely need probe execution and stay weekly.  It insists this ships as ITS OWN controlled harness round, not a rider."
tools/pipeline\ledger.json:23611:     "note": "Four concrete Section-B findings, ALL VERIFIED AND ALL MINE: the comment's census table went STALE INSIDE ITS OWN DIFF (138/59/59 -> 139/60/60, because this round added wdc_latch_census.py to the population it froze -- a frozen count in a comment ABOUT counting); `*/artifacts/*` excludes ANY component named artifacts, not uniquely $HERE's; the pty alternation is not a general guest-driving census (hpcabort_witness.py, hpcmips_ctor_probe.py, sgi_eaddr_probe.py drive via subprocess.run or GDB --args); and the staged diff failed `git diff --cached --check` on trailing whitespace."
tools/pipeline\ledger.json:23739:     "note": "*** RULED THE ROUND ALREADY RAN, AND RULED AGAINST ITS OWN RECOMMENDATION IN THE SAME ANSWER. ***  Reconstructed the chronology from the repo: the 10:36 matrix, then `#479` at 12:18 shipping F-narrow, `#480`'s L2 call-site fix, `#481` closing the ALT-CTRL gap, `#482` splitting the joint row into four one-tenant rows.  A3: **the standing RED row it had itself recommended is DEAD** -- '`a permanently red row is the dual of this project's check-that-cannot-fail -- a check that cannot PASS`', and it violates the blanks rule since a deliberate red and a regression red render identically.  `#481` closed the gap instead, which is better.  It gave the disclosure ladder in order: close the gap; else an accept/tripwire row pinning today's behaviour GREEN; else prose plus a FILED item.  A2: **corrected MY premise** -- `#492` did NOT touch `dev_wdc.c` (`git show d9efd33 -- src/devices/dev_wdc.c` is empty); it touched a checker.  It then checked the interaction precisely and found the two detectors cover DISJOINT mutant classes, so both are needed.  A5: the witness string is defective THREE ways -- a stale count, `probe:` where `detector:` was adopted for exactly this shape (the mis-scoring `check_witness.py`'s own header calls laundering), and the rung-3 accept probe owed its own clause.  It required PER-ROW CARRIER NAMING or three closures are mutually indistinguishable, which is the overtaken-row rule's condition 2 unmet.  It named the structural remedy that was adopted: a BRIEF_TEMPLATE settled-premise line, because the carrier's own finding is that the prose which fires is the prose in the artifact present at the moment of decision."
tools/pipeline\ledger.json:24325:   "note": "*** THIS EARNED ITS SECOND INSTANCE IN ONE DAY, IN THE SAME ARTIFACTS DIRECTORY, WHICH IS THIS PROJECT'S OWN TRIGGER FOR PREFERRING STRUCTURE OVER MEMORY. ***  **Instance 1, `runswap.sh`:** the base leg was `git show HEAD~1:`.  True when written.  An unrelated commit (a citation repair) then landed on top, pushing `#495` down to `HEAD~1` itself, so BOTH legs would have been built from code CONTAINING the fix.  Pinned to `67120fd^`.  **Instance 2, `killswap.sh`:** LEG 0's pre-extension probe was `git show HEAD:`.  True while the round was in flight -- HEAD held the 11-row probe and the working tree held 15.  **The round then SHIPPED**, HEAD became the 15-row probe, and the script refused a run in which there was no extension left to measure.  Pinned to `e61ccd8^`.  *** THE SHAPE IS SPECIFIC AND WORTH NAMING: a round's artifacts are written while the round is UNCOMMITTED, so every relative ref in them is written from a vantage point the round itself destroys by shipping. ***  The artifact is durable; the reference frame is not.  **BOTH WERE CAUGHT BY THE SCRIPTS' OWN GUARDS, NOT BY A READER**, and that is the reassuring half: each refused to measure rather than measuring the wrong thing.  A reproduction months from now would fail loudly instead of producing a confident wrong table.  PROPOSED STRUCTURAL FIX, filed rather than built: a precommit check warning when a file under `regress/artifacts/` contains `HEAD~`, `HEAD^` or `git show HEAD:`.  Bounded, existence-only, forward-only from a cutoff.  **NOT built in the round that found it** -- a tooling change owing two negative controls (it must fire on a relative ref and must NOT fire on a pinned SHA), which ships as its own harness round.  *The population is probably larger: only two files were exercised today.  Other rounds' artifacts have never been re-run, so a stale ref there would not yet have announced itself.  The count is unknown and is deliberately not guessed.*"
tools/pipeline\ledger.json:25625:   "note": "*** THIS ROW WAS FILED CLAIMING I BUILT A TOOL.  I DID NOT.  I OVERWROTE ONE THAT WAS ALREADY THERE, ALREADY CORRECT, AND ALREADY IN THE SAME DIRECTORY AS THE LEDGER I HAD BEEN EDITING ALL EVENING.  Caught by a `git diff --cached --stat` line reading `416 insertions(+), 126 deletions(-)` on a file I believed I had just created. ***  **THE `Write` TOOL SAID 'has been updated successfully', NOT 'created', AND I DID NOT READ IT.**  The standing rule is *before deleting or overwriting, look at the target*, and I did not look.  Restored from HEAD; `git status` on the path is clean, so nothing was lost.  *** WHAT THE EXISTING TOOL ALREADY DID, ALL OF WHICH I RE-DERIVED FROM SCRATCH: ***  it strips C comments with a state machine that respects string literals; it reports the denominator beside the numerator so a pattern selecting nothing is visible; it tags each site by enclosing construct (`DEVICE_ACCESS` / `DEVINIT` / `DEVICE_TICK` / `static`) so guest-reachable sites are separable from construction-time ones; and **its docstring already carries the exact lesson I thought I had found** -- *a naive grep gets steadily MORE wrong as this fork gets more honest, because every de-fatalisation leaves a comment QUOTING the exit(1) it removed.*  It even records the same false positive shape, from a different file.  **IT REPORTS `dev_sgi_re.c = 21`, WHICH IS THE ANSWER I SPENT AN HOUR REACHING BY HAND** -- after asserting 25 into two seat briefs and telling a seat its 21 was probably wrong.  One command would have settled it at the start.  *** THE REAL LESSON, and it is a generalisation of one this project already has: BEFORE WRITING A TOOL, CHECK WHETHER IT IS THERE. ***  The carrier already says a hand-maintained inventory of FILES is a wrong record waiting for someone to add a file, and mandates `ls _scratchpad/*.pdf` over trusting the list.  **The same applies to `tools/pipeline/` -- `ls` it before building anything.**  This is the third instance today of the same shape: an absent-source note that was wrong (the footbridge datasheet, twelve days), a census asserted rather than derived, and now a tool re-implemented because nobody looked.  **PER-FUNCTION ATTRIBUTION WAS THE ONE THING MY VERSION ADDED, AND IT IS DELIBERATELY NOT BEING ADDED.**  The existing tool counts per FILE and tags by construct, and its own design note says why: *UNIT is SITES, not defects* and *DEVICE_ACCESS means WHERE TO LOOK; reachability stays a per-site question*.  A per-function count invites exactly the false precision it warns against, and the addition licence requires a MEASURED DELTA or a TRANSFERRED WARRANT -- 'it would have caught my brief typo' is neither.  **My 13-in-sgi_re figure was a detail in a brief, not a decision input; the file-level 21 was the number that mattered and the tool had it.**  Closed as a RECORDS row: no code shipped, and the correct code was already in the tree.  *** AND THE TOOL DID HAVE THE BOUNDARY DEFECT AFTER ALL.  I SAID IT DID NOT, AN HOUR AGO, IN THIS ROW.  Measured 2026-08-30. ***  I closed this row concluding the existing tool *counts per FILE and tags by construct, so it never had defect 2* -- and its per-file totals ARE right, which is what I checked.  **Its CONSTRUCT split is not.**  `MARK` is `^(DEVICE_ACCESS|DEVINIT|DEVICE_TICK|static\\s)`, and a **plain NON-STATIC function definition is not a boundary** -- `void draw_primitive(...)`, `void do_mte_transfer(...)` -- so every terminating call inside one was attributed to the preceding `DEVICE_ACCESS`.  **WHY IT MATTERS RATHER THAN BEING TIDY: that bucket is a REACHABILITY CLAIM used to rank defects** -- the tool literally tags it *on a guest access path BY CONSTRUCTION*.  Over-counting it over-states how many host kills a guest can reach, and at least one ledger row (`sgirekills`) took its title from the wrong figure.  **MEASURED DELTA, tree-wide over `src/devices` (118 files, 172 live calls): DEVICE_ACCESS 97 -> 83**, a 14-call / 14.4% over-count.  `static` also fell 51 -> 13, which is the same defect from the other side: a plain function FOLLOWING a static one was inheriting `static`.  **CONTROLLED against a hand-derived fixture** -- `dev_sgi_re.c`, whose true split was derived by hand and independently reproduced by two seats: `DEVICE_ACCESS` **15** (sgi_re 13 + sgi_de 1 + sgi_mte 1) and plain functions **6** (draw_primitive 1 + do_mte_transfer 5).  Before the fix the tool put all 21 in `DEVICE_ACCESS`; after it, 15/6 exactly.  **AND THE FIRST DRAFT OF THE FIX HAD A DEFECT OF ITS OWN, caught before shipping:** the new pattern also matched `DEVICE_ACCESS(foo)` lines, yielding two marks at one offset -- 4 duplicates in that file alone.  Harmless *today*, because at equal offset the `DEVICE_ACCESS` label sorts last and wins, **but that is a silent dependence on sort order inside a tool whose output ranks defects.**  Excluded the four MARK forms from the new pattern; duplicates now 0 across five spot-checked files.  *The honest shape of today: I diagnosed this defect correctly by hand, then wrongly concluded the shipped tool did not have it, because I checked the number people quote (per-file) rather than the number that ranks (per-construct).*"
tools/pipeline\ledger.json:25634:   "note": "*** THE WIDEST DOOR IN THIS DEVICE, AND NO LEDGER ROW COVERED IT.  Found by the adjudicating seat while ranking the OTHER four sgi_re rows; verified here by opening the file and deriving the gap arithmetic. ***  **THE SITE** is the final `else` of the window cascade in `DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_re)` -- a `fatal()` for each direction and then a bare `exit(1)`, with **NO WIDTH PREDICATE AT ALL**.  Every other kill in this handler is reached only by choosing an unusual access WIDTH (the six `len != 8` arms) or a DIRECTION (the six read arms).  This one needs neither: any width, either direction, any unmatched address.  **THE DOOR, derived rather than asserted.**  `memory_device_register` covers `baseaddr + 0x1000` for `DEV_SGI_RE_LENGTH` = `0x1000`, and the handler restores the full offset with `relative_addr += 0x1000`, so the dispatched range is `0x1000-0x1fff`.  The six windows occupy `0x1000-0x17ff` with one internal gap:      TLB_A 0x1000-0x11ff   TLB_B 0x1200-0x13ff   TLB_C 0x1400-0x15ff      TEX   0x1600-0x16df   LINEAR_A 0x1700-0x177f  LINEAR_B 0x1780-0x17ff  leaving **`0x16e0-0x16ff` (32 bytes) plus `0x1800-0x1fff` (2048 bytes) = 2080 bytes** that fall through to the kill.  Guest KSEG1: a single `lb` at `0xb5001800` ends the host process.  *** IT SURVIVES BOTH ROUNDS THAT LOOK LIKE THEY WOULD FIX IT, WHICH IS WHY IT MUST BE FILED BEFORE THE NEXT COMPLETENESS SENTENCE SHIPS. ***  `#497` repairs the six WIDTH guards and never reaches this arm.  `sgirekills` as scoped repairs the six READ arms inside the windows and never reaches it either.  A reader of either round record could reasonably conclude the device no longer kills on a stray access.  It does.  **AND ITS FIX CANNOT BE ACCEPT**, which is what keeps it out of `sgirekills`' round rather than a scheduling preference: nothing backs an unmapped address, so there is no stored value to return.  It is DECLINE + a latched `(once)` announce -- a different fix design, so the round-scope rule's same-fix-design arm fails.  *The agy audit listed this arm as '1 unhandled offset fallback' and never asked whether it was reachable.  A census that enumerates a site without ranking it is how the widest door stays open.*  *** DESIGN SETTLED 2026-08-30 BY ADJUDICATION, BEFORE EITHER ROUND STARTS, SO NEITHER BURNS ITS PANEL ARGUING SCOPE. ***  **TWO ROUNDS, and both separate from `#497` -- all THREE round-scope conjuncts fail, worked through rather than asserted.**  SAME DEVICE fails: `sgi_de` and `sgi_re` are separate `memory_device_register` registrations at `baseaddr+0x2000` and `+0x1000`.  SAME SEMANTIC CLASS fails: `sgidewidth` is the WIDTH class with a real accept set `{4,8}` a decline must PRESERVE, while `sgireunmapped` is the BADOFFSET class where nothing legal reaches the arm at all.  **SAME DETECTOR / NO NEW ORACLE fails, and this is the one that decides it:** `sgi_de`'s registers are READABLE so its rows read back through `odata` -- a real device-signature channel -- while `sgi_re`'s else arm has NO BACKING STORE and its rows can only observe the diagnostic and the guest-gets-zero channel.  *Those are different oracles in kind, and the tempting collapse -- 'both are a refused access ending the host' -- is exactly what the no-new-oracle clause exists to refuse.*  *** THE CHANNEL FINDING, AND IT IS THE MOST IMPORTANT LINE IN THIS BLOCK. ***  `#497`'s `d_returnzero` escaped 35/35 and the reason generalises to BOTH these rounds: **the `(once)` announce prints IDENTICALLY for a correct decline and for a `return 0`**, because the announce happens before the return statement.  So the DIAGNOSTIC channel does not kill it either -- and neither does host survival, since a guest bus error leaves the host alive.  **The only discriminator is what the GUEST RECEIVED.**  Every round in this family owes a SENTINEL-SEEDED READ ROW: seed the destination register, perform the declined read, and assert it reads back 0 -- a correct decline delivers zero, `return 0` faults and the register keeps its seed.  *That is the row `#497` was missing, and the measuring seat reached the same repair independently while this adjudication was being written.*  **TWO LATCH BITS, NOT ONE, and the precedent is MEASURED rather than stylistic.**  The arm prints two distinct messages -- `unimplemented write to address` and `unimplemented read from address`.  `#490` built the one-bit form as `M3shared` and **it ESCAPED**: a session declining at both sites sees the second fall silent because the first already latched.  `#279` states the rule outright -- *one flag per DISTINCT MESSAGE and never one shared flag*.  A single bit here means the first stray READ silences the first stray WRITE's message forever, and vice versa.  **THE DE-ESCALATION ACCEPT CLAUSE IS N/A, WITH ITS REASON RECORDED -- not unrun.**  The clause enumerates predicates that used to gate death and pins each accept side.  This arm **has no predicate**: it is the fall-through when none of the six window conditions match, so there is no adjacent modelled value because **nothing legal reaches it**.  The enumeration is the empty set.  *Adding a row to test the WINDOW predicates instead would be the addition licence's 'cannot hurt' wearing detector clothes -- and it is unnecessary, because `#497`'s own accept rows already prove those addresses reach their own arms rather than this one.  That is inherited coverage, not an uncovered hole.*  **WHAT IT OWES INSTEAD IS A CONTRACT SENTENCE (clause (e)):** this fix makes every unmatched address a silent zero-decline, so a future window-boundary regression that drops a legal address here no longer announces -- and that class is pinned by `#497`'s window accept rows, not by this arm.  *** AND A CONTRACT LIMIT WORTH KNOWING BEFORE ROWS ARE WRITTEN: the WRITE arm's `return 0` mutant is NOT distinguishable by a value-only cold-debugger probe. ***  A declined write and a faulting write both leave no readback.  The READ arm is killable by the seeded-read row; the write arm needs exception-state observation, which is a heavier probe shape than `#497` used.  **State it as a limit rather than shipping a completeness claim the rows cannot support.**  *** THE 32-BYTE GAP IS NOT DEAD SPACE.  IT IS `CRIME_RE_CLIP_IDS`, A SEVENTH REAL HARDWARE WINDOW. ***  `crmfbreg.h` defines `CRIME_RE_CLIP_IDS 0x16e0` between `CRIME_RE_TEX 0x1600` and `CRIME_RE_LINEAR_A 0x1700`, and `struct sgi_re_data` carries `// todo: clip_ids registers.` exactly where they would go.  The codex seat reached the same conclusion from the CRIME spec and put it best: **\"six real windows\" should read \"six MODELED windows\"** -- CLIP_IDS is real hardware with no dispatch and no state.  **The 2080-address count is UNCHANGED** (CLIP_IDS still falls to the kill) **but the characterisation splits, and the decline message must follow it:** 32 bytes are a documented, unmodelled register block; 2048 are genuinely unmapped.  A single \"unimplemented address\" diagnostic is accurate for the 2048 and WRONG for the 32.  *The brief asked whether `CRIME_RE_TEX + 0xe0` was a bug or the gap was real; both options were wrong, and a seat answering as posed would have had to pick the wrong one of two.  Three local facts agree the bound is deliberate: the next defined constant, the struct's own todo comment, and `re_tex[112]` x 2 bytes = 0xe0 exactly.*  *** THE fable5 SEAT'S EQUIVALENCE CLAIM IS REFUTED, AND THE REFUTATION CHANGES A DISPOSITION RATHER THAN SCORING A POINT. ***  It reported that an early `return 1` after the announce is OBSERVATIONALLY EQUIVALENT because *\"`memory_rw.c`'s #95 block memsets `data` to 0 on EVERY device read before the handler runs\"*, and advised disposing of that mutant as EQUIVALENT rather than chasing it.  **Checked both halves and both are false.**  The device handler is called at `memory_rw.c` `res = mem->devices[i].f(cpu, mem, paddr, data, len, ...)` and NOTHING zeroes `data` before it -- the three `memset(data, 0, len)` sites in that file are all on NON-DEVICE paths (the MIPS Sprite area, the beyond-physical_max arm, and the `memblock == NULL` unmapped-RAM arm), each followed by a `goto do_return_ok`.  And the MIPS caller's buffer is `uint8_t data[LS_SIZE];`, an **UNINITIALISED STACK ARRAY** with no memset anywhere in that file.  **SO THE MUTANT IS NOT EQUIVALENT -- BUT THE CORRECT DISPOSITION IS NOT 'DETECTABLE' EITHER, AND THAT NUANCE IS THE POINT.**  Skipping the tail leaves the guest whatever was on the stack, which is neither 0 nor any fixed value: the observable is REAL but NONDETERMINISTIC.  A row asserting a specific sentinel would be FLAKY, not sound.  *Filing it as EQUIVALENT would have retired a live mutant on a false mechanism; filing it as ordinary coverage debt would invite a flaky row.  It is neither, and the round must say so.*  **The seat's own remedy for the sibling `return 0` case is the one that works here too: seed the destination register with a sentinel and prove execution continued past the load** -- which pins the mutant without depending on stack contents.  *Everything else in that answer verified: `dev_unreadable.c` IS `return 0;` and `machine_sgi.c` installs it TEN times, so the FAULT member is real and in use on this machine.*  *** THE PASS-1 PANEL RESOLVED THE ROUND'S ONLY DISAGREEMENT BY WARRANT, NOT BY VOTE. ***  grok said ONE latch bit, deepseek said TWO.  I grepped first and found ZERO device files in the tree splitting a latch by direction -- which looked like precedent for grok.  **fable5 showed the framing was wrong: the house latch is per-MESSAGE-SITE, and this arm has TWO message texts (the write arm prints the data value, the read arm does not).  So two bits is not a new rule, it is the existing rule counted correctly** -- and my grep found nothing because I searched for the wrong property.  Two further warrants: a declined READ fabricates data the guest acts on while a declined write is a no-op, and one shared bit order-couples the two message rows so 'read message exactly once' becomes unpinnable.  *** WITNESSED AT RUNG 3, 32/32.  The row's witness field said `none`. ***  **AND ONE ROW FAILED FOR A REASON WORTH KEEPING: `lw` from 0x16ff SURVIVED** -- the log shows `exception ADEL`, because an unaligned load is refused by the CPU before `memory_rw` is reached.  Re-cut to 0x16fc it kills.  *The row was wrong, not the code, and the seat said so rather than adjusting the claim to fit.*  **THE ESCAPES, ranked by how little they cost:**  (1) *** A WINDOW-BOUND SHRINK (`+ 0x80` -> `+ 0x40`) SCORED A TOTAL ESCAPE, 9/9, AGAINST THE FIRST DETECTOR -- because the accept row wrote to the window's BASE, where a move of the FAR bound is invisible by construction. ***  That is clause (f)'s anchor rule arriving on the accept side, and the repair is one word: **an accept row must use the window's LAST slot.**  (2) a latch-bit collision reusing `SGIRE_W_TEX_LEN` scores 13/14, caught only by a row that touches another latch site first.  (3) `return 0` escapes both \"host survives\" and \"message once\" -- and the seat measured the discriminator side by side: with `v0` pre-set to **0** the row PASSES the mutant; pre-set to **0xa5a5a5a5** it FAILS, because a DBE leaves the register untouched.  The log is decisive (`reading from device 'sgi_re' ... failed` / `exception DBE`).  **So the brief's stated pin \"the declined read returns 0\" is MEASURED VACUOUS unless the sentinel is non-zero.**  **A REFINEMENT TO THE SIX-MEMBER OPTION SET, and it is not a quibble:** the set collapses THREE DISTINCT ANNOUNCE DISCIPLINES living in this one file -- unbounded-and-always-visible (`fatal()`, in sgi_de and sgi_mte), **unbounded but VERBOSITY-GATED (`debug()`, in sgi_de_status, invisible at default verbosity)**, and latched-once.  Folding the second into \"already declines\" hides exactly the flood question the round has to answer.  *** ROW COVERAGE, STATED RATHER THAN GLOSSED: ONLY 10 OF 14 ROWS EVER FIRED, AND THREE OF THE FOUR SILENT ONES ARE AN UNDISCHARGED OBLIGATION. ***  `accept tlba`, `accept tlbb` and `accept tlbc` never fired because **no shrink mutant was built for the three TLB windows**.  By the accept-row failability rule an accept row is discharged by a MEASURED KILL, not by existing -- so these three are NOT discharged, and the round owes three more mutants (about 1.5 s per build).  **AND THEY ARE NOT THE SAME MUTANT SHAPE AS THE FOUR ALREADY BUILT.**  TLB_A and TLB_B are bounded by *the next window's constant* while the other four use *base + size*, so shrinking a TLB bound perturbs TWO arms at once -- it moves a boundary two windows share, rather than opening a gap.  A mutant written on the base+size pattern would not exercise them.  The fourth silent row, `read0 VACUOUS`, is **deliberately** never red: it is the control that DEMONSTRATES the brief's stated pin is vacuous.  Same load, `v0` pre-set to 0 instead of 0xa5a5a5a5, and it PASSES on the mutant where the real row fails.  *A row that never fires because it is a negative control and a row that never fires because nobody built its mutant must not be counted alike, which is why they are separated here.*  **DISPOSITION: this is L3, an undischarged obligation, not coverage debt** -- the three rows are the sole carriers of the de-escalation accept side for their windows, and the round's claim to have shipped accept rows is false about them until the mutants exist.  It is owed BEFORE the fix ships, not filed after.  *** RETRACTED.  MY REFUTATION OF fable5's EQUIVALENCE CLAIM WAS WRONG, AND I READ THE PRISTINE 2021 UPSTREAM COPY OF `memory_rw.c` TO PRODUCE IT.  fable5 WAS RIGHT. ***  I wrote that *\"nothing zeroes `data` before the handler call -- the three `memset(data, 0, len)` sites are all on NON-DEVICE paths\"*, and shipped it in `a115a4a`.  **The fork's `src/cpus/memory_rw.c` has SIX such sites, and one of them is exactly what fable5 described**, inside the device-found branch, before `paddr -= baseaddr` and before the dispatch:  `/*  #95: zero the caller's buffer up front on a read so a device handler that fills only part of it ... can never hand back uninitialised host memory to the guest.  */  if (writeflag == MEM_READ) memset(data, 0, len);`  *** HOW IT HAPPENED, AND IT IS THE HAZARD THIS PROJECT DOCUMENTS IN CAPITALS FOR SEATS -- IT CAUGHT THE MAIN LOOP INSTEAD. ***  There are two `memory_rw.c` under the project root: the fork at **794 lines** (`GXEMUL-SEC`, `est`, `build`, `build-asan`, md5 3d47dd66) and a PRISTINE 2021 UPSTREAM copy at **530 lines** (`./src/cpus/`, md5 75fbe149, plus two archive copies).  My grep ran as `src/cpus/memory_rw.c` while the cwd had drifted to the PROJECT ROOT, so it resolved to the upstream file.  **Every line number I reported -- 161, 222, 425, 437, 486 -- is below 530, and the fork's are 215/267/492/671/683/732.  The numbers themselves were the tell and I did not read them as one.**  **THE CONSEQUENTIAL ERROR, not the embarrassing one:** I dispositioned the early-`return 1` mutant as *\"REAL but NONDETERMINISTIC -- a row asserting a sentinel would be FLAKY\"*.  With #95 in place the guest gets a DETERMINISTIC ZERO, so on the value channel that mutant is **EQUIVALENT**, and a sentinel row is not flaky -- it is simply BLIND to it.  The opus5 seat measured this with two builds differing only in #95's fill byte: filling 0x00 gives `lbu = 0x0`, filling 0xa5 gives `lbu = 0xa5`, tracking byte-for-byte.  *So the disposition inverts: not coverage debt on a flaky channel, but an EQUIVALENT mutant that the mutant-that-will-not-die rule says to dispose of as a RESULT.  In `sgi_de` it is not equivalent overall, because that tail also DRAWS and `dec_switch` separates them -- but in `sgi_re`, whose tail is bare, opus5 expects true equivalence.*  **AND I TOLD THE OWNER I HAD REFUTED A SEAT.**  The correction is recorded here, in the carrier, and reported back -- because a wrong refutation that stands is worse than the original error: it teaches the next round to distrust the seat that was right.  *** L4 -- THE REASON THIS ROW GIVES FOR ITS OWN GAP IS WRONG FOR ONE OF ITS THREE MEMBERS, found 2026-08-31 by #499's PRE-TABLE block (sibling parity) before any mutant was built. ***  The note explains all three missing kills with *the TLB windows are bounded by the NEXT window's constant, not base+size*.  Re-derived from the file: that is TRUE of TLB_A (`< CRIME_RE_TLB_B`) and TLB_B (`< CRIME_RE_TLB_C`) and **FALSE of TLB_C, which is `< CRIME_RE_TLB_C + 0x200` -- structurally IDENTICAL to TEX, LIN_A and LIN_B, all three of which got a shrink mutant and all three of which FIRED.**  So `accept tlbc` was never blocked by window shape; it was simply not built.  **The asymmetry is TWO arms, not three, and a third of the filed obligation is the same edit that already worked three times.**  Recorded because a wrong reason gets reused: a future round reading this note would have gone looking for a special mutant shape for a window that needs the ordinary one.  Durable anchor: `grep -nE 'relative_addr < CRIME_RE' src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c` prints all six far bounds in one screen, which is what made the asymmetry visible at all.  *** #499's MUTANT TABLE (regress/artifacts/sgireunmapped/killtable_20260831/): THE THREE ACCEPT ROWS ARE DISCHARGED, AND THE SEAT OVERTURNED MY FRAMING TWICE. ***  **MY PREMISE WAS FALSE AND I GAVE IT TO THE SEAT AS SETTLED.**  I briefed that the `exit(1)` \"was replaced by a latched refusal\", reading the row's `commits: a643e18` as the fix.  **At HEAD the catch-all is STILL `exit(1)`** -- `grep -n 'unimplemented read from address' src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c`, with the kill two lines below.  `a643e18` shipped the WITNESS and the analysis; **#499 is the round that ships the fix**, not one auditing a shipped one.  *A premise-audit opener would have caught this and my brief did not carry one -- I wrote the settled-premises block and then stated a premise in it that was wrong.*  The seat also corrected my expected-results line: I asked for a baseline that is all-green, and **base is 10/14 BY DESIGN** -- that is test-first, and the all-green control is the FIX.  **THE TABLE.  base 10/14, fix 14/14, and each shrink reddens EXACTLY its own row:** `M1 tlba_shrink` 13/14 (`accept tlba`), `M2 tlbb_shrink` 13/14 (`accept tlbb`), `M3 tlbc_shrink` 13/14 (`accept tlbc`).  **No shrink perturbs a neighbour**, which also refutes this row's own \"a shrink to a next-constant bound moves a boundary two windows share\" -- editing the `<` EXPRESSION opens a gap and does not touch the sibling arm; only editing `CRIME_RE_TLB_B` in `crmfbreg.h` would.  **So all three accept rows now carry measured kills and the L3 is dischargeable.**  **M4 bitcollide ESCAPED at 14/14, exactly as the PRE-TABLE block predicted** -- and the prediction was measured rather than trusted.  The seat then BUILT the killing row: **one emulator, two NARROW (`len != 8`) accesses, one in TLB_A and one in TLB_B**, oracle `count(\"CRIME_RE_TLB_B -- declined\") == 1`.  The session shape is the whole point -- the accept rows do ONE 8-byte store in a fresh emu, so they never enter a width guard and never set a latch bit, making the collision unobservable to them BY CONSTRUCTION.  Generalised for one session's cost: six narrow accesses, one per window, asserting all six decline texts appear, which pins every pairwise collision among the six bits.  *** AND A DEFECT NEITHER OF US HAD ENUMERATED: `#498` SILENTLY BROKE THIS ROW'S WITNESS GENERATOR. ***  `opus_witness_20260830/mkvariant.py` inserts `SGIRE_W_UNMAP_WR = 1 << 6`; `#498`/`cb28a6f` added `SGIRE_W_DE_LEN = 1 << 6` to the same enum.  **A duplicate enumerator is legal C and silent**, and `DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_de)` casts the same `struct sgi_re_data *`, so `d->warned` is shared across both devices.  Measured as `fix6`: **14/14, total escape**, with a transcript showing one guest `sb` to sgi_de permanently silencing sgi_re's catch-all diagnostic.  The replacement `mkvariant499.py` uses `1<<7`/`1<<8` and hard-fails on duplicates.  *A cross-round interaction: one round's enum addition invalidated another round's generator, with no gate able to see it.*  *** M5 -- THE ACCEPT SUITE TESTS THE FAR BOUND AND NEVER THE NEAR BOUND, AND THIS FALSIFIES A CARRIER RULE MINTED FROM THIS VERY ROW EARLIER THE SAME DAY.  Ranked a FLOOR BREACH (clause (d), L2) by the measuring seat. ***  The carrier said: *an accept row written at a window's BASE tests the near bound twice and the far bound never -- put it at the LAST legal slot, **where both bounds are load-bearing***.  The first half is right and is why these rows were moved to the top slot. **The second half is measured FALSE.**  `M5`: `>= CRIME_RE_LINEAR_A` -> `>= CRIME_RE_LINEAR_A + 0x40`, **one token**, scores a **14/14 CLEAN SWEEP** -- while a control row storing at the window BASE reds on M5 and is serviced normally on the fix.  **Moving the rows from base to top SWAPPED the blindness rather than removing it.**  **The reason is structural, not a detail of this device:** an accept row's oracle is *this access was serviced*, which is a single bit, and ONE bound can explain that bit on its own.  So there is no slot from which both bounds are observable, and a single accept row pins exactly the bound it is near.  **THE REPAIR COSTS NO EXTRA SESSIONS AND IS STRICTLY BETTER THAN TWO ROWS:** the store's own `debug()` already prints the destination -- `[ d->re_linear_b[30] = 0x... ]`.  Assert **the array NAME and the INDEX** rather than merely that the catch-all was missed.  That converts each relative row into an ANCHORED row in clause (f)'s sense, pins BOTH bounds from one access, and additionally catches TRANSLATIONS -- which no base-or-top accept row can see at all.  Two rows (base AND top) is the fallback where no such channel exists, and it is weaker: it pins two bounds and still cannot see a re-map that keeps both.  **Because M5 weakens a predicate the fix DEPENDS ON and dominates the edited catch-all, it is clause (d) and therefore L2 -- repaired IN-ROUND, not filed as coverage debt.**  The carrier has been corrected in place; the detector repair ships with the fix.  *This is the freshly-minted-exemplar rule firing on itself: a rule minted from ONE instance carried that instance's shape as if it were the class, and the first round to APPLY it measured the general claim false within hours.  The instruction survived; the universal clause did not.*  *** LATCH-BIT DECONFLICTION BETWEEN TWO ROUNDS IN FLIGHT, caught 2026-08-31 BEFORE either shipped rather than measured afterwards. ***  `dev_sgi_re.c`'s `SGIRE_W_*` enum uses bits **0-6** today (`TLBA/TLBB/TLBC/TEX/LINA/LINB` 0-5, `DE_LEN` 6).  **`#499` takes `1 << 7` (`SGIRE_W_UNMAP_WR`) and `1 << 8` (`SGIRE_W_UNMAP_RD`)**, per its measured `mkvariant499.py` fix.  **`#500` was designed for `SGIRE_W_PRIMOP = 1 << 7` and MUST TAKE `1 << 9` instead.**  *A duplicate enumerator is legal C and silent, and both devices cast the same `struct sgi_re_data *`, so a collision makes one arm's decline permanently suppressible by unrelated traffic to the other -- measured as a 14/14 total escape when it happened between `#498` and `#499`'s generator.*  *** THE PORT SHIPPED AND IT CAUGHT THREE MUTANTS THE ARTIFACT DETECTOR LET THROUGH -- which is the L2 repair working, measured on the artifact that will actually run rather than inherited from the one that will not.  `regress/sgi_re_unmapped_probe.py`, 28 rows, self-contained, artifacts under killtable_20260831/port/. ***  **Re-measured on the SHIPPED probe:** `m4_bitcollide` **14/14 ESCAPED -> 27/28 KILLED**; `m5_lina_near` **14/14 -> 25/28** (three rows); the duplicate-1<<6 variant **14/14 -> 27/28**.  Base 10/14 -> **18/28**, fix 14/14 -> **28/28**.  Five further mutants built and killed: `mret0` 26/28, `m6_idxshift` 26/28, `m7_wintrans` 24/28, `mnolatch` 26/28, `mbothsame` 27/28, `mrdcollide` 27/28.  *** THE TRANSLATION CLAIM WAS MEASURED AND CAME BACK NARROWER THAN I STATED IT, which is why it was asked for as a measurement. ***  I recorded that the anchored-oracle repair *should also catch translations*.  Measured: **anchoring ALONE does not catch a translation; anchoring PLUS a base-slot row does.**  Under `m7_wintrans` the TOP-slot row `tlba acc hi` stayed **GREEN** -- a row inside the moved window cannot see that the window moved -- while `tlba acc lo` reddened by falling out, and `tlbb acc lo` reddened on the MIS-ATTRIBUTION channel (the transcript says `d->re_tlb_a[0]` where the row wants `d->re_tlb_b[0]`), which no unanchored oracle can see at all.  An index re-map (`m6_idxshift`) IS caught outright, both rows.  **So the true claim is: anchored + BOTH bounds catches index re-maps outright and catches window translations at the base row.**  *** AND THE SEAT CHECKED WHAT ALREADY EXISTED BEFORE BUILDING WHAT I ASKED FOR. ***  I proposed generalising the latch-collision row to all six windows.  `sgi_re_width_probe.py`'s `xsite 6msg` **already ships that exact generalisation, is gate-wired, and measured 32/35 against m4** -- so a duplicate would have been a row that can never fail alone.  It re-aimed the idea at THIS round's own two bits instead (`xwin wr`/`xwin rd` walk all six #497 latches plus #498's sgi_de bit, since the four handlers share one malloc'd struct), with `xwin7 pre` as the partner control.  **ONE ROW DELIBERATELY DROPPED and one row's base value deliberately CHANGED, both disclosed rather than buried:** `read0 VACUOUS` was a pedagogical control that cannot fail under the mutant it names, so it is gone and its lesson survives as the reason `rd sent` presets a sentinel; and `latch fresh` (green on base, because base ALSO prints its text before dying) became `xwin wr`, which counts the `-- declined.` literal and is RED on base.  *A port that silently changes a score is the failure mode here, so both are named.*  **COVERAGE DEBT, stated rather than glossed: 8 of 28 rows never failed on any of 14 variants.**  Two are the witness ladder's clause-(ii) controls (`live`, `devsig`) and fire only if the harness or machine breaks.  The other six are per-window accept replicas of shapes discharged elsewhere, filed WITH the mutants that would discharge them (near-grow at TLB_C, far-shrink at TEX/LIN_B, near-grow at LIN_B).  **The seat stopped rather than chase per-row, which is the `#392` shape** -- the right call.  *One caveat it raised against its own work: `xwin7 pre` is a control that a product defect can redden (m4, m5, m7).  That is a valid kill, but the round must not cite it as the PRIMARY m4 detector -- `xsite 6msg` names that properly.*  *** INDEPENDENT REVIEW OF THE RE-PIN, 2026-08-31: DO NOT SHIP IT AS WRITTEN.  It repairs `otherkilldeath` and walks STRAIGHT INTO `exit0oracle`. ***  The proposed oracle is `exit0 + text`.  **Mutant: keep the fix's latch and message and add `exit(0);` after the if/else at the catch-all.**  The message prints (text True), the process exits 0 (`how == \"exit0\"`), `got == want` -> **GREEN, total escape** -- and `other kill` is the ONLY row in that 35-row probe that touches `OUTSIDE` (verified: two hits in the file, the definition and one use), so nothing else catches it.  *** AND THE KNOWLEDGE WAS ALREADY IN THE GATE, ADDRESSED TO THIS EXACT CLASS, WRITTEN THE SAME DAY. ***  `regress/gate_arm.sh` carries the flagship seat's note against re-pinning `tail_acc_x` onto `exit0` -- *exit status cannot vary between 'declined and then quit' and 'died with status 0'* -- together with the repair.  **The `#499` re-pin was written without it.**  That is this project's unread-review shape: the answer sat in a file the round had itself just edited.  **REPAIR, using material already in the probe:** assert a DEVICE SIGNATURE through the existing `DE_STATUS`/`DE_STAT_VAL` constants (the `devsig stat` idiom), giving survival AND identity in one channel; or the `lw surv` sentinel-preset idiom, which distinguishes *the tail ran and wrote odata 0* from *the load never completed*.  **Minimum:** `session()` already returns `alive` and the proposal throws it away.  **So the round owes a FOURTH mutant of its own -- `exit(0)` at the sgi_re catch-all -- and the order measurement must be RE-RUN with whichever oracle actually ships.**  Worth knowing: the NEW 28-row probe would survive that mutant; the escape is confined to the re-pinned shipped row, so this is a floor question about the RE-PIN, not about the new detector.  *** THE ORDER CLAIM OVERCLAIMS BY ONE WORD, AND A STRONGER WARRANT WAS ALREADY IN HAND. ***  `REPIN_REQUIRED.md` is headed *\"ORDER DISCIPLINE -- measured, not asserted\"*.  **The RED is measured; the ORDER is not** -- every artifact carries the same copy-time mtime, so nothing shows the oracle was written before the fix was built.  **But the oracle was PRE-REGISTERED in a committed artifact by a prior round**: `git log -S \"death is the shared answer\"` gives `112867a` (#497, 2026-08-30 22:41), the commit that filed `otherkilldeath` and prescribed this repair -- **six hours before** this round's baseline `3ded1e8` (2026-08-31 04:34).  *That is checkable by `git show` rather than by trusting a timestamp, and it is what should carry the not-laundering claim.*  L4: correct the heading.  *** THE PORTED DETECTOR IS INDEPENDENTLY VALIDATED BY THE COORDINATOR, NOT ONLY BY THE SEAT THAT WROTE IT.  2026-08-31, log preserved at `regress/artifacts/sgireunmapped/killtable_20260831/port/coordinator_validate.out`. ***  Run against the COMMITTED tree at `fe5e2c1` with `build/gxemul`, from the gate's own working directory: **`PROBE_RC=0`, `OPUS499T_CONTROL=OK`, `OPUS499T_RESULT=18/28`, `OPUS499T_DONE` present.**  18 ok + 10 FAIL = 28, so the tally is internally consistent and no row was lost.  **AND THE TEN RED ROWS ARE EXACTLY THE ONES THE FIX ADDRESSES**, which is the test-first evidence rather than a bare number:  `wr surv`, `rd surv` (the host dies today), `wr kill`, `rd kill` (the kill text is present today), `wr decl`, `rd decl` (the decline text is absent today), `rd sent` (the sentinel survives an unanswered read), `wr rd 2msg` (no per-direction messages exist yet), and `xwin wr`/`xwin rd` (the new latch bits do not exist yet).  **Every accept row and both witness-ladder controls stay GREEN**, so the detector is not simply red about everything.  *Three things this checks that a seat's summary cannot: that the probe RUNS from the gate's cwd rather than only the seat's, that it emits its completion sentinel so a score is a measurement rather than a truncation, and that the reddening rows are the RIGHT ones.  The figure matches the seat's report exactly, which is what makes it a confirmation rather than a second opinion.*  *** CLOSED 2026-08-31 at `9f666a8`. ***  The catch-all is a per-direction latched decline falling through to the common tail; live `exit(1)` in `dev_sgi_re.c` 14 -> 13.  Two bits (`1 << 7`, `1 << 8`) placed AFTER `SGIRE_W_DE_LEN` in numeric order, because an earlier draft left the enum ending at `1 << 6` and a maintainer scanning to the last enumerator would have taken an already-used bit.  **The re-pin that shipped is NOT the one proposed**: `exit0+txt` was measured to ESCAPE an `exit(0)` mutant, and the measuring seat's own first-choice replacement escaped two more, so the sentinel-preset read of the unmapped address itself is what shipped.  Detector wired in the same commit, both hygiene pins raised in the same commit, all four build trees propagated and both clearance parts verified.  *** L4 CORRECTION, FOUND BY THE LATE PASS 2 -- AND IT IS THE EVIDENCE THAT THE LATE PASS WAS NOT THEATRE. ***  The measuring seat wrote that the shipped oracle's three failures are *three DISTINCT values*, and I repeated it into the pass-2 brief and the round's report.  **It is two, not three.**  Measured, from `repin2/table500b.out`:  `mexit0` -> `exit0/txt/died`;  `mret0` -> `exit0/txt/0xdeadbeef`;  `mrdexit0` -> `exit0/txt/died`.  **`died` appears twice**, so it is three FAILURES carrying two DISTINCT values.  **The substance survives and the overclaim does not.**  What the measurement supports is that the sentinel channel VARIES where the alternatives do not -- N2 fails all three while N1 (the device-signature read) fails only one -- which is the argument that actually mattered.  What it does not support is the stronger *three distinct values* framing used to dismiss the one-channel-wearing-three-hats objection.  *Caught by a packet-fed seat reading the parenthetical `(died / 0xdeadbeef / died)` against the claim it was offered to support -- i.e. by checking a claim against its OWN cited evidence, which is the cheapest audit there is and which neither I nor the seat that wrote it performed.*  **AND IT IS DIRECT EVIDENCE ON THE QUESTION OF WHETHER A LATE PASS 2 IS WORTH RUNNING**: this round shipped without one, the gate refused the next commit for it, and the pass run afterwards found a wrong record in the round's own justification within minutes.  *** codex's SEMANTIC CORRECTION, recorded because the records use the wrong word: THIS IS NOT A DECLINE AT THE CALLBACK BOUNDARY. ***  The handler returns **1** for an unmatched access exactly as for a serviced one, so `memory_rw` sees a handled access either way.  What the fix changes is that the host survives and a latched message is printed -- the access is still ACCEPTED at the boundary and answered with a defined 0 on the read side.  **The six-member option set distinguishes DECLINE from ACCEPT by precisely this boundary**, so calling it a decline blurs the distinction the set exists to draw.  *The behaviour is right and the word is wrong; a future round reading `sgireunmapped` as a DECLINE precedent would be citing something that returns success.*  *** L2 FLOOR BREACH, MEASURED BY THE PASS-2 SEAT: TWO MUTANTS SWEEP ALL 28 ROWS CLEAN, AND ONE RESTORES THIS ROUND'S OWN CITED WITNESS VERBATIM.  Repair is owed IN-ROUND however late. ***  **`W` -- re-add the pre-`#499` `len != 8` kill at the top of the catch-all, lifted verbatim from `9f666a8^`.  28/28, zero red -- and `sb` AND `lb` at `0xb5001800` die, printing the pre-fix kill text.**  That is floor clause (b): a verbatim reinstatement of the removed defect passes the detector.  **`T` -- `relative_addr < CRIME_RE_TEX + 0xe0` -> `+ 0x100`, ONE HEX DIGIT.  28/28, zero red -- and `ld` at `0xb50016e0` dies with `TODO: read from CRIME_RE_TEX`**, handing 32 bytes back to `exit(1)`.  Clause (d): a bound relaxed on a predicate dominating the edited lines.  *** THE TWO REASONS ARE STRUCTURAL, NOT LUCKY, AND I VERIFIED BOTH. ***  **(1) EVERY catch-all row uses len 8 and nothing else.**  `store()` defaults to `OP_SD_T0_A3` and `load()` to `OP_LD_V0_AT`; `OP_SB_T0_A3` exists but is aimed at the six mapped windows.  **So the detector pins the DIRECTION axis -- which the contract argues at length -- and never the WIDTH axis at the catch-all, while the round's HEADLINE is *no width predicate at all* and its WITNESS is a `lb`.**  **(2) All four catch-all offsets (`0x800/0x8c0/0x900/0x9c0`) land in the SECOND component.**  The round's own CHANGELOG names two: `0x16e0-0x16ff` (32 bytes) and `0x1800-0x1fff` (2048).  **The 32-byte component has ZERO rows.**  *This is `#498`'s `len == 2` hole one round later, in the same file: a detector that exercises one axis exhaustively and the other not at all.*  **AND THE PRETABLE MANDATE WAS ONLY PARTLY DISCHARGED, which is why the CHANNEL check did not catch it.**  The block's own header names baseline `e898c84`, the ARTIFACT detector, and the subject *discharge accept tlba/tlbb/tlbc*.  It does not cover the port to 28 rows, the fix, the re-pin, or the two new bits -- its GUARD-FIELD section enumerates SEVEN bits and says *all seven distinct today*, written before `1<<7` and `1<<8` existed.  **The CHANNEL check was never run over the catch-all rows, which is precisely where both mutants escape.**  The block's own opening line says `#498` skipped it and paid; the same shape recurred here one scope level up.  *** THE L2 IS REPAIRED IN-ROUND AND MEASURED.  Detector 28 -> 34 rows; both escaping mutants now die.  Artifacts: killtable_20260831/l2repair/. ***    fix **34/34**  |  `T` (`CRIME_RE_TEX + 0xe0` -> `+ 0x100`) **33/34**, `clip decl` red, was 28/28  |  `W` (the pre-`#499` `len != 8` kill re-added verbatim) **30/34**, all four narrow rows red, was 28/28.  **Six rows: four on the WIDTH axis** (a byte write and a 4-byte read at the catch-all, each with a survival row AND a positive channel -- the decline announced, the sentinel replaced by a defined 0, because a survival-only row is satisfied by a mutant surviving for the wrong reason) **and two on the FIRST COMPONENT** at `RE + 0x6e0`, which the handler's own `+0x1000` makes `0x16e0`.  *** AND THE VERIFICATION HIT TWO TRAPS THIS PROJECT HAD ALREADY WRITTEN DOWN, BOTH MINE. ***  **(1)** The first `W` build died with `error: missing terminating \" character` -- the `\\n` in the inserted C string cooked into a REAL NEWLINE through the Bash -> heredoc -> Python path, splitting the literal.  **The measuring seat had warned me about this exact path in the same report** (*do not hand-type it ... it cost me one round-trip*) and the carrier prescribes `chr(92)`.  I hand-typed it.  Rebuilt with `chr(92)` plus a check that the literal landed on ONE line.  *The stale-binary guard is what caught it: the build failed, left the old binary, and the run ABORTED rather than scoring a false escape.*  **(2)** My extra confirmation -- *does `W` really restore the witness?* -- used a debugger `put b` and reported ZERO kill messages.  **`put b` never reaches the device**: the byte path passes `NO_EXCEPTIONS` and `memory_rw` skips the device call for `DM_DEFAULT` devices.  That is the trap measured on `pcc2` and recorded by me hours earlier.  **The probe's own rows use real guest `sb`/`lw` instructions, which is exactly why they caught `W` while the debugger poke saw nothing** -- so the four red rows are the evidence and the `put b` line is an apparatus artifact.  *** AND THE L2 REPAIR ITSELF HAD THE SAME DEFECT ONE LEVEL DOWN, FOUND BY THE PASS THAT THE `no recursion` RULING WOULD HAVE SKIPPED.  Detector 34 -> 36. ***  I diagnosed the breach as *one axis pinned exhaustively, the other not at all*, repaired the width axis at the addresses the mutants used, and left the identical hole at the other component: the two `clip` rows took `store()`'s default `OP_SD`, so the matrix read **three cells of a two-by-two** -- component1 x narrow had nothing.  **A guard re-added at the catch-all was caught; a guard re-added and CONDITIONED on the first component would not have been.**  Two rows added (`clip narrow surv`, `clip narrow decl`).  Measured:  fix **36/36**;  `T` **34/36** with TWO reds where the 34-row version had one;  `W` **30/36** with **SIX** reds where it had four -- narrow now catches at BOTH components.  *** THIS ALSO ANSWERS THE SEAT THAT DOUBTED THE ROWS: deepseek called the first-component rows *suspect as survival-only*, i.e. possibly unable to vary.  They vary -- both `decl` rows moved 1 -> 0 under `T` while their paired `surv` rows held.  The positive channel is doing the work, which is why each row was paired that way. ***  *Found by three packet-fed seats reviewing the repair -- while BLINDED, because my brief claimed to inline the diff and inlined zero hunks.  Two of them said so plainly rather than answering around it, and found the gap anyway.*",
tools/pipeline\ledger.json:28747:   "state_note": "\n\n*** CODEX REFUSAL #7, 2026-09-07, ON THE `lcaconfwidth` CENSUS BRIEF -- AND MY OWN LAUNCHER\nREPORTED IT AS A CLEAN RUN.  Two defects, both mine, both of the check-that-cannot-fail family. ***\n\n    codex rc=1        stdout 0 bytes        stderr 768,161 bytes\n    stderr tail:  ERROR: This content was flagged for possible cybersecurity risk.\n    tokens used:  108,432\n\n**The seat did 108k tokens of real work first** -- the stderr shows it reading `dev_osiop.c` and\nenumerating diagnostic sites -- and the filter discarded the answer at the end.  So this is not a\nseat that declined the task; it is a seat whose completed answer was destroyed on the way out.\n\n**LAUNCHER DEFECT 1: the content check read the WRONG STREAM.**  It grepped the `.txt` for refusal\ntext.  A content-filter refusal writes NOTHING to stdout and puts the ERROR line on STDERR, so the\ncheck printed `refusal hits: 0` **on a refusal**.  A check that looks in the wrong stream returns\nthe same answer whether or not the thing it tests is true -- which is the exact shape this project\nnamed after `panel.sh`'s phantom re-fire.\n\n**LAUNCHER DEFECT 2: the script exited 0 while holding `rc=1`.**  It captured codex's status into a\nvariable, printed it, and then ended on a successful command.  **The harness reported the task\nCOMPLETED.**  That is the failure-laundered-into-a-clean-completion class, and this project has\nthree prior instances (a `diff` under `set -e`, a Windows path to `wsl`, a truncated preserved log).\n\n> **BOTH FIXED in `cxlaunch.sh`, which greps BOTH STREAMS and exits 7 on a refusal, 8 on a wall, 6\n> on an empty answer with neither, and `rc` otherwise.**  A refused seat can no longer render as a\n> completed one.\n\n**THE TRIGGER, added to the recorded set.**  The brief asked, of a kill diagnostic, *\"say whether a\n64-bit value exists that trips the guard and prints as something the guard would accept -- give the\ndiscriminating value where one exists.\"*  That is a diagnostic-correctness question, but its SHAPE\nis *find the input that defeats the check*, and it sits beside an enumeration of `exit(1)` sites.\n**Chain-shaped content, whatever the question form** -- the trigger this project already recorded,\nmet again.\n\n**ROUTED TO GROK**, which the carrier records as the working path for a refused brief and which has\nanswered three of them, finding something material in each.\n\n*** GROK CENSUS 2026-09-07 (`regress/artifacts/lcaconfwidth/grok_census_20260907.txt`), after\ncodex refused the brief.  IT CORRECTED MY COUNT IN BOTH DIRECTIONS AND NARROWED THE FIX TO TWO\nLINES. ***\n\n**MY CENSUS MISSED A SPELLING.**  I grepped `(int)idata`; the tree also writes **`(int) idata`**\nwith a space.  Real total **309 sites in 70 files**, not 226 -- and 309 itself includes\nCOMMENTED-OUT casts (`dev_clmpcc.c:125`, `dev_dc7085.c:236`, `dev_dec21143.c:914/1072`), so the live\nfigure is lower again.\n\n**AND MY PROXIMITY SET WAS SHORT: 18 live sites within +/-3 of an `exit(1)`, not 15.**  The three I\nmissed are `g2` CHAN_ENABLE, `g2` XFER_ENABLE and the `osiop` default -- a blank line inside the\nwindow is still inside the window.  grok also priced the string-vs-call distinction: a scanner that\ncounted comment text would report **30** (18 live + 2 commented-out `exit(1)` + 10 `dev_pvr.c`\n*\"don't exit(1)\"* comments).\n\n*** THE FINDING THAT SETS THE DISPOSITION, and it is the opposite of a tree-wide class: ***\n\n    (uint64_t)idata  IS next to a live exit(1) FIVE times, ALL IN dev_lca.c\n        225/226  TB_ENA      240/241  W_BASE0     255/256  W_MASK0\n        279/280  W_BASE1     294/295  W_MASK1\n    (uint32_t)idata  13 sites, NONE within +/-3 of a live exit(1)\n    (long)idata       1 site, not near one\n    (int32_t) / (unsigned int) idata   0\n\n**Those five LCA guards already print the FULL 64-bit value.**  So `dev_lca.c` is mostly correct\nalready, and the narrowing print survives at exactly **two** sites in it -- `:205` (IOC_CONF, the\nexemplar) and `:313`.  **The repair is two lines in one file, matching the idiom its own siblings\ntwelve lines away already use.**  That is a transferred warrant from inside the same function, which\nis the strongest kind this project recognises, and it makes the sibling-disposition check answer FOR\na local fix here -- the opposite of how it answered for [[sgirebufshiftub]].\n\n**Also found: the filed PVR 'sharpest instance' is ALREADY DE-FATALISED by `#166`** -- the fourth\nalready-fixed claim surfaced today.\n\n> **ORDERING vs [[lcabadval]]:** that row's witness must match `fatal()` TEXT rather than exit status\n> (because the interrupt stubs kill with the same status), and IOC_CONF is one of the arms it would\n> read.  With the repair confined to two lines whose siblings already do it right, taking this first\n> is cheap and removes the oracle's one unsound arm.\n\n*** REACHABILITY ESTABLISHED BY READING, 2026-09-07 -- so a rung-3 witness is a construction\nproblem rather than an open question. ***\n\n    alpha_lcareg.h      LCA_IOC_BASE 0x180000000 ; LCA_IOC_CONF = BASE + 0x020 = 0x180000020\n    dev_lca.c :359-361  memory_device_register(..., \"lca_ioc\", LCA_IOC_BASE, 0x20000000,\n                        dev_lca_ioc_access, ..., DM_DEFAULT, NULL)   <- NO length constraint\n    dev_lca.c :188      idata = memory_readmax64(cpu, data, len)     <- takes the full width\n    machine_alpha.c :61 device_add(machine, \"lca\") -- inside `case ST_ALPHABOOK1:` ONLY\n\n**And the technique is already proven on that exact machine.**  `regress/alpha_harness_probe.py`\nran today on an unmodified `-E alpha -e alphabook1` at **7/9**, with `live`, `exec` and `palgate`\ngreen; it builds K0SEG addresses as `0xfffffc0000000000 | x` and K0SEG translation is\n`vaddr & 0x000003ffffffffff`, so `k0(0x180000020)` reaches the register with no page tables.\n\n**The 64-bit question is answered too:** Alpha `STQ` is opcode `0x2d` with `loadstore_type = 3`\n(quadword) -- established in the grok harness review against `cpu_alpha_instr.c` -- so a guest `stq`\ndelivers `len = 8` and `idata` holds bit 32.  **The discriminating store is therefore\n`0x100000000` to `k0(0x180000020)`: the guard `idata & ~1` fires, and `(int)idata` prints `0x0` --\na value that same guard ACCEPTS.**\n\n> **THE CONTROL THIS WITNESS OWES, and it is the device form of the trap that nearly voided the\n> Alpha harness:** if `k0(0x180000020)` does NOT land on the LCA window, the store goes to unmapped\n> space and **nothing happens at all** -- no message, no exit -- which is indistinguishable from\n> *the guard did not fire*.  So the probe needs a positive control proving the store reached\n> `dev_lca`: a legal `0x1` write that SURVIVES, and/or a read-back of `d->ioc_conf` through\n> `LCA_IOC_CONF`.  **That question is out to the grok seat rather than settled here**, because\n> whether either is sufficient alone is exactly the judgement this project keeps getting wrong.\n\n*Design brief: `regress/artifacts/lcaconfwidth/WITNESS_brief.md`.  Sent to grok rather than codex --\ncodex refused the sibling census brief on its content filter, and the shape 'find the value that\ndefeats the check' is the recorded trigger even when the subject is a printf format specifier.*\n\n*** GROK WITNESS DESIGN, 2026-09-07 (`grok_witness2_20260907.txt`).  VERDICT: CONSTRUCTIBLE as a\nrung-3 witness on stock `alphabook1`.  It corrected FOUR of my premises, one of which was a wrong\nrecord in this row's own OUTSTANDING_BUGS entry. ***\n\n**1. THE RECOMMENDED FIX WAS WRONG, AND SO WAS ITS WARRANT.**  The entry said `0x%llx` with a\n`(long long)` cast, *\"the file's own idiom elsewhere\"*.  `dev_lca.c` uses **`PRIx64` five times and\n`%llx` never**, and the siblings **KEEP** the cast as `(uint64_t)idata`.  My design brief compounded\nit by saying to DROP the cast -- a third wrong form.  Corrected in place.\n\n**2. `load_const` CANNOT BUILD EITHER OPERAND.**  LDA/LDAH make 32-bit sign-extended constants; the\nK0SEG address needs bits 63..42 and the test value needs bit 32.  **A probe using `load_const` for\neither is not this witness.**  The working bootstrap is the one the Alpha harness already uses:\n`put d` the 64-bit words into RAM below 64 MB, then guest `ldq` them while `ptbr` is still 0.\n\n**3. THE MISS MODE IS NOT ONLY 'UNMAPPED -> NOTHING'.**  Offset 0 is `LCA_IOC_BASE` (HAE), which\n`break`s with no kill -- so a store to `k0(0x180000000)` is **silent while INSIDE the LCA window**.\nUnmapped and HAE both read back 0, which is why the control has to distinguish them.\n\n**4. TWO ROUTES THAT LOOK LIKE CONTROLS AND ARE NOT:**\n  * **debugger `dump` cannot read the register.**  `dump` calls `memory_rw` with `NO_EXCEPTIONS`, and\n    LCA is `DM_DEFAULT` rather than `DM_READS_HAVE_NO_SIDE_EFFECTS`, so the handler is **SKIPPED** and\n    the caller gets the pre-zeroed buffer -- *the unmapped signature, produced by a hit.*\n  * **`put d` of the killing value is not rung-3.**  `store_64bit_word` goes through `memory_rw` with\n    `CACHE_DATA` and no `NO_EXCEPTIONS`, so it DOES enter the handler and `exit(1)` from the\n    debugger -- a real kill, by no guest instruction.\n\n> **THE CONTROL THAT WORKS: a guest `stq` of `0x1` followed by a guest `ldq`, same decode, BEFORE\n> the killing store.**  It is the analogue of the Alpha harness's `swpctx mapped` row.  Neither it\n> nor the kill text is sufficient alone -- the round-trip proves the window is THIS arm and not HAE\n> or unmapped; the text `IOC_CONF: 0x0` proves the arm fired and that a 32-bit zero store could not\n> have produced that pair.\n\n**Q3 CONFIRMED IN DETAIL, and it hardens S2:** `stq` is opcode `0x2d` -> `loadstore_type 3` -> `LS_Q`;\n`host_store` is NULL for a device page, so it falls to `LS_GENERIC_N` and calls `memory_rw(..., 8,\nMEM_WRITE)`; the memblock granule is 1 MB so `0x20+8` crosses nothing; Alpha is little-endian and\n`memory_readmax64` rebuilds the value.  **And the guard's own arithmetic is load-bearing:** `~1` is a\nsigned `int` (-2) converting to `0xfffffffffffffffe`, **so it sees bit 32 -- `~1U` would NOT, and\nthat is a different mutant worth building.**  The same function already compares against\n`0x300800000ULL`, so 64-bit values are this handler's native width.\n\n**One honest weakening:** `(int)0x100000000 == 0` is IMPLEMENTATION-DEFINED, not guaranteed by C99.\nGCC/Clang truncate, so the witness holds on this build -- but the row should not claim a language\nguarantee.\n\n*** AND THE 309 FIGURE WAS ALREADY IN THIS ROW'S OWN ENTRY, DATED 2026-08-31. ***  I ran a census\ntoday, got 226, treated it as new, and was corrected to 309 by a seat -- while\n`OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md` had recorded **\"309 casts across 70 device files\"** five days earlier, with\nits deriver.  **The unread-review class, in the file I was writing about.**  (Its companion figure,\n45 host-kill sites, is not in conflict with today's 18: the two used different window sizes, and\nneither states one, which is the reason they cannot be reconciled.)\n\n*** WITNESS BUILT AND RUN THE SAME HOUR THE DESIGN LANDED.  HEAD 4/6. ***\n\n    live          alive   alive          ok    the control session survives the legal write\n    ioc rt        0x1     0x1            ok    guest stq 0x1 then guest ldq, SAME decode\n    kill fired    yes     yes            ok    the IOC_CONF arm announced -- not HAE, not unmapped\n    kill width    0x0     0x100000000    FAIL  the message must name the value that ACTUALLY killed\n    kill notzero  0x0     NOT 0x0        FAIL  0x0 is a value this guard ACCEPTS\n    died          died    died           ok    exit(1) really ran\n\nAll four of grok's design corrections are implemented and each is a comment in the probe: the\n64-bit operands are planted with `put d` and loaded with `ldq` (`load_const` cannot build them);\nthe read-back is a guest `ldq` (debugger `dump` would skip a `DM_DEFAULT` handler and hand back the\npre-zeroed buffer -- the unmapped signature from a hit); the killing store is a guest `stq` (a\n`put d` would kill from the debugger and not be rung-3); and `ioc rt` distinguishes the real arm\nfrom HAE at offset 0, which `break`s silently INSIDE the window.\n\n*** AND MY FIRST DRAFT HAD THE ORACLE POLARITY BACKWARDS -- it scored 6/6 on the committed build. ***\nIt asserted the DEFECT as the passing state, so it read as success and would have gone RED the\nmoment the bug was fixed.  **The ladder requires a witness to show the symptom pre-fix and go green\npost-fix WITH THE SAME SCRIPT**, which is clause (i).  The sibling `alpha_harness_probe.py` had it\nright (7/9) and this one did not, in the same tree, on the same day.\n\n> *A witness that passes on the committed build is a detector-of-the-defect wearing a witness's\n> clothes.  The tell is cheap and I nearly missed it: **ask what the row does after the fix.**  If\n> the answer is 'goes red', the polarity is inverted whatever the score says.*\n\n**The repair is now fully specified and unblocks [[lcabadval]]:** `0x%\" PRIx64` with\n`(uint64_t)idata`, copying the five sibling arms verbatim; no gate, hygiene grep or probe in\n`regress/` matches this message's text, so changing it turns nothing else red.\n\n*** `#506` APPLIED AND MEASURED, 2026-09-07.  THE ROUND IS COMPLETE AND HELD FOR THE 9th. ***\n\n    HEAD  4/6   probe_head_20260907.out   `kill width' 0x0,          `kill notzero' 0x0\n    fix   6/6   probe_fix_20260907.out    `kill width' 0x100000000,  `kill notzero' wider\n\n**Same script, red before and green after** -- ladder clause (i) discharged by the pair rather than\nasserted.  Both sites repaired: `:205` (IOC_CONF, the sharp instance -- guard on the VALUE) and\n`:319` (the `default:` arm, weaker -- guard on the OFFSET, so its narrowed value is wrong without\nbeing one the guard accepts).\n\nPropagated to all four trees; `build/`, `build-asan/` and `/tmp/gxsec-build` rebuilt with the\ntwo-part clearance CLEAN/FRESH on each, `est/` part 2 N/A.  **Nothing committed** -- the Fable hold\nstands, and this round now waits as FINISHED WORK rather than as a proposal.\n\n*** THE WITNESS HAD A ROW THAT COULD NOT FAIL, AND A PASS-2 REVIEW OF THE ROUND FOUND IT.\nREPAIRED AND CONTROLLED, 2026-09-07. ***\n\nThe rows tested `\"IOC_CONF: 0x100000000\" in text` -- a SUBSTRING -- and manufactured the displayed\n`got` from the boolean.  codex: *\"Append one literal 0 before the diagnostic's newline.  The message\nbecomes 0x1000000000, yet `kill width` still reports a green 0x100000000.\"*  `zero` had the mirror\nflaw (`0x0abc` contains `0x0`).\n\n> **That is the check-that-cannot-fail shape INSIDE THE WITNESS FOR THE DEFECT IT EXISTS TO PIN** --\n> and it survived my own review, a design review, and the run.  It was found only by asking a seat\n> what edit keeps the witness green.\n\n**REPAIRED** to parse the number and compare integers, reporting the value actually found.\n**CONTROLLED in both directions:**\n\n    fixed build, repaired oracle          6/6      kill width 0x100000000\n    codex's escape (TRAILING 0)           5/6      kill width 0x1000000000  RED\n\n*** AND MY FIRST CONTROL TESTED THE WRONG MUTANT, which is worth recording because it would have\nbeen a false proof. ***  I applied the extra `0` BEFORE the value (`0x0%\" PRIx64`), giving\n`0x0100000000` -- **numerically identical to `0x100000000`**, so the row passed correctly and proved\nnothing.  That is the carrier's own *a mutant that will not die is a RESULT* rule, case 1: the mutant\nis mis-built.  **The leading-zero form is a genuine EQUIVALENT mutant** and is recorded as such\nrather than as an escape.  The trailing-zero form is codex's, and it reds.\n\n*Artifacts: `probe_fix_newora_20260907.out`, `probe_mutant_trailzero_20260907.out`, and the\nmis-built `probe_mutant_extrazero_20260907.out` kept as the record of the equivalence.*\n\n*** COMMIT ATTRIBUTION RESTORED 2026-09-08: this row's fix shipped in **`7a41b3f`** and the\n`commits` field was empty.  Verified before writing, not assumed. ***\n\n    git show --stat 7a41b3f        touches src/devices/dev_lca.c\n    git show 7a41b3f -- src/devices/dev_lca.c\n                                   contains the `#506' round tag 6 times\n\n**ALL FOUR ROUNDS IN THAT COMMIT HAD THE SAME GAP.**  `7a41b3f` is titled *\"#505 #506 #507 #508:\nfour test-first rounds, three new rung-3 witnesses, and a witness row that could not fail\"*, and\n**not one of the four rows carried its commit.**  `sgirergbroundtrip` (#505) was found first, by\naccident, while scoping an unrelated round in the same file; the other three were found by asking\nthe general question afterwards.\n\n> **THE CLASS: A COMMIT THAT SHIPS SEVERAL ROUNDS LOSES THE PER-ROW ATTRIBUTION FOR ALL OF THEM.**\n> The committer is performing ONE act and the ledger records N rows, so the per-row field is the\n> thing that falls out.  A one-round commit does not have this failure mode -- which is why it has\n> not been seen before and why it appeared four times at once.\n\n*The `state` field is deliberately NOT touched here.  `in work` may be correct for a row with\nresidual obligations, and changing it would be a claim I have not checked.  Only the attribution,\nwhich is unambiguous, is filled in.*",
tools/pipeline\ledger.json:31646:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07 from the RETROSPECTIVE pass 2 on `79cd12e' -- the receipt that round never filed, which\n`precommit_check.sh' section F refused the next commit over.  **This is the pass's one actionable finding.**\n\n**THE PREDICTION, from the grok seat, verbatim:** *\"The `#502` detector, asked of the shipped composite, has\none clause-(d) mutant I predict sweeps **59/59**: `implemented = true` -> `implemented =\n(cpu->machine->ncpus < 2)' on the seven arms.  I could not build it (read-only seat; gates forbidden).  If\nthat score is real it is **L2 of the detector**, not of `cpu_m88k.c`.\"*\n\n**IT IS THE CANONICAL CLAUSE-(d) SHAPE**: the guard is kept and its repair is CONDITIONED on a field the\nguarded body reads -- `if (X)` -> `if (X && Y)` on the dominating predicate.  The floor says a clause-(d)\nmutant that sweeps a detector clean is an **L2 breach, repaired in-round however late it is found**.\n\n*** STATUS: PREDICTED, NOT MEASURED.  That distinction is the whole disposition. ***  The floor's L2 rule\nfires on a MEASURED false pass; a prediction is a candidate.  grok said plainly it could not build the mutant\n-- read-only seat, gates forbidden -- and did NOT dress the prediction as a score.  Recording it as owed rather\nthan acting on it is the honest reading, and recording it at all is what stops it becoming an unread review.\n\n**WHY IT IS CREDIBLE ENOUGH TO MEASURE:** the same seat, in the same answer, correctly settled two other\npremises by re-derivation -- that `machine->ncpus` has three assignment sites all preceding any device (so\n`#503`'s DEVINIT snapshot is stable, which is what minimax had named as the settling fact and could not check\nfrom the packet), and that `#502` delivers all seven classes at BOTH sites, i.e. it is NOT the `#499`\none-cell-repair shape that glm's arithmetic had suggested.  A seat that got two checkable things right in the\nsame answer has earned the build.\n\n**TO MEASURE IT:** the mutant is one edit repeated on seven arms, scored against the shipped\n`regress/m88k_exc_probe.py` (59 rows).  This is what the **agy measurement reserve exists for** -- it is the\nonly compile-and-measure seat not behind the Agent-tool transport, and Opus 5 / Fable 5 are held for the\n2026-09-09 adjudication.  agy has no sandbox, so the brief must say: COPY the tree, build the COPY, never\nwrite to GXEMUL-SEC, `rm src/cpus/*.o' first, and check `[ binary -nt source ]' because MAKE_RC has been\nobserved reporting 0 on a failed build.\n\n**IF IT SWEEPS 59/59 it is L2 of the DETECTOR** -- `m88k_exc_probe.py`, not the product fix, which every seat\nagrees is in the right place.  **If it does not sweep, the row closes as a measured non-finding** with the\nscore recorded, which is a result and not a waste.\n\n*** SECOND, INDEPENDENT PREDICTION OF THE SAME HOLE -- FROM A DIFFERENT SEAT, ON A DIFFERENT MUTANT.\nThat is the one kind of agreement this project counts as evidence. ***\n\nThe `kimi-k3:cloud` substitute (the CLI being 403-walled) returned **NOT A CLEAN RATIFY** and predicted:\n\n    *\"a one-line deletion of `implemented = true;' on an unprobed exception class re-opens #502 exactly,\n     and every probe row named in this packet stays green.\"*\n\n**It is a DIFFERENT mutant from grok's** -- a bare DELETION on a class the probe never drives, where grok's is\na CONDITIONED repair (`implemented = (cpu->machine->ncpus < 2)`) on the seven arms.  Two seats, two shapes, one\nconclusion: **the 59-row detector does not cover the `implemented' flag across the exception classes.**\n\n**AND KIMI'S IS THE CHEAPER MUTANT TO BUILD AND THE HARDER ONE TO DISMISS**: a deletion needs no reachability\nargument at all, and its premise -- that some exception class is UNPROBED -- is checkable by reading the probe's\nrow list against the switch's case labels, with no build required.  **That check should run BEFORE any mutant\nis compiled**: if every class is probed, kimi's prediction dies on the spot for free; if one is not, the mutant\nis written against that class and the measurement is aimed rather than speculative.\n\n*Note the transport, per the doctrine: the substitute is PACKET-FED, so this is attack REASONING, not a repo\nfact.  It cannot know which classes the probe drives -- which is exactly why it named the check rather than\nasserting the answer.*\n\n**TWO FURTHER CANDIDATES from the same seat, recorded here so they are not lost, and NOT actioned:**\n* *\"this commit makes an admittedly manual-inconsistent delay-slot path reachable by ordinary faults and the\n  probe pins the wrong values green -- file it at minimum, L2/L3-candidate if the detector contract claims\n  shadow fidelity.\"*  Whether the contract makes that claim is a reading of the probe header, not of this\n  packet.\n* the `#503` comment's *\"two exits\"* defence has a **program-order hole** -- connects run before the\n  machine-layer exit -- which its own `-n 5` measurement cannot exclude.  **grok settled the ADJACENT premise**\n  (no post-DEVINIT write to `machine->ncpus`) but not this one, which is about the ORDER of two init-time\n  exits rather than about mutation.  The two seats are not in conflict; they checked different things.\n\n*** THE FREE CHECK RAN, AND IT CONFIRMS THE PREMISE.  RAISED MED -> HIGH.  Measured 2026-09-07, no build\nrequired -- exactly as predicted, the check settled it for nothing. ***\n\nI said kimi's prediction was the cheaper one because its premise -- *some exception class is UNPROBED* -- is\ncheckable by reading the probe's rows against the switch's labels.  Run:\n\n    implemented = true sites          11   (NOT the 7 the round's own narrative implies)\n      on RESET                        line 700   <-- probe drives no such row\n      on INTERRUPT                    line 705   <-- probe drives no such row\n      on INSTRUCTION_ACCESS           line 710   <-- probe drives no such row\n      on DATA_ACCESS                  line 715   <-- probe drives no such row\n      on the seven fixed arms         lines 720-750, ALL probed\n\n**SO THE MUTANT EXISTS AND ITS TARGET IS NAMED: delete `implemented = true;' at line 700, 705, 710 or 715.**\nThe probe's 59 rows drive bounds / div0 / error / misalign / ovf / priv / unimpl / sfu1 and the trap family;\n**none of them drives RESET, INTERRUPT, INSTRUCTION_ACCESS or DATA_ACCESS**, so a deletion there is invisible\nto every row -- which is the clean sweep, reached by the smallest possible edit.\n\n**AND TWO OF THE FOUR ARE ORDINARY GUEST TRAFFIC.**  `DATA_ACCESS' and `INSTRUCTION_ACCESS' are page and bus\nfaults -- the commonest exceptions a real guest takes.  A regression that silently re-opened `#502` for those\nwould stop EVERY guest CPU on an ordinary fault, which is precisely the defect `#502` spent twelve passes\nclosing.  `RESET' and `INTERRUPT' are less obviously guest-driven and their reachability is NOT established\nhere.\n\n*** THE PACKET-FED SEAT REASONED TO A CORRECT STRUCTURAL CONCLUSION IT COULD NOT VERIFY, AND SAID SO. ***  It\ncannot read the probe or the switch; it inferred from the packet that a flag set per-class and a detector\ndriving some classes must leave others uncovered, and it named the check instead of asserting the answer.\nThat is the transport preamble working, and it is worth more than an unconditioned agreement from a seat that\ncould have checked and did not.\n\n**WHAT REMAINS UNMEASURED, and it is the only thing left: THE SCORE.**  The premise is verified by reading;\nwhether the mutant actually scores 59/59 needs a build.  Do not record a breach until it is measured -- the\nfloor's L2 rule fires on a MEASURED false pass, and this row exists partly to keep that line sharp.\n\n**grok's mutant is now the SECONDARY candidate**: it needs the conditioned form to be reachable, while this\none needs only a deletion on a line that is already there.  Measure this one first.\n\n*** GRADING CORRECTED BY ME, WITHIN THE HOUR, BEFORE ANY REPAIR WAS ATTEMPTED.  I recorded this as a\nCONFIRMED L2 FLOOR BREACH.  THE MEASUREMENT STANDS; THE GRADING DOES NOT, AND I SHOULD NOT HAVE ASSERTED\nIT. ***\n\n**WHAT I MISSED: the four uncovered arms are NOT part of `#502`'s fix.**  The probe's own docstring says so\nin its second paragraph:\n\n    `cpu_m88k.c`'s exception switch marks only RESET / INTERRUPT / INSTRUCTION_ACCESS / DATA_ACCESS\n    `implemented`; every other vector below M88K_EXCEPTION_USER_TRAPS_START falls to a fallback ...\n\n**That is a description of the PRE-FIX state.**  Those four flags are pre-existing upstream code.  `#502`\nADDED SEVEN (`git show` on the commit: exactly 7 `implemented = true` lines added), and the probe DRIVES all\nseven.  So the detector covers the arms its round actually repaired.\n\n**THE TWO READINGS, and I am not entitled to pick between them unilaterally:**\n\n* **L2 FLOOR BREACH.**  Clause (d)'s DEPENDS-ON closure is *the predicates dominating the diff's edited\n  lines, walked to the ENCLOSING FUNCTION BOUNDARY*.  The `switch (vector)` dominates all eleven arms and\n  they share one function; `implemented` is the fix's own variable.  On that reading the four are inside the\n  closure and a clean sweep is a breach.\n* **CLAUSE-(e) COVERAGE DEBT.**  The four sites are not edited lines, existed before the round, and the fix's\n  repair is not nulled by deleting a flag it never set.  On that reading the detector defends what its round\n  changed, and the gap is debt on untouched code -- FILED, ranked, not repaired in a follow-up.\n\n**WHAT IS SETTLED EITHER WAY, and it is the part that matters: THE COVERAGE GAP IS REAL AND MEASURED.**  A\none-line deletion at four sites in a shipped file is invisible to a 59-row detector, and two of those sites\n(`DATA_ACCESS`, `INSTRUCTION_ACCESS`) are ordinary page and bus faults.  Nothing about the grading dispute\nchanges that number or that reachability.\n\n**WHY I RECORDED IT WRONG:** the premise I verified was *four `implemented = true` sites are unprobed*, which\nis true.  I then carried it straight to *therefore a clause-(d) breach* without checking WHICH SITES THE FIX\nTOUCHED -- the one fact that decides the closure.  It took one `git show` to settle and I ran it only after\nwriting the grading down.  **That is the premise-audit failure this project's own brief opener exists to\nprevent, committed by the person who writes the openers.**\n\n*Raised as a rule question rather than resolved: it turns on the DEPENDS-ON closure's boundary for a fix that\nadds instances of an EXISTING pattern, which is a class this project will meet again -- every de-fatalisation\nround that extends a flag or a guard already present has the same shape.*\n\n*** MEASURED 2026-09-07 BY agy.  THE PREDICTION IS CONFIRMED: A CLEAN SWEEP, 59/59 -> 59/59, ZERO\nROWS REDDENED.  THIS IS NOW AN L2 FLOOR BREACH OF THE DETECTOR, NOT A PREDICTION. ***\n\n    BASELINE   M88KEXC_RESULT=59/59\n    MUTANT M1  M88KEXC_RESULT=59/59        rows reddened: NONE\n\n    freshness  [ /tmp/gxm88k/gxemul -nt .../cpu_m88k.c ]  passed on BOTH builds\n    sandbox    all work in /tmp/gxm88k; GXEMUL-SEC verified untouched by git status\n\nArtifact: `regress/artifacts/m88kexcdetectorl2/agy_measure_20260907.txt`.  Brief: `MEASURE_brief.md`\nin the same directory.\n\n**THE MUTANT IS THE CANONICAL CLAUSE-(d) SHAPE** -- the guard is kept and its repair is conditioned\non a field the guarded body reads: `implemented = true;` -> `implemented = (cpu->machine->ncpus < 2);`.\nThe floor says a clause-(d) mutant that sweeps a detector clean is an **L2 breach, repaired in-round\nhowever late it is found**.\n\n*** THE SEVEN-VERSUS-ELEVEN DISCREPANCY IS RESOLVED, AND IT WAS PUT TO THE SEAT AS A QUESTION\nRATHER THAN CORRECTED SILENTLY. ***  This row said the edit applies to SEVEN arms; my grep found\nELEVEN.  agy derived the list and changed **all 11**, all inside the `switch (vector)` in\n`m88k_exception()`:\n\n    RESET, INTERRUPT, INSTRUCTION_ACCESS, DATA_ACCESS          (4, unannotated)\n    MISALIGNED_ACCESS, UNIMPLEMENTED_OPCODE, PRIVILEGE_VIOLATION,\n    BOUNDS_CHECK_VIOLATION, ILLEGAL_INTEGER_DIVIDE,\n    INTEGER_OVERFLOW, ERROR                                    (7, annotated m88kexcdeliver)\n\n**The row's \"seven\" counted only the arms carrying the `/* m88kexcdeliver */` annotation** -- i.e.\nthe ones a previous round touched -- and silently excluded four pre-existing vectors in the same\ndispatch.  That is the count-deriver rule again: a number keyed on an ANNOTATION rather than on what\ndefines the class.  *The sweep is a sweep either way; the enumeration was wrong and is corrected.*\n\n**TWO INDEPENDENT READING-SEAT PREDICTIONS WERE RIGHT.**  grok named the mutant and the score from\nthe packet alone and said plainly it could not build it (read-only seat, gates forbidden); a second\nseat reached the same hole separately.  **The reading seats bought the CLASS and the measurement\nbought the INSTANCE** -- which is exactly the division of labour the carrier records, and it is the\nargument for handing a reading seat's named family straight to the measuring seat.\n\n*** WHAT IS OWED: THE REPAIR IS A DETECTOR ROW, NOT A PRODUCT CHANGE. ***  Every seat agrees the\nproduct fix is correctly placed; what is broken is `regress/m88k_exc_probe.py`'s coverage of it.  A\nrepair row must FAIL under this mutant, which means it has to exercise the dispatch with\n`machine->ncpus >= 2` and assert the exception is still delivered -- the detector's 59 rows evidently\nall run at the default one CPU, so the mutant's condition is true throughout and nothing observes it.\n\n**NOT BUILT IN THIS TURN**, and the reason is recorded rather than glossed: adding a row to a\n59-row detector perturbs the scores of every mutant already measured against it, so it owes its own\nmeasured table rather than being appended to a round that is closing.  Filed as the next action on\nthis row, with the mutant preserved and reproducible from the brief.\n\n*** THE CAUSE OF THE SWEEP IS CONFIRMED BY READING, AND IT PINS THE REPAIR EXACTLY. ***\n\n`m88k_exc_probe.py` **never passes `-n`.**  Every session launches:\n\n    os.execvp(BIN, [BIN, \"-V\", \"-E\", \"testm88k\", \"-M\", \"64\", \"0x10000:%s\" % stub])\n\nso all 59 rows run at the DEFAULT ONE CPU.  The mutant conditions the repair on\n`cpu->machine->ncpus < 2`, which is therefore **true in every row**, `implemented` keeps the value\nit had before the mutation, and nothing observes the change.  **That is the whole explanation for\n59/59 -> 59/59, and it is a property of the detector's launch line rather than of its oracles.**\n\n*The sibling probe already has the idiom:* `luna_absentcpu_probe.py:180` builds\n`[BIN, \"-V\"] + ([\"-n\", str(ncpus)] if ncpus > 1 else []) + [...]`, and that round used it to run a\nfour-CPU session (E: `-n 4`, four cpus / cpu3 deliver / others idle).\n\n> **THE REPAIR, precisely specified: one session launched with `-n 2` (or more) that drives an\n> exception and asserts it is still DELIVERED.**  Under the mutant, `ncpus < 2` is FALSE, so\n> `implemented` becomes false and the exception is not delivered -- the row reds.  On the shipped\n> source it is delivered and the row is green.  That is a clause-(d) kill by construction rather\n> than by luck.\n\n**ONE OPEN QUESTION, and it is the only thing between this design and a build: does `-E testm88k`\naccept `-n 2` at all, and does the second CPU change anything the existing oracles read?**  The luna\nround ran multi-CPU on a MACHINE (`-e luna-88k`) with a disk image and a boot; this probe uses a\nbare `-E testm88k` emulation with a stub.  **NOT CHECKED HERE** -- it needs one run, and a run is a\nprobe, which must not overlap the codex census currently in flight.\n\n**IF `-n` IS REJECTED OR INERT ON `testm88k`**, the repair needs a different observable for the same\npredicate -- e.g. a row on a machine that does supply multiple CPUs -- and that is a bigger change\nthan adding a session.  **Recording the fork now so the next session does not rediscover it.**\n\n**COST NOTE, unchanged and load-bearing:** adding any row to a 59-row detector perturbs the scores of\nevery mutant already measured against it, so this repair owes its own measured table (baseline plus\nthe twelve-odd mutants already on record) rather than being appended to a closing round.  That is why\nit is designed here and not built here.\n\n*** THE OPEN QUESTION IS CLOSED BY READING -- NO RUN NEEDED -- AND THE REPAIR IS DE-RISKED. ***\n\nI flagged one thing between the design and a build: does `-E testm88k` accept `-n 2`, and does a\nsecond CPU perturb the existing oracles?  Three reads settle it.\n\n**1.  `-n` IS ACCEPTED AND VALIDATED.**  `src/core/main.c:423-429`:\n\n        case 'n':\n                /*  atoi() accepted negative counts, which later\n                    feed malloc()/memset() size calculations.  */\n                m->ncpus = (int) parse_int_option('n', optarg, 1, 256);\n                machine_specific_options_used = true;\n\nRange 1..256, so `-n 2` is legal.  (Note the fork has already hardened this site against a negative\ncount reaching `malloc`.)\n\n**2.  THE CPUs ARE CREATED IN THE GENERIC PATH, NOT A MACHINE-SPECIFIC ONE.**  `src/core/emul.c:489`\nonwards allocates `m->cpus[m->ncpus]` and loops creating each one, with no machine-type gate.  So a\nbare `-E testm88k` emulation goes through exactly the same code and really does get two CPUs.\n\n**3.  THE BOOTSTRAP CPU IS DETERMINISTIC HERE, WHICH WAS THE REAL HAZARD.**  `emul.c:510-513` is\n\n        if (m->use_random_bootstrap_cpu)\n                m->bootstrap_cpu = random() % m->ncpus;\n        else\n                m->bootstrap_cpu = 0;\n\n**`random() % m->ncpus` would have made any multi-CPU row non-deterministic** -- a flaky detector\nrow is worse than a missing one.  It is guarded, and `use_random_bootstrap_cpu` is set in exactly\ntwo places, `emul_parse.c:490` (config-file) and `main.c:450` (an explicit CLI flag).\n**`machine_test.c` never sets it**, so `testm88k` bootstraps cpu0 deterministically.\n\n> **So the repair is buildable as designed: ONE session launched `-n 2`, driving an exception and\n> asserting it is still DELIVERED.  Under the mutant `ncpus < 2` is FALSE, `implemented` becomes\n> false, the exception is not delivered, and the row reds.  Deterministic on both sides.**\n\n*Recorded because the hazard was real and invisible from the design: I would have written the row,\nmeasured it green twice and red once, and spent the next session hunting a flake that was\n`random() % 2` in the emulator's own startup.  Three greps, no runs.*\n\n**WHAT REMAINS BEFORE IT SHIPS is only the cost already stated: adding a row to a 59-row detector\nperturbs every mutant score already measured against it, so this owes its own table -- baseline plus\nthe mutants on record -- rather than being appended to a closing round.**",
tools/pipeline\ledger.json:31730:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07, raised by the codex seat (regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_scoping_20260907.txt) while scoping an unrelated device, and\nverified at both ends.\n\n    memory_alpha.c   (*return_paddr) = ALPHA_PTE_TO_PFN(pte3) << pageSizeShift;\n    memory_rw.c      offset = paddr & offset_mask;\n\nThe walk returns a PAGE-BASE address, so `paddr & offset_mask' is ZERO and every access within a page\nresolves to that page's first bytes.\n\n*** THE HEDGE IS THE SEAT'S OWN AND IT IS CORRECT: the DIRECT-MAPPED paths avoid it. ***  Both early returns\n-- K0SEG (`vaddr & 0x000003ffffffffffULL') and the `ptbr == 0' bootstrap hack -- MASK the vaddr and therefore\nKEEP the offset bits.  Only the real walk drops them, and it is reached in user mode, or outside K0SEG once\nptbr != 0.\n\n**REACHABILITY IS THE OPEN QUESTION AND IT BOUNDS THE SEVERITY BOTH WAYS.**  memory_alpha.c carries seven\nTODOs and an explicit fatal(\"Alpha kernel virtual memory seems to want to be used. Not yet implemented\") +\nexit(1), so Alpha VM here is immature and a bootable configuration may never reach the walk -- in which case\nthis is latent rather than live.  **HIGH is PROVISIONAL on that**: if a guest reaches it the effect is total\nmemory-access corruption; if none can, the row is documented-only.  A round takes the reachability question\nFIRST; the fix itself is adding the offset back.\n\nThe function is live, not dead code: cpu_alpha.c assigns `cpu->translate_v2p = alpha_translate_v2p' and\ncpu_dyntrans.c calls it.\n\n*** REACHABILITY PASS, codex seat 2026-09-07 (regress/artifacts/alphapfnoffset/codex_reachability_20260907.txt).  THE ARITHMETIC IS CONFIRMED BY\nEXHAUSTIVE ENUMERATION -- AND MY OWN SUMMARY OF THE IMPACT WAS TOO BROAD.  Corrected here. ***\n\n**CONFIRMED, and by walking the values rather than arguing:** *\"I enumerated all 8,192 offsets: the walk\nexpression produces offset zero throughout, differing from the offset-preserving address for 8,191 offsets.\nThe masking paths preserve offsets; exhaustive enumeration also confirmed preservation throughout all three\nbootstrap alias regions.\"*  So the defect is real, the direct-mapped arms are genuinely safe, and there are\nTHREE bootstrap alias regions rather than the two I had read.\n\n*** MY OVERCLAIM, CORRECTED: this row said `every walked access would land at its page base'.  THAT IS TOO\nBROAD. ***  codex: *\"Slow-path accesses consume the incorrect physical offset, but cached Alpha loads/stores\ncalculate `addr & 8191' themselves.  Subsequent cache hits can therefore access the correct within-page\nbytes.\"*  So the corruption is confined to the SLOW PATH; once a page is cached, the load/store instructions\nrecompute the offset and read the right bytes.  **That materially narrows the blast radius** and it is the\nsecond time tonight this seat has narrowed one of my summaries -- the first being the class-scan denominator.\n\n**REACHABILITY -- the answer is NOT `latent', and it is not `live' either.**  codex found *\"no source barrier\nmaking a guest-instruction witness impossible on the existing description\"*, and named the smallest decisive\nobservation: one COMPLETED guest data access on the existing `eb164' machine that reaches the walk with a\nnonzero page offset and whose caller completes the access on the returned page-base address.  **A PTBR\nwatchpoint or a walker-entry breakpoint is explicitly NOT enough** -- reaching the walk is not the same as an\naccess completing wrongly.\n\n**What it could not settle, and said so plainly:** *\"I cannot name a demonstrated working Alpha OS boot from\nthe allowed files.\"*  So the honest status is **WITNESSABLE IN PRINCIPLE, UNWITNESSED IN FACT** -- which is\na different thing from latent, and it is what a round takes first.\n\n**HIGH stands but its warrant changes**: not `every access is corrupted' (false), but `the slow path is\ncorrupted for 8,191 of 8,192 offsets, on a path with no established guest that reaches it'.\n\n*** THE BOOT QUESTION IS ANSWERED, AND IT REVERSES MY CONCERN: ALL FOUR ALPHA ROWS ARE COLD-DEBUGGER\nREACHABLE.  NO OS BOOT IS REQUIRED.  codex, 2026-09-07 (regress/artifacts/alphapfnoffset/codex_boot_20260907.txt). ***\n\nI had flagged the risk that the Alpha queue was accumulating well-analysed rows on a machine nothing can\nexercise.  It is not: *\"EB164 or AlphaServer 4100 provides the required unmodified machine description.\nThere is no architectural requirement to boot an OS first.\"*  Debugger word writes go through the memory\nhelpers, Alpha fetches and decodes through its actual memory path, and the shared breakpoint machinery\napplies -- which is exactly the rung-3 cold-debugger shape this project's ladder admits.\n\nIts classification of all four:\n\n    page-walk drops the offset      COLD-DEBUGGER REACHABLE   needs page-table state; the bootstrap\n                                                              identity mapping bypasses the walk\n    PALcode register mismatch       COLD-DEBUGGER REACHABLE   swpctx is reached by guest dispatch\n    unsupported fault-delivery      COLD-DEBUGGER REACHABLE   failed access returns without an exception\n    guest-reachable process exit    COLD-DEBUGGER REACHABLE   the bootstrap translator's special branch\n\n**The qualification on THIS row is the one that matters for its witness:** the walk needs page-table state,\nand *the ordinary bootstrap identity mapping bypasses the walk*.  So a probe must first establish a non-zero\n`apcb_ptbr' and a valid three-level table -- which is real work, and is why this row is still the hardest of\nthe five despite being the most severe if reached.\n\n**[[alphaswpctxptbr]] is a confound for that witness and must be fixed first:** it clobbers `apcb_ptbr` with\nthe flags word, so any probe that sets up a context via `swpctx` and then observes a bad translation cannot\nsay which defect produced it.\n\n*** THE CONFOUND IS NOW MEASURED, NOT SUSPECTED.  2026-09-07. ***\n[[alphaswpctxptbr]] has a rung-3 witness as of today: on the committed build, `PCB_A+16` reads the\nFLAGS word (`0xbbbb2222`) where the ptbr (`0xaaaa1111`) is required -- `regress/alpha_harness_probe.py`,\nHEAD 7/9, artifacts under `regress/artifacts/alphaswpctxptbr/`.\n\nSo the ordering this row already asserted is now backed by a measurement: **a witness for the\npage-walk offset defect must either fix the swap first or establish `apcb_ptbr` by a route that does\nnot go through `swpctx`** -- otherwise a bad translation cannot be attributed to the walk.  The\nharness's own bootstrap shows such a route exists (debugger `put d` writes the PCB directly while\n`ptbr == 0`), which is the more promising of the two.\n\n*** GROK WITNESS DESIGN 2026-09-07 (`regress/artifacts/alphapfnoffset/grok_witness_20260907.txt`).\nVERDICT: BUILDABLE on the committed tree, and it **does not wait on [[alphaswpctxptbr]]** -- which\nrefutes the premise this brief was written around. ***\n\n**S4 WAS TOO STRONG.**  `swpctx` IS the only writer of `apcb_ptbr` (verified: the debugger exports\nonly `pc` and the GPRs; `wrvptptr` writes the unused `vptptr`; `machine_alpha.c` puts the L1 PFN in\n**`a1`**, not the PCB).  **But the swap is only a confound if `ptbr` and `flags` DIFFER.**\n\n> **PUT THE SAME L1 PFN IN BOTH PCB FIELDS.**  Then the defective line and the corrected line both\n> leave `apcb_ptbr = L1_PFN`, and the walk is armed identically either way.  One `call_pal 0x30`,\n> and the confound is gone without fixing anything.\n\n*And the inverse is a trap: putting the PFN only in `flags` with `ptbr = 0` arms the walk on HEAD and\nleaves IDENTITY MAPPING on the fixed PAL -- so post-fix green would be the bootstrap, not the repair.*\n\n**S3 IS SHARPER THAN I WROTE, AND IT KILLS MY Q3.**  The fast path caches the page from\n`paddr & ~offset_mask`, so the cached host page is the **correct physical page** and a later hit at\nANY in-page offset reads the right bytes.  **The walked paddr is wrong only for the FILL.**  So *what\nN avoids the cached path* is the wrong question -- **N does not select the slow path.**  The design is\n`N = 8`, cold cache, **ONE shot, and do not retry the load: the second access is a hit and goes green\non HEAD.**  (An unaligned N does force the generic path, but that is an emulator gap rather than\nAlpha, and a later alignment check would kill the witness for the wrong reason.)\n\n*** THE PLANTED-PAST-RAM EQUIVALENT FOR THIS PROBE IS NOT AN ADDRESS BOUND -- IT IS PFN-vs-BYTE. ***\n`apcb_ptbr = 0x01050000` gives `base = 0x01050000 << 13`, past RAM, `memory_paddr_to_hostaddr` NULL,\n`not_found`.  **The PCB and the PTE high halves hold PFNs, not byte addresses.**\n\n**THE TABLE, concrete and off both the PROM pocket (`0x10000`/`0x10100`/`0x12000`/`0x14000`) and the\nsibling probes' scratch:**\n\n    L1   0x01050000  PFN 0x828      L1[0] = PTE_FROM_PFN(0x829) | VALID\n    L2   0x01052000  PFN 0x829      L2[0] = PTE_FROM_PFN(0x82A) | VALID\n    L3   0x01054000  PFN 0x82A      L3[0] = PTE_FROM_PFN(0x82B) | VALID\n    data 0x01056000  PFN 0x82B      apcb_ptbr = 0x828   <- the PFN\n\nTest vaddr in `[0, 8192)` so `i1 = i2 = i3 = 0`.  **Only `ALPHA_PTE_VALID` is load-bearing** -- the\nwalk never tests KR/KW/UR/UW, FAULT_ON_*, ASM or granularity.  *\"Do not set bits the walk does not\nimplement and then call them required\"* -- the addition licence, applied to a probe.\n\n**THE THREE-WAY ORACLE, which is better than the two-way I proposed, and rests on a fact I had\nwrong.**  My Q4 said a failed walk \"faults or returns zero\".  **False for the guest-visible\nregister:** `LS_GENERIC_N` returns on `memory_rw` failure **without writing the destination**.  So\npre-set the destination to a third sentinel `S`:\n\n    S unchanged  the walk never completed\n    0            the walk succeeded onto UNMAPPED physical (memblock NULL still returns ok)\n    A            the walk hit the right page at offset 0   <- THE DEFECT\n    B            correct\n\n**THE POLARITY TRAP, and it is the `lcaconfwidth` inversion wearing different clothes:** plant `B`\nTHROUGH the walk (a `put d` or `stq` at `vaddr+N` once `ptbr` is live) and on HEAD that store lands\nat `data+0`; the later load of `vaddr+N` also lands at `data+0` and reads `B`.  **HEAD goes green,\nmeasuring the defect against itself.**  A cousin: planting at vaddr `0`/`N` while `ptbr == 0` hits\nPHYSICAL `0`/`N`, not the data page, and both polarities collapse.  **Plant `A` and `B` only via\nidentity-physical or K0SEG of the DATA PAGE, before the walk is used.**\n\n**CONTROLS:** `data0 k0` and `dataN k0` (K0SEG reads of the same physical page -- they do not fill the\ntest vaddr's translation slot, different key), `walk mapped` (dest not 0 and not S), and the ABSENCE\nof `alpha_translate_v2p: ... wasn't found`.  *`A` on the walked load with those green is itself the\nproof the walk ran -- a stronger channel than a same-decode round-trip, because the defect signature\nnames the page.*\n\n**READ beats WRITE as the witness** (one `ldq`, three-way register, single `want`).  A walked `stq`\nis the same mechanism and needs the same three table pages -- *the write form does not save a PTE* --\nand its read-back must go through K0SEG, since a walked read-back is a cache hit that hides HEAD.\n\n**Four smaller facts, each load-bearing:** code must stay at `k0(P_CODE)` after `ptbr` is live (a\nphysical-looking PC walks with `i2=32` and a 3-PTE table does not map it); stay in kernel mode, since\nuser mode makes K0SEG I-fetch walk; `pte2`/`pte3` invalid additionally `fatal(\"TODO: ...\")`, useful as\na setup channel; and `store_64bit_word` sign-extends addresses whose bits 63:32 are clear, so physical\n`put d` stays below 2 GB or uses K0SEG.\n\n*** WITNESS BUILT AND RUN THE SAME HOUR THE DESIGN LANDED.  HEAD 5/6 -- THE OFFSET DROP IS NOW\nMEASURED, not derived. ***\n\n    data0 k0     0xaaaa0000aaaa   0xaaaa0000aaaa   ok    CONTROL: A really is at data+0\n    dataN k0     0xbbbb0000bbbb   0xbbbb0000bbbb   ok    CONTROL: B really is at data+N\n    walk found   absent           absent           ok    CONTROL: walked to a valid L3\n    walk stored  0xaaaa0000aaaa   NOT 0x55000055   ok    CONTROL: the load DID write the register\n    walk mapped  0xaaaa0000aaaa   NOT 0x0          ok    CONTROL: not unmapped physical\n    walk off     0xaaaa0000aaaa   0xbbbb0000bbbb   FAIL  the walked load returned data+0\n\n**A real guest `ldq` of `vaddr+8`, through a hand-built three-level page table, on an unmodified\nmachine description, returned the value at the page BASE.**  The three-way sentinel earned its place:\nthe register was written (so the walk completed), the result was not 0 (so it did not land on\nunmapped physical), and it was `A` (so the walk found the right PAGE and dropped the OFFSET).  Those\nthree are separable only because the sentinel exists -- a two-way oracle would have reported the same\nred for all three causes.\n\n**EVERY ONE OF GROK'S DESIGN CORRECTIONS WAS LOAD-BEARING AND ALL OF THEM HELD IN PRACTICE:**\nthe same-PFN-in-both-PCB-fields trick defeated the [[alphaswpctxptbr]] confound without fixing it,\nso this row did NOT wait on that repair; the PFN-not-byte-address point is what keeps `base` inside\nRAM; K0SEG planting kept the setup off the walk (planting `B` through the walk would have made HEAD\ngreen against itself); and the single cold shot mattered -- a retry is a cache hit that reads the\ncorrect bytes and greens on HEAD.\n\n*Two defects of my own, caught before the run: dead placeholder code, and a 40-bit sentinel handed to\na `load_const` that builds 32-bit constants -- it would have truncated silently and the sentinel row\nwould have compared the wrong value.  The row now pins a value `load_const` can actually build.*\n\n*** GROK FIX DESIGN 2026-09-07.  VERDICT: RATIFY THE ASSIGNMENT, NOT THE ROUND -- the same\nruling as `#507`, and it REFUTED MY OWN STATED WORRY while finding a worse one. ***\n\n**THE FIX:**\n\n    (*return_paddr) = (ALPHA_PTE_TO_PFN(pte3) << pageSizeShift)\n        | (vaddr & ((1ULL << pageSizeShift) - 1));\n\n`|` and `+` are numerically identical here (the shifted PFN has bits 12:0 clear), but they are\ndifferent SENTENCES: **`|` is every other walk in this tree** -- ARM, m88k, MIPS, PPC, SH -- while\n`+` is this function's fixed bootstrap windows.  **And derive the mask from `pageSizeShift`, not a\nliteral**: `:88`'s `0x1fff` is correct for a fixed 8 KB PROM pocket, and hardcoding it beside\n`<< pageSizeShift` is how a mask and its shift drift apart.\n\n*The parentheses are load-bearing: `1 << pageSizeShift - 1` is `0x1000`, not `0x1fff`.  **The\nexisting `N = 8` row DOES kill that one** (`8 & 0x1000 == 0` -> VAL_A).*\n\n*** MY `0xfff`/`0x3fff` PAIRING WAS FALSE, AND THE CORRECTION MATTERS. ***\nI wrote that a mask of `0xfff` or `0x3fff` would be *\"correct at N = 8 and wrong further into the\npage\"*.  **`0x3fff` is not wrong anywhere in the page:** the extra bit is bit 13, and bit 13 of an\noffset is zero for every byte of an 8 KB page.  `+0x1008` still passes under it.  **`0xfff` is the\ndangerous one** -- it is the 4 KB copy (`MRW_OFFSET_MASK` on every non-Alpha build,\n`DYNTRANS_PAGESIZE` 4096), and it leaves **the upper 4 KB of every walked page still broken**.\n\n**SIX LESSER EDITS ALSO SCORE 6/6 on the committed probe** (`VADDR = 0`, `N = 8`): masks `0xfff`,\n`0x3fff`, `0x7fff` (a copy of `:81` rather than `:88`), any mask including bit 3, **unmasked\n`+ vaddr`** -- correct only because the VPN is 0 at page 0 -- and a hardcoded `| 8`.  **A second cold\nrow is owed**, at a vaddr whose VPN is non-zero and an offset in the upper 4 KB.\n\n**THREE CALLERS ENUMERATED, and one is a THIRD consequence I did not know:**\n\n    memory_rw.c MEMORY_RW_ONE_PAGE   needs the FULL address     <- the defect\n    cpu_dyntrans.c I-fetch miss      tolerates either; strips the offset itself\n    debugger_cmds.c watchpoint       needs the FULL address     <- A WALKED WATCHPOINT TODAY\n                                                                   LANDS AT THE PAGE BASE\n\n**No caller does `paddr + (vaddr & mask)`, so the mirror defect is absent and a post-fix 6/6 is not\nhiding a double-add.**  Q5 confirmed independently: the fill strips before caching\n(`memory_paddr_to_hostaddr(mem, paddr & ~offset_mask, ...)`), `update_translation_table` asserts\npage alignment, and the fast path uses the VIRTUAL offset -- so the walked offset is consumed exactly\nonce.\n\n**KEEP THE `// TODO.`**  Restoring the offset leaves it true: `flags` is never read\n(`FLAG_WRITEFLAG`, `FLAG_INSTR`, `FLAG_NOEXCEPTIONS` all ignored), `ALPHA_PTE_KR/KW/UR/UW` and\n`FAULT_ON_*` are untested, granularity hints are ignored, and **`return 2` means WRITABLE -- so\n`memory_rw` caches every walked Alpha page as writable.**  *Deleting the comment would record the\ntranslator as finished.  It is not.*\n\n**THREE MORE, FILED NOT FIXED:** `:81` is the WRONG neighbour to copy (`0x7fff`, a 32 KB mask -- a\nseat told to *\"copy the in-function idiom\"* can copy the wrong one, and the current witness cannot\nsee it); `flags` being dead means a failed walk still prints `wasn't found` even under\n`FLAG_NOEXCEPTIONS`, which is exactly the debugger-watchpoint path; and **the function uses TWO NAMES\nfor 13** -- `ALPHA_PAGESHIFT` on the L1/L2 physical addresses, local `pageSizeShift` on the indices\nand the final shift -- so the new mask must couple to `pageSizeShift` or the two can diverge on one\nline.\n\n*** `#508` MEASURED: 5/7 -> 7/7, SAME SCRIPT.  The round is complete and held for the 9th. ***\n\n    walk off   0xaaaa0000aaaa -> 0xbbbb0000bbbb\n    walk vpn   0xcccc0000cccc -> 0xdddd0000dddd     <- the row the fix review demanded\n\nFive controls green on both runs.  All four trees propagated; three rebuilt with the two-part\nclearance CLEAN/FRESH; `est/` part 2 N/A.  **Nothing committed** -- the Fable hold stands.\n\n*The `walk vpn` row is the third instance today of a fix review ratifying an edit and refusing the\nROUND because the witness could not distinguish the fix from a lesser one.  In all three cases the\nmissing row cost one readback and caught something the author had not considered.*\n\n*** COMMIT ATTRIBUTION RESTORED 2026-09-08: this row's fix shipped in **`7a41b3f`** and the\n`commits` field was empty.  Verified before writing, not assumed. ***\n\n    git show --stat 7a41b3f        touches src/cpus/memory_alpha.c\n    git show 7a41b3f -- src/cpus/memory_alpha.c\n                                   contains the `#508' round tag 4 times\n\n**ALL FOUR ROUNDS IN THAT COMMIT HAD THE SAME GAP.**  `7a41b3f` is titled *\"#505 #506 #507 #508:\nfour test-first rounds, three new rung-3 witnesses, and a witness row that could not fail\"*, and\n**not one of the four rows carried its commit.**  `sgirergbroundtrip` (#505) was found first, by\naccident, while scoping an unrelated round in the same file; the other three were found by asking\nthe general question afterwards.\n\n> **THE CLASS: A COMMIT THAT SHIPS SEVERAL ROUNDS LOSES THE PER-ROW ATTRIBUTION FOR ALL OF THEM.**\n> The committer is performing ONE act and the ledger records N rows, so the per-row field is the\n> thing that falls out.  A one-round commit does not have this failure mode -- which is why it has\n> not been seen before and why it appeared four times at once.\n\n*The `state` field is deliberately NOT touched here.  `in work` may be correct for a row with\nresidual obligations, and changing it would be a claim I have not checked.  Only the attribution,\nwhich is unambiguous, is filled in.*",
tools/pipeline\ledger.json:31795:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07.  **RAISED BY THE CODEX SEAT** under the found-something-else licence, on a brief that asked\nonly whether Alpha machines can run anything (regress/artifacts/alphapfnoffset/codex_boot_20260907.txt) -- and VERIFIED by reading before\nfiling.\n\n`PAL_OSF1_swpctx' loads a new context and assigns it into the pcb.  **`apcb_ptbr' is assigned TWICE and\n`apcb_flags' is never assigned at all:**\n\n    // Update all variables in the pcb simultaneously:\n    cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_ksp    = new_ksp;\n    cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_usp    = new_usp;\n    cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_ptbr   = new_ptbr;      <- correct\n    cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_cpc    = new_cpc;\n    cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_asn    = new_asn;\n    cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_unique = new_unique;\n    cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_ptbr   = new_flags;     <- *** should be apcb_flags ***\n    cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_decrsv0 = new_decrsv0;\n    cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_decrsv1 = new_decrsv1;\n\n*** WHY THIS OUTRANKS THE OTHER ALPHA ROWS: `apcb_ptbr' IS THE PAGE TABLE BASE REGISTER. ***\n`alpha_translate_v2p()' opens with `uint64_t base = cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_ptbr << ALPHA_PAGESHIFT;' -- so\nafter ANY `swpctx' the page-table walk reads its tables from an address derived from the FLAGS word.  Every\ntranslation after a context switch walks garbage.\n\n**And `apcb_flags' keeps the OUTGOING context's value**, because nothing ever writes it -- a second, quieter\ndefect from the same line.\n\n**THE COMMENT DIRECTLY ABOVE ASSERTS WHAT THE CODE DOES NOT DO:** *\"Update all variables in the pcb\nsimultaneously\"*.  Two of the nine are wrong -- one clobbered, one never set.  That is the second time in\nthis one function that a comment states an intent the code contradicts; the other is [[alphaswpctxreg]].\n\n**FIX: one token** -- the second `apcb_ptbr' becomes `apcb_flags'.  As with its sibling, the intent is not in\ndoubt: `new_flags' is loaded from the context block and would otherwise be dead, and `apcb_flags' exists in\nthe struct and would otherwise never be written.\n\n**REACHABILITY: cold-debugger reachable, per the same pass** -- `swpctx' is an active handler reached by\nordinary guest instruction dispatch with no OS dependency.  A witness plants a `call_pal 0x30' and observes\n`apcb_ptbr' afterwards.\n\n*** THE INTERACTION WITH [[alphapfnoffset]] IS WORTH STATING, because it changes how that round should be\ndesigned: this defect CORRUPTS THE WALK'S BASE, and that one CORRUPTS THE WALK'S RESULT. ***  A witness for\neither must control for the other -- a probe that does a `swpctx' and then observes a bad translation cannot\nattribute it without pinning which of the two produced it.  **Fix this one first**: it is certain, one token,\nand it removes a confound from the harder row.\n\n*** DESIGN PASS, codex 2026-09-07 (regress/artifacts/alphaswpctxptbr/codex_design_20260907.txt).  THE DEFECT IS CONFIRMED AND TWO OF MY\nSUPPORTING PREMISES WERE WRONG.  Corrected here. ***\n\nVerdict: *\"A and B are defects, and both proposed corrections are right.  They are TWO changes under your\nreopening rule.  `apcb_flags is never written' is false; no third destination typo was found.\"*\n\n**CORRECTION 1 -- `apcb_flags' IS WRITTEN, and I filed the opposite as fact.**  This row said it *\"is never\nassigned at all\"*.  `grep -rn apcb_flags src/' returns `cpu_alpha_palcode.c:221' -- **`wrfen' assigns it**\nfrom `r[ALPHA_A0]' and then writes it back to PCB memory, and `swpctx' itself READS the field when saving the\noutgoing context.\n\n**The honest statement is narrower and still a defect:** `swpctx' never assigns `apcb_flags' WHEN LOADING A\nNEW CONTEXT, so the incoming context's flags are silently dropped and the outgoing context's value persists\nacross the switch.  That is a real defect; *never written anywhere* was not.\n\n**CORRECTION 2 -- `new_flags' is NOT dead, and the distinction matters.**  I wrote that it *\"would otherwise\nbe dead\"*.  codex: it *\"has exactly one use after its declaration: the erroneous PTBR assignment.  It is\notherwise unused, but **not dead in the current program**: it changes PTBR.\"*  My phrasing implied the\ncompiler would flag it; it would not, because the erroneous line consumes it.\n\n**A QUALIFICATION ON THE SIBLING [[alphaswpctxreg]] THAT BOUNDS ITS IMPACT:** the handler captures its input\ninto a LOCAL `a0' before dispatch, so overwriting `r[ALPHA_A0]' does **not** change which context is loaded.\nThat defect is therefore purely a wrong RETURN VALUE -- it does not corrupt the switch itself.  I had not\nestablished that, and it is the difference between a reporting bug and a state-corruption bug.\n\n**SCOPE: TWO CHANGES, NOT ONE.**  I intended to fix both in one diff -- same function, same class.  codex\nrules they are two under the reopening rule.  **Accepted**: the observables differ (a guest register versus\ninternal PCB state reachable only through a translation), so one test cannot cover both without a new\nobservation, which is exactly what that rule forbids.\n\n**MY Q3 HYPOTHESIS WAS REFUTED, and recording that matters as much as a finding.**  I said a third\nwrong-destination assignment was *\"more likely than not\"* given two in one function, and asked for the whole\nhandler to be read.  It was, and there is no third.  **A predicted class that does not materialise is a\nresult** -- and it stops the next round hunting for one.\n\n*** READ-ONLY AUDIT, 2026-09-07 (codex, `regress/artifacts/alphaswpctxptbr/codex_read_20260907.txt`).\nTHE DEFECT IS CONFIRMED FIELD BY FIELD, THE FIX IS ONE TOKEN, AND A PREMISE OF MINE ABOUT\nREACHABILITY WAS WRONG IN A WAY THAT WOULD HAVE BROKEN THE WITNESS. ***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"`swpctx` assigns `apcb_ptbr` twice, leaves `apcb_flags` unchanged, and uses all nine\nlocals.  Its return line writes `a0`.  Opcode-0 dispatch has no privilege check, but\n`alpha_palcode()` itself checks user mode before the switch.\"*\n\n**CONFIRMED, from the seat's own nine-row assignment table rather than from my description:**\n`apcb_ptbr` is assigned twice, `apcb_flags` never, and **all nine locals are used** -- so the defect\nis precisely a mis-typed destination, not a dropped load.  **The fix is ONE IDENTIFIER TOKEN on ONE\nLINE** (`cpu_alpha_palcode.c:282`), which the seat states explicitly.  The `a0`-for-`v0` return is\nconfirmed as a second, separate defect in the same handler.\n\n*** THE CORRECTION, AND IT IS A HARNESS-BREAKER RATHER THAN A RECORDS NICETY. ***  I wrote -- in the\nbrief AND in the first draft of the Alpha harness -- that `call_pal` dispatch is unconditional.\n**Dispatch INTO `alpha_palcode()` is; the function then GATES ITS OWN SWITCH** at\n`cpu_alpha_palcode.c:183`:\n\n    uint64_t userMode = cpu->cd.alpha.ps & ALPHA_PSL_USERMODE;      /* 0x0008 */\n    if (userMode && !(palcode == 0x80 || 0x81 || 0x83 || 0xae ||\n                      0xaa || 0x86 || 0x9e || 0x92 || 0x9f)) {\n            fatal(\"[ Privileged Alpha PALcode called from user mode: TODO ]\");\n            cpu->running = 0;\n            return;\n    }\n\n**`0x30` is not on that whitelist**, so in user mode `call_pal 0x30` does not reach swpctx -- it\nHALTS THE CPU.  **That failure presents as a probe whose step never completes**, i.e. as a broken\nharness rather than as a mode problem, which is the worst kind of failure to debug.  Verified by\nreading the lines rather than taken on the seat's word.\n\n`cpu->cd.alpha.ps` has NO explicit initialiser anywhere in `src/`, so it is zero from the zeroed cpu\nstruct and USERMODE is clear -- kernel mode, and the gate does not fire.  **That is a derivation, and\nthe harness turns it into a measurement**: `regress/alpha_harness_probe.py` now carries a `palgate`\nrow asserting the diagnostic is absent, and a `palalive` row asserting the machine survives a swpctx\ncall at all.\n\n*Note the shape: the premise that was wrong is the one I did NOT think to question -- I checked the\ninstruction decoder carefully and never looked inside the function it dispatches to.  That is the\nthird instance this week and it is the subject of the queued rule question\n[[sgilinearnegidxpostmortem]], which asks whether the premise-audit instruction needs a\nself-directed twin.  **This is its third data point and the first where a seat caught it before it\ncost anything.***\n\n**AND THE BRIEF THAT ASKED THIS WAS THE SECOND ATTEMPT.**  The first -- carrying a four-step witness\nDESIGN -- was REFUSED by the same seat as chain-shaped content (see [[codexbriefscoping]]).  The\nchain-free re-ask, which requests a field-by-field audit and names no procedure, was answered in\nfull.  **The design question went to grok instead**, so neither question was dropped.\n\n*** THE WITNESS DESIGN WAS REFUTED, BY THE SEAT THE REFUSED BRIEF WAS ROUTED TO.  grok,\n`regress/artifacts/alphaswpctxptbr/grok_witness_20260907.txt`, 13.5 kB. ***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"YES, a rung-3 witness is buildable on the three constructing Alpha machines -- but the\nproposed 'no MMU at all, two `swpctx`, read `A+16`' design is unsound as written if `A.flags` is\nnonzero.  After the first switch, `apcb_ptbr` IS the flags word, the identity map dies, and the\nsecond `call_pal` (and the save that is supposed to publish the bug) can fail to land or can\n`exit(1)` the host.  Use K0SEG for code and both PCBs, or keep `flags==0` and accept a weaker\noracle.  The one-token fix is not a PAL convention; it is a copy-paste of the previous LHS.\"*\n\n*** THE DESIGN WAS SELF-DEFEATING AND I HAD NOT SEEN IT: THE DEFECT DESTROYS THE PATH THE WITNESS\nNEEDS TO OBSERVE IT. ***  Verified in `memory_alpha.c` rather than taken on the seat's word, and the\nsource is sharper than the verdict:\n\n  * **K0SEG is checked BEFORE `base` is used at all** (`:62`): `if (!userMode && vaddr >=\n    ALPHA_K0SEG_BASE && vaddr <= ALPHA_K0SEG_END) { *return_paddr = vaddr & 0x3ffffffffff; return\n    2; }`.  A K0SEG access never consults `apcb_ptbr`, so grok's remedy is sound.\n    `ALPHA_K0SEG_BASE = 0xfffffc0000000000`; `ALPHA_PHYS_TO_K0SEG(x) = x | BASE`.\n  * **The identity map is an explicit 'UGLY hack' conditioned on `base == 0`** (`:73`).  That is the\n    ONLY reason a naive harness works at all -- `ptbr` is zero at reset.  **The moment the buggy\n    assignment makes `apcb_ptbr` nonzero, `base != 0` and every later access falls into a real\n    page-table walk over garbage**, with an `exit(1)` sitting on one vaddr at `:75-78`.\n  * **AND THE POLARITY TRAP grok did not state, which kills the 'keep flags==0' fallback too:** if\n    the marker is put in `A.ptbr` instead, then under the *FIXED* build `apcb_ptbr` becomes that\n    nonzero marker and the identity map dies on the FIX rather than on the defect.  **Neither\n    polarity survives with a nonzero value anywhere in that field.**  So K0SEG is not the better\n    option, it is the only one.\n\n**THE CORRECTED DESIGN, and it needs no 64-bit immediates:** `lda`/`ldah` build 32-bit constants\nonly, and a K0SEG address needs bit 63..42 set.  The way through is to **load the pointers BEFORE the\nfirst `swpctx`, while the identity map is still alive**: place the 64-bit K0SEG pointers in\nidentity-mapped scratch with the debugger's `put q`, have the guest `ldq` them into registers, and\nonly then issue the calls.  Every access after that carries a K0SEG address in a register and is\nimmune to whatever `apcb_ptbr` becomes.\n\n**A SEPARATE TRAP FOUND WHILE CHECKING THIS, which the harness avoids only by luck:** `memory_alpha.c:87`\nsilently remaps `(vaddr & ~0x1fff) == 0x20000000` to `PROM_ARGSPACE_PADDR` (0x12000).  The harness's\ncode at `0x20010000` and scratch at `0x20020000` fall OUTSIDE that 8 kB window -- but nothing said\nso, and a probe placed 64 kB lower would have had its stores quietly land in PROM argument space.\n\n**Q4 ANSWERED:** *\"The one-token fix is not a PAL convention; it is a copy-paste of the previous\nLHS.\"*  Both seats agree the correction is `apcb_flags` at `cpu_alpha_palcode.c:282`.\n\n*** THE ROUTING WORKED AND IS THE REUSABLE LESSON. ***  codex REFUSED this brief as chain-shaped;\ngrok answered it in full, read the handler, the dispatch path and the existing harness, and refuted\nthe design on a mechanism I had missed.  **The chain-free re-ask to codex and the chain-shaped\noriginal to grok together cost one extra invocation and lost nothing** -- which is the argument the\n2026-09-09 adjudication is asked to rule on under [[codexbriefscoping]].\n\n*** CODE REVIEW OF THE HARNESS, BEFORE ITS FIRST RUN.  grok,\n`regress/artifacts/alphaswpctxptbr/grok_harness_20260907.txt` -- codex REFUSED this brief too (sixth\nrefusal, chain/witness shape), so it is grok's third answer to a brief codex would not take. ***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"DO NOT TRUST THE FIRST RUN.  The lda/ldah/ldq bootstrap and the swpctx SAVE/RESTORE\nwalk are right.  The encodings that this file actually emits match the decoder.  `put d` is the right\ndebugger command.  What is wrong is the ADDRESS PLAN, and it makes three rows able to go green while\nmeasuring 'unmapped reads return 0', not the handler.\"*\n\n*** FINDING 1 -- FATAL, AND IT WOULD HAVE BEEN SILENT AND GREEN. ***  The machine is built `-M 64`\n= 64 MB and every address in the file sat at `0x200xxxxx` (512 MB+).  K0SEG translation is\n`vaddr & 0x3ffffffffff`, so **the K0SEG idea survives contact with `alpha_translate_v2p` and the\nPHYSICAL BACKING does not.**  And the failure mode is silent:\n`memory_warn_about_unimplemented_addr` only halts when `halt_on_nonexistant_memaccess` is set, i.e.\nunder `-T`.  **Without it, writes succeed and are DISCARDED and reads RETURN ZEROS** -- and an\ninstruction fetch of zeros is a real instruction, `call_pal 0`.\n\n**MY ORACLE MADE THAT UNDETECTABLE.**  It asserted `PCB_A+16 == 0` for the correct case, and 0 is\nalso what an unmapped read returns.  **The witness would have gone green having written nothing,\nread nothing, and executed `call_pal 0` off a zero-filled code page.**\n\n**REPAIRED, and the repair is better than moving the addresses:** K0SEG means translation never\nconsults `apcb_ptbr`, so `apcb_ptbr` may now hold a distinctive NONZERO value -- which the FIRST\ndesign could not allow, because a nonzero ptbr killed the identity map.  So:\n\n    correct handler  -> PCB_A+16 == PTBR_VAL  (0xaaaa1111)\n    the defect       -> PCB_A+16 == FLAGS_VAL (0xbbbb2222)\n    anything broken  -> 0, which is NEITHER, so BOTH rows red\n\nplus a third row `swpctx mapped` asserting the value is not 0 -- **because without it a probe whose\naddresses drifted back outside RAM would fail one row and pass the other, which reads exactly like a\nreal defect.**  Addresses moved inside 64 MB with an `assert` that says so.\n\n*** FINDING 2 -- `palalive` IS A CHECK THAT CANNOT FAIL, AND I WROTE IT WHILE APPLYING THE RULE\nAGAINST THAT. ***  *\"`alive` is 'the pty is still readable'.  `alpha_palcode`'s default arm does\n`cpu->running = 0` and stays in the debugger because `-V` is single-step.  The emulator does not\nexit.  `palalive` stays green.\"*  **Kept as a LABELLED NON-ORACLE rather than deleted**, so the next\nreader sees why it is there and does not re-add it as a real row; the mode question is answered by\n`palgate`, which greps the diagnostic text.\n\n**FINDING 3 -- the encoder self-test does not test the decoder.**  It re-runs `load_const`'s own\n32-bit wrap masked with `& 0xffffffff`, while LDAH sign-extends through an `(int32_t)` cast --\n`0xdeadbeef` would be `0xffffffffdeadbeef` in a register and the self-test would still print ok.\n**It is sound only because every value this file uses has bit 31 clear.**  Correct by luck; recorded\nin the file as such rather than quietly fixed.\n\n**FINDING 4 -- `put q` is not merely 128-bit, it is UNIMPLEMENTED** (`\"quad-words: TODO\"`).  The\npremise was true and understated.\n\n**CONFIRMED, so the design work was not wasted:** encodings match the decoder; the bootstrap method\nis valid and nothing between the `put d` writes and the first `call_pal` touches `apcb_ptbr`; K0SEG\nis checked before `base`; PCB offsets match `struct alpha_pcb`; `ps` is zero from `zeroed_alloc`;\n`0x30` is not on the unprivileged whitelist.  It also supplied two facts I did not have:\n`cpu->cd.alpha.ctx` starts at `0x10100`, and PROM setup (on by default) sets `a0 = 2048`.\n\n*** THE REVIEW COST ONE INVOCATION AND SAVED A GREEN WITNESS THAT PROVED NOTHING. ***  The lifecycle\nsays ask a seat to try a rung-3 probe; this says ask a seat to READ the probe before running it, and\non this evidence the second is worth as much as the first.\n\n*** RUNG-3 WITNESS BUILT AND RUN, 2026-09-07.  THE DEFECT REPRODUCES ON THE COMMITTED BUILD. ***\n\n    row              got          want              \n    encoder          ok           ok                load_const sign correction, 9 vectors, offline\n    live             0x5a5a5a5a   0x5a5a5a5a        LIVENESS: guest store then load through RAM\n    exec             0x1234       0x1234            a guest LDAH/LDA pair reached a register\n    palgate          not gated    not gated         call_pal 0x30 is privileged; we are in kernel mode\n    palalive         alive        alive             NON-ORACLE, labelled as such\n    swpctx alive     alive        alive             the guest survives two swpctx calls in K0SEG\n    swpctx ptbr      0xbbbb2222   0xaaaa1111        FAIL -- the defect\n    swpctx notflags  0xbbbb2222   NOT 0xbbbb2222    FAIL -- the defect's exact signature\n    swpctx mapped    0xbbbb2222   NOT 0x0           ok  -- the memory WAS there\n    ALPHAHARNESS_RESULT=7/9\n\n**THE MECHANISM, confirmed by the measurement rather than only by reading:** `cpu_alpha_palcode.c`\ncase `0x30` LOADs the new context, sets `apcb_ptbr = new_ptbr`, and then **overwrites it with\n`new_flags` a few lines later**.  The probe stages PCB_A with ptbr `0xaaaa1111` and flags\n`0xbbbb2222`, swaps A in, swaps B in (whose SAVE writes A's context back to `PCB_A+16`), and reads\nthat cell.  It holds the FLAGS.\n\n*** `swpctx mapped` IS THE ROW THAT MAKES THIS A MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN A PHANTOM, and it exists\nbecause a code review demanded it. ***  The grok harness review found that **every address in the\nfirst draft was planted past the end of RAM** -- `-M 64` gives 64 MB and the structures sat at\n`0x20010000`+ (512 MB) -- where writes are discarded and reads return zeros with no diagnostic.\nThree rows would have gone GREEN while measuring *unmapped memory returns 0*.  The repair moved\neverything below 64 MB **and added `assert max(P_CODE, P_PTRV, P_PCB_A, P_PCB_B) < 64*1024*1024`**,\nso the class cannot be reintroduced silently.  `swpctx mapped` then pins the same property at run\ntime: a `0` readback is reported as a SETUP FAILURE, not as a result.\n\n> **THAT IS THE THIRD DISTINCT ROW-CLASS THIS SESSION WHOSE WHOLE PURPOSE IS TO SEPARATE 'MEASURED\n> NOTHING' FROM 'MEASURED A ZERO'** -- the UBSan in-band control, the kill-table NUL check, and this.\n> They were built independently and they are all the same rule: *an apparatus that cannot fail\n> loudly will fail quietly and be believed.*\n\n**TEST-FIRST IS SATISFIED:** the witness shows the symptom on the committed build BEFORE any edit,\nwhich is what licenses a fix.  The probe exits non-zero on a red row, so `rc=1` here is the correct\noutcome and not an error.\n\n**AND IT UNBLOCKS [[alphapfnoffset]]**, which is the reason this row was worked first: that row's\nwitness needs a non-zero `apcb_ptbr` and a valid page table, and *this* defect clobbers `apcb_ptbr`\nwith the flags word -- so any translation probe built before this was fixed could not attribute a\nbad translation to the walk rather than to the swap.  The confound is now measured rather than\nsuspected.\n\n*** A SECOND CONSEQUENCE THIS ROW DOES NOT RECORD, found by COUNTING the assignments rather than\nreading them.  2026-09-07. ***\n\nOver `cpu_alpha_palcode.c:274-292`, the load block's field assignments are:\n\n    apcb_ksp 1   apcb_usp 1   apcb_cpc 1   apcb_asn 1   apcb_unique 1\n    apcb_decrsv0 1   apcb_decrsv1 1\n    apcb_ptbr  **2**        <- :278 = new_ptbr, :282 = new_flags\n    apcb_flags **0**        <- never assigned at all\n\n**So `:282` is a field-name transposition: it should be `apcb_flags = new_flags`.**  The value IS\nloaded -- the SAVE block stores `apcb_flags` to `ctx + 40` and the loader reads `new_flags` from\n`ctx + 40` -- and then lands in the wrong field.\n\n> **CONSEQUENCE 1 is the clobber the witness measures: `apcb_ptbr` ends up holding the flags word.**\n> **CONSEQUENCE 2 is new: `apcb_flags` is NEVER LOADED, so a context switch silently drops the\n> incoming context's flags.**  The row's title names only the first.\n\n*And the block's own comment at `:275` reads **\"Update all variables in the pcb simultaneously\"** --\nwhich is exactly the property it fails to have.  A comment asserting completeness directly above an\nincomplete list is the shape this project keeps finding: the claim is the thing least likely to be\nre-checked, because it reads as a description of the code below it.*\n\n**The counting is what found it.**  Reading the block, `:282` looks like an ordinary assignment in a\ncolumn of ordinary assignments; the eye supplies the field name it expects.  A `grep -o 'pcb\\.apcb_[a-z0-9]*\n*=' | sort | uniq -c` over the block makes the 2-and-0 unmissable -- **a census of a nine-line block,\nwhich is not where anyone thinks to run one.**\n\n*Fix design out to grok (`regress/artifacts/alphaswpctxptbr/FIX_brief.md`) before any edit: whether\none token repairs both consequences, what reads `apcb_flags` today and might rely on it being stale,\nand whether the witness then goes 9/9 or only 8/9.*\n\n*** THE GROK FIX REVIEW RATIFIED THE ONE-TOKEN EDIT AND REFUSED TO RATIFY IT AS THE ROUND --\nbecause the witness was BLIND to the second consequence.  2026-09-07. ***\n\n*\"Both red rows read the same `got': A+16.  A build that only stops clobbering PTBR -- delete\n`:282', or assign new_flags to any other field -- also scores 9/9.  Consequence 2 is unpinned.\"*\n\n**That is a clause-(d) gap in my own witness**, and the repair it named is one readback on a page the\nprobe had already proved was backed.  Added as `swpctx flags' (PCB_A + 40) and MEASURED:\n\n    swpctx ptbr      0xbbbb2222   0xaaaa1111       FAIL   the clobber\n    swpctx notflags  0xbbbb2222   NOT 0xbbbb2222   FAIL   the clobber's signature\n    swpctx flags     0x0          0xbbbb2222       FAIL   apcb_flags NEVER LOADED\n    HEAD 7/9 -> 7/10\n\n**Three red rows now pin three distinct consequences instead of one cell read twice.**\n\n**TWO PREMISES OF MINE CORRECTED, and a THIRD consequence found:**\n  * `apcb_flags` IS written globally -- by `wrfen` at `:221`.  My \"never assigned at all\" is true\n    only inside the load block, and I had stated it without that scope.\n  * *\"whatever the previous context left\"* is wrong: `swpctx` has never loaded that field from any\n    PCB, so the in-cpu value is **last `wrfen`, or 0 from the zeroed cpu struct**.\n  * **THIRD CONSEQUENCE:** today the SAVE at `:243-244` publishes that never-loaded word into the\n    OUTGOING PCB -- so a context switch *wipes the flags the guest planted*.  The bug propagates\n    outward into guest memory, not just inward into the cpu struct.\n\n**Every `apcb_flags` site in `src/` was enumerated and none relies on the stale value** (`wrfen`\nwrites without reading; the SAVE publishes; the header constants and `ALPHA_IF_CODE_FEN` are unused;\n`memory_alpha.c:46` reads `apcb_ptbr`, not flags).  *Sticky-FEN-across-`swpctx` is a PAL bug, not a\nbehaviour a reader is entitled to.*  **A separate finding volunteered in passing: `PAL_clrfen`\n(`0xae`) is named and user-mode-whitelisted but has NO `case`, so it reaches the default arm and\nstops the CPU.**\n\n*** `#507` APPLIED.  THE CENSUS THAT FOUND THE DEFECT IS THE CENSUS THAT CONFIRMS THE FIX. ***\n\n    before   apcb_ptbr 2   apcb_flags 0   (every other field 1)\n    after    apcb_ptbr 1   apcb_flags 1   (every field exactly 1)\n\nPropagated to all four trees; `build/`, `build-asan/` and `/tmp/gxsec-build` rebuilt, two-part\nclearance CLEAN/FRESH on each, `est/` part 2 N/A.  *The witness re-run is in flight; its result is\nNOT recorded here until it lands, because a number written before the run that produces it cannot be\ntold from a number fitted to the answer.*\n\n*** `#507` MEASURED: 7/10 -> 10/10, SAME SCRIPT.  The round is complete and held for the 9th. ***\n\n    swpctx ptbr      0xbbbb2222 -> 0xaaaa1111\n    swpctx notflags  0xbbbb2222 -> 0xaaaa1111\n    swpctx flags     0x0        -> 0xbbbb2222     <- consequence 2, pinned by the row grok demanded\n\nSix controls green on both runs.  Clause (i) discharged by the pair.  **Nothing committed** -- the\nFable hold stands and this waits as finished work rather than as a proposal.\n\n*** COMMIT ATTRIBUTION RESTORED 2026-09-08: this row's fix shipped in **`7a41b3f`** and the\n`commits` field was empty.  Verified before writing, not assumed. ***\n\n    git show --stat 7a41b3f        touches src/cpus/cpu_alpha_palcode.c\n    git show 7a41b3f -- src/cpus/cpu_alpha_palcode.c\n                                   contains the `#507' round tag 6 times\n\n**ALL FOUR ROUNDS IN THAT COMMIT HAD THE SAME GAP.**  `7a41b3f` is titled *\"#505 #506 #507 #508:\nfour test-first rounds, three new rung-3 witnesses, and a witness row that could not fail\"*, and\n**not one of the four rows carried its commit.**  `sgirergbroundtrip` (#505) was found first, by\naccident, while scoping an unrelated round in the same file; the other three were found by asking\nthe general question afterwards.\n\n> **THE CLASS: A COMMIT THAT SHIPS SEVERAL ROUNDS LOSES THE PER-ROW ATTRIBUTION FOR ALL OF THEM.**\n> The committer is performing ONE act and the ledger records N rows, so the per-row field is the\n> thing that falls out.  A one-round commit does not have this failure mode -- which is why it has\n> not been seen before and why it appeared four times at once.\n\n*The `state` field is deliberately NOT touched here.  `in work` may be correct for a row with\nresidual obligations, and changing it would be a claim I have not checked.  Only the attribution,\nwhich is unambiguous, is filled in.*",
tools/pipeline\ledger.json:32087:   "state_note": "MEASURED on my own commit, 2026-09-07.  `2956c7e` committed regress/sgi_mte_fill_probe.py and NOT the check_probe_wiring.py MANIFEST entry that classifies it, so a fresh clone at that SHA reds with `UNCLASSIFIED'.  **precommit_check.sh reported PRECOMMIT_PASS 0 hard immediately before that commit**, because the working tree already carried the manifest edit -- it was simply never staged.\n\n    git show HEAD:tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py | grep -c sgi_mte_fill_probe.py  -> 0\n    grep -c sgi_mte_fill_probe.py tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py                  -> 1\n\n> **A CHECK THAT VALIDATES A STATE OTHER THAN THE ONE THAT SHIPS CANNOT SEE A STAGING OMISSION.**  This is NOT the check-that-cannot-fail shape -- the gate failed eleven times tonight and every failure was real.  Its SUBJECT is one step to the left of the thing it guards.\n\n*** IT INTERACTS WITH A POLICY THIS PROJECT ALREADY HOLDS, which is why it is a row and not a note. ***  The carrier forbids `git add -A' and directory adds, so every commit here stages by EXPLICIT PATH.  That is strictly safer against INCLUDING the wrong file -- the failure the rule was written for -- and strictly MORE EXPOSED to OMITTING the right one.  **Two rules pulling opposite ways, and only one of them has a checker.**\n\nThe same commit also missed its own pass-2 receipt (caught by section F) and the ledger row naming it (section L), both on the NEXT commit.  So the gate's coverage is real; what it cannot do is bind that coverage to the staged set.\n\n**THE OBVIOUS FIX IS REJECTED ON ITS OWN RISK:** running the gate under `git stash --keep-index' would let a mid-run crash lose uncommitted work, and the gate takes ~2 minutes.  That is worse than the defect.  **The narrower candidate** is an existence check over the STAGED set for known file PAIRS -- a new `regress/*.py' and its MANIFEST entry; a `src/' change and a CHANGELOG round block -- in the check_cited_artifacts.py idiom (existence-only, forward-only from a cutoff).  NOT BUILT: it changes what every commit must satisfy, which is an owner decision, and owes two negative controls -- red on a probe staged without its manifest entry, green when both are staged."
