| [M](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/check_checkpoint.py:46) — checkpoint | External checkpoint versus actual HEAD | **No staged-content counterpart.** Intentionally a soft operational check. |
| [N](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/check_optpin.py:77) — optimization pins | Filesystem `diff_*.c` inventory/content and `gate_offline.sh` | **FG/FR.** Untracked differentials also enter this inventory. |
| [O](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/check_boilerplate.py:84) — independent seat notes | Working ledger | **FG/FR.** |
| [P](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/check_bugfile_sync.py:102) — bug-file synchronization | Working ledger and `OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md` | **FG/FR.** |
| [Q](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/tools/precommit_check.sh:455) — matrix freshness | Working generator/ledger; temporary render compared with working matrix | **FG/FR.** All three repository inputs must belong to the candidate snapshot. |
| [R](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1430) — probe wiring | Index-derived probe membership, filtered by disk existence; working manifest, runner and gate scripts | **FG/FR** for classification/wiring. Probe-header content contributes an advisory, not the hard wiring verdict. |
| [T](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/tools/precommit_check.sh:389) — grades | Working ledger | Can suppress warnings or misreport candidate coverage. **Already soft**, so no hard protection either way. |
| [U](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/check_score_claims.py:118) — score claims | Working gate files and matching probe docstrings; manifest literal in running shell script | **FG/FR.** Probe docstrings are checked only when their names match derived stems. |
| [V](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/seat_verdicts.py:849) — extractor selftest | Running filesystem extractor, its source and embedded cases | **FG/FR** when staged and working extractor versions differ. |
| [W](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/check_row_states.py:117) — row states | Working ledger/bug file, filesystem inventory and historical Git metadata | **FG/FR** through working records. |
| [X](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/check_cited_artifacts.py:192) — cited artifacts | Working changelog; filesystem resolution; genuine index-membership check | **FG/FR**, notably through unstaged citation corrections. Artifact **bytes** are deliberately outside its predicate. |
| [Y](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/tools/precommit_check.sh:1242) — NULs | Indexed text-file names; `tr` reads working files | **FG/FR.** It does not examine indexed text bytes. |
| [Z](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/check_wsl_dollar.py:58) — script hygiene | Indexed script names; `open()` reads working files | **FG/FR.** |
| [Unlettered hook controls](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/tools/precommit_check.sh:1098) | Selected names trigger execution of the filesystem suite | The executed suite can differ from its staged version. Its artifact implementation was outside this review’s scope. |

R and X’s conditional selftests have the same version problem as V: staged/unstaged/untracked names decide whether to run, but the filesystem checker is exercised.

For the original nine-of-ten plan, D would inspect the omitted tracked edit’s additions rather than the nine staged diffs. A/B would still use the correct candidate source bytes **if their selected source files were fully staged and remained unchanged afterward**.

**Q3. J is affected, but your description of the two files needs correcting.** The project-root originals are outside the Git repository. Only their `GXEMUL-SEC/tools/` copies belong to this index.

[J’s decisive line](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/tools/precommit_check.sh:728) is exactly:

```bash
elif cmp -s "$ROOT/$_c" "$SEC/tools/$_c"; then
```

This covers both `CLAUDE.md` and `PIPELINE.md`.

Updating the external original and its working copy, while omitting the copy’s update from staging, lets J pass while the commit retains the old carrier. That is the failure you are concerned about.

Also, J’s “tracked copy” existence test is merely `-f`; it does not verify index membership.

**Q4. A correct conversion needs an explicit choice of snapshot.** For a fix that continues accepting intentional partial staging:

- **Index mode:** repository membership, contents and checker selftests come from the candidate index. D uses `git diff --cached -U0`.
- **Working-tree preview:** preserve the useful before-staging audit. D uses ordinary `git diff -U0`.
- Keep deliberately external inputs—carrier originals, build trees, panels—and historical Git queries explicitly separate.

Choose and announce the mode **before the first pipeline invocation**, not at today’s late `CHANGED` assignment.

“Nothing staged” normally means the index equals HEAD, not that the index contains no files. Blindly replacing reads with `git show ":$path"` would therefore inspect old versions during the common before-staging run. Preserve that run as a preview; commit approval requires checking the subsequently prepared index.

Given the helpers’ filesystem interfaces, a complete isolated index snapshot is a practical implementation approach. It must include unchanged dependencies and preserve access to real Git history and external project paths. The helpers derive paths from `__file__`, so simply redirecting the shell’s `ROOT` is insufficient.

Thus conversion costs more than changing one flag: enumeration, existence checks, helper paths, tested checker versions and failed blob reads all need consistent handling. Byte comparisons also require deliberate treatment of Git filters/newline conversion; locally, the inspected carrier copies use LF and `core.autocrlf` is false.

**Q5. The agreement guard is useful, but insufficient alone.** Its conservative core is:

```bash
git diff --quiet --exit-code --
```

In staged mode, run it early and treat differences or Git errors as a hard refusal. Checking only `$CHANGED` would miss unstaged dependencies that the gate nevertheless reads. A repository-wide tracked-file guard catches those conservatively.

The read-only check restricted to your excluded probe returned **exit status 1**. This guard has a live failure condition here; it would refuse that intentional exclusion once staged-mode enforcement applies.

Two distinctions matter:

1. **Passing after staging everything is correct for an agreement check.** Agreement plus a valid content check can establish the indexed predicate. The content check must still reject invalid matching versions.
2. **D currently breaks that argument.** When the agreement guard passes, ordinary `git diff` is empty. Guard-only mitigation would leave D unable to inspect any staged additions. Changing D’s diff source is mandatory.

A tracked-file guard also does not cover untracked inputs admitted by filesystem enumeration, guarantee literal byte equality under Git filters, or protect against inputs changing during the run.

Therefore: **guard plus the D correction is a useful conservative interim repair for tracked-content mismatch.** Full candidate validation additionally needs controlled input membership and stable inputs. To accept your intentional partial staging, use the indexed snapshot instead of refusing the discrepancy.

**Q6. High-priority gate-integrity defect; the workflow is ordinary, and a shipped inconsistency is documented.** The permitted [probe-checker source](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:169) explicitly says:

> a tracked probe with no entry is UNCLASSIFIED … `2956c7e` shipped the second one — see `[[gateworktree]]`.

Read-only historical inspection confirmed that commit contains `regress/sgi_mte_fill_probe.py`, while its `check_probe_wiring.py` contains **zero manifest entries** for that file. That establishes an actual shipped invariant violation. I did not verify historical gate output.

The [main script’s own history](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/tools/precommit_check.sh:868) also records selective staging during concurrent work as the reason for its staged-name selection.

The general defect produces a false green when an omitted or partially staged change supplies something a check needs. **Every subset commit is not necessarily invalid.** D’s coverage failure is broader: fully staging tracked edits always leaves it no additions to inspect.

No gate/selftests were executed and no files were changed. The index changed during inspection, so this is not approval of its current contents. One malformed search accidentally returned excluded ledger text; the findings above rely on harness source and Git metadata.
